\textbf{Abstract}
\par
In research on recognition memory processes, concerns have been raised that multiple
\par
post-recognition judgments may influence each other (e.g., Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010). To test
\par
for influence, acrossthis in three experiments judgments of subjective experience, confidence,
\par
and source were compared and with order of judgments was counterbalanced across items.
\par
Comparison of responses across four categories of subjective experience (Remember, Know,
\par
Familiar, and Guess) and four levels of confidence (High, Medium, Low, and None) showed
\par
that confidence judgments were faster, more lenient, less sensitive to source accuracy, and
\par
influenced to a greater degree by manipulation of source, than were judgments of subjective
\par
experience. Overall, confidence and subjective experience judgments were not utilised in the
\par
same manner; however, both response distributions and response time (RT) data demonstrated
\par
that order of judgments did not influence responding. Crucially for understanding subjective
\par
experience judgments, Know and Familiar responses differed in recognition accuracy, source
\par
accuracy, confidence, and recognition RT. In contrast, Remember and Know judgments were
\par
only differentiated by source accuracy. Results suggest that subjective experience, source, and
\par
confidence judgments can safely accompany one another post-recognition, and subjective
\par
experience can go beyond the traditional Remember-Know(-Guess) procedure in simple
\par
episodic tasks. THIS LAST BIT NEED TO BE MORE SPECIFIC.
\par
