\par
\emph{``As remarked by an anonymous reviewer, whether confidence ratings can safely}
\par
\emph{accompany an R-K(-G) judgement, and whether they should follow or precede such judgement,}
\par
\emph{is as much an empirical as it is a theoretical question.''} (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010, footnote 2,
\par
p. 127).
\par
In recognition memory, three type of judgment have been used to examine the processes
\par
people use to make their recognition decisions: judgments of confidence, subjective experience,
\par
and source. The current experiments examine relationships between these judgment types.
\par
Confidence judgments ask participants to rate the level of confidence they hold for their
\par
recognition decision and vary from binary judgments (e.g., sure/unsure; Gardiner \& Java, 1990)
\par
to 20-point rating scales (e.g., Wixted \& Mickes, 2010). Subjective experience is typically
\par
measured using the Remember-Know (RK) procedure where participants make a judgment
\par
about their subjective experience for each item retrieved from memory by making a
\par
`Remember' or `Know' response. An item should be classified as Remember when the
\par
participant retrieved accompanying detail about something thought or experienced at the time of
\par
encoding and categorised as Know when the participant was aware that the item had been on the
\par
study list but could not recall anything experienced for the item (Gardiner \& Richardson-
\par
Klavehn, 2000; Tulving, 1985). When the RK procedure was first introduced Remember and
\par
Know responses were posited as reflecting autonoetic and noetic consciousness based on
\par
retrieval from episodic and semantic memory respectively (Tulving, 1985). However,
\par
subsequently the RK procedure has been primarily utilised in the study of episodic recognition
\par
processes (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010; Yonelinas, 2002). In this sphere, while some view the
\par
subjective states of Remembering and Knowing as orthogonal to the underlying processes of
\par
recollection and familiarity (for example see Gardiner, 2000, and Wixted \& Mickes, 2010), theSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 5
\par
majority of studies using the RK procedure assume that a Remember response is an index of
\par
recollection while a Know response is an index of familiarity (Yonelinas, 2002).
\par
Source judgments are somewhat similar to subjective experience judgments as they are
\par
also based on retrieval (or not) of information from encoding. In source monitoring procedures,
\par
participants make a judgment about some aspect of the context in which an item was originally
\par
presented. Examples are whether an item was written in a particular font/colour/size, was
\par
shown on the left or right of the screen, was spoken in a particular voice, or a combination of
\par
these contexts (e.g., Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002). A key difference between source judgments and
\par
judgments of subjective experience is that source judgments involve a stricter criterion. The
\par
only form of recollection which `counts' in this procedure is recollection which allows the
\par
individual to determine which list the item was shown on. Noncritical recollection, for example
\par
recalling that you pictured your favourite coffee shop when the word `caf\'e' was shown, is not
\par
helpful unless it supports source discrimination.
\par
In this paper, three experiments are presented that examine the influence that judgments
\par
of confidence, source, and subjective experience have on each other. Analysis focuses on
\par
distribution of responses across confidence levels, subjective experience categories, and source
\par
accuracy, dependent on whether judgments were made first or second following a recognition
\par
decision. Reaction times to make Old/New recognition responses are analysed dependent on
\par
subsequent judgment and, in a novel approach to this field, the time taken to make the actual
\par
judgments is also explored.
\par
The relationship between confidence and subjective experience is of critical importance
\par
to the debate between single- versus dual-process accounts of memory. Dual-process models
\par
assume that two distinct processes or forms of memory underlie recognition and that successfulSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 6
\par
recognition is determined by the relative contributions of both processes. Conversely, single-
\par
process accounts propose that recognition relies on only one continuous dimension of familiarity
\par
or memory strength, and successful recognition is determined by the strength of this single
\par
dimension (for comprehensive reviews of the literature summarizing the opposing viewpoints
\par
see Diana, Reder, Arndt, \& Park, 2006; Dunn, 2004, 2008; Gardiner, 2008; Parks \& Yonelinas,
\par
2007; Wixted \& Stretch, 2004; and Yonelinas, 2002). Through the current experiments we aim
\par
to better understand recognition processes theoretically by comparing the more subjective
\par
measures of RK and confidence with the relatively more objective measure of source. Here we
\par
are not concerned with providing evidence which supports one or other of the increasingly
\par
complex single- or dual-process models (Gardiner, 2008); rather the experiments presented here
\par
take post-recognition judgments at face value and examine how people make and understand the
\par
three types of judgment under study. The move `back' towards understanding Remembering
\par
and Knowing at a more conceptual level was advocated by both Dunn (2004) and Gardiner
\par
(2008).
\par
As stated in the opening quotation, whether judgments influence each other is of interest
\par
both empirically and theoretically. However, many experimental paradigms combine judgments
\par
without the issue of influence having been explored. Experiments that have compared
\par
combinations of two of these three judgments are discussed in subsequent sections.
\par
Furthermore, since Tulving (1985) introduced the RK paradigm researchers have used a number
\par
of variations on the paradigm. Methodological differences pertinent to the design of the current
\par
experiments are discussed. In addition to focusing on response patterns for subjective
\par
experience, source, and confidence judgments when different judgments are compared,
\par
experiments that have explored reaction times associated with different responses are alsoSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 7
\par
reviewed. Reaction time to make judgments was a primary analysis of interest in the current
\par
experiments as, to our knowledge, no one has yet compared how long it takes participants to
\par
make these three different types of judgment.
\par
\textbf{Methodological issues for measuring subjective experience}
\par
One methodological issue in the RK paradigm is whether use of one-step or two-step RK
\par
procedures influences recognition accuracy or makes Remember responding more lenient.
\par
Two-step procedures involve separate judgments of recognition (old/new) and subjective
\par
experience. Participants initially judge whether they recognise an item, and only if they do
\par
recognise the item are they asked to judge their state of awareness regarding that recognition.
\par
One-step procedures combine the recognition judgment with the judgment of subjective
\par
experience: participants are asked to judge whether an item is Remembered, Known, or New.
\par
Comparisons suggest that no differences in overall recognition accuracy are found using one-
\par
step and two-step procedures. However, if interested in the experiential states underlying
\par
recognition and use of Remember and Know responses across different experimental conditions,
\par
the two methods produce different patterns of responding, primarily for assignment of hits and
\par
false alarms to Know (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010; Eldridge, Sarfatti, \& Knowlton, 2002; Hicks
\par
\& Marsh, 1999). Moreover, if interested in reaction time (RT) measures, separation of the
\par
recognition decision from the judgment of subjective experience -- as occurs in a two-step
\par
procedure -- is critical for isolating the time taken to perform these separate processes.
\par
Examination of source, confidence, and subjective experience judgments using one-step and
\par
two-step procedures has produced some divergent results and methodological differences are
\par
drawn upon in later discussion of experimental findings.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 8
\par
A second methodological issue is whether the original two categories of Remembering
\par
and Knowing are sufficient or whether other states of awareness can be identified. Whilst the
\par
addition of a Guess response option is now common in Remember-Know experiments, and is
\par
included in order to remove experimental `noise' caused by participants assigning guesses to the
\par
Know category (Eldridge et al., 2002; Gardiner, 2008), how Know responses are conceptualised
\par
varies greatly across studies. In their review of the Remember-Know literature, Gardiner and
\par
Richardson-Klavehn (2000) identified interpretation of Know responses as ``the most vexatious
\par
problem in the remember/know paradigm'' (p. 238). This problem has arisen because
\par
researchers' definitions of Knowing vary in terms of the emphasis put on familiarity or certainty
\par
(Geraci, McCabe, \& Guillory, 2009); and relatedly, whether Know responses are interpreted as
\par
reflecting an underlying process of familiarity or a state of knowing.
\par
In the current experiments participants are asked to assign responses to one of four
\par
categories of subjective experience: Remember (R), Know (K), Familiar (F), and Guess (G).
\par
Full definitions provided in Table 1. The separation of the subjective experiences of Know and
\par
Familiar was first performed by Conway, Gardiner, Perfect, Anderson, and Cohen (1997). In
\par
their study of student learning, Conway et al. found that for some multiple-choice questions
\par
(MCQ) students reported that their subjective state for an answer was one of neither recollection
\par
or familiarity, instead they felt that they `just knew' the answer. Across learning, Conway et al.
\par
demonstrated evidence of a shift from Remembering to Knowing amongst higher performing
\par
students. Conway et al. discuss this `R-to-K shift' as reflecting a change in knowledge
\par
representation from episodic to semantic memory brought about by loss of episodic details from
\par
memory and the emergence of conceptual organisation. Evidence of an R-to-K shift using the
\par
four categories of subjective experience (R, K, F, G) has been demonstrated in similar studies ofSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 9
\par
student learning by Barber, Rajaram, and Marsh (2008) and Herbert and Burt (2001, 2003,
\par
2004) and a study exploring learning of rare word definitions by Dewhurst, Conway, and Brandt
\par
(2009). Separated Know and Familiar categories have also been utilised with complex materials
\par
in studies of face recognition (Wright \& Sladden, 2003) and eyewitness identification
\par
(Sauerland \& Sporer, 2009).
\par
[Insert Table 1 about here]
\par
One might argue that the separation of Know and Familiar would only apply to learning
\par
of conceptually rich materials over long time periods (e.g., Conway et al., 1997; Herbert \& Burt,
\par
2001, 2003, 2004). In laboratory recognition tasks with lists of unrelated words and short study-
\par
test intervals, the experiential state of `Knowing' may not appear to be applicable as integration
\par
of the studied information into any body of knowledge might not be able to occur. However,
\par
Dewhurst et al. (2009) used definitions of rare words precisely because these were unrelated
\par
facts that would be more difficult to integrate into a wider schema. Dewhurst et al.'s (2009)
\par
participants assigned 10\% of items to Know at first test -- analogous to a single-time-point
\par
recognition experiment. Although this is lower than the 22\% reported by Conway et al., it
\par
demonstrates that even for less meaningful materials, participants judged the Know response to
\par
reflect their experiential state for some items only five minutes post-study with no opportunity
\par
for integration into wider conceptual organisation1. These 10\% of unrelated definitions were
\par
`just known' without recollection or feelings of familiarity about the study episode only a short
\par
time after said study episode. This suggests that separate categories of Know and Familiar
\par
might well be applicable to the subjective experiences in typical episodic recognition paradigms
\par
where the materials have lower intrinsic meaning (e.g., lists of unrelated words).SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 10
\par
Other researchers have also utilised modified RK procedures. Montaldi, Spencer,
\par
Roberts, and Mayes (2006) trained participants to distinguish between recollection and three
\par
levels of familiarity: very weak familiarity, moderate familiarity, and strong familiarity.
\par
Similarly, Yonelinas, Otten, Shaw, and Rugg (2005) instructed participants to assign recollected
\par
items to Remember and non-recollected items to four levels of confidence. While these
\par
procedures do not map directly on to the four categories of subjective experience used in the
\par
present experiments, they do demonstrate that other researchers are exploring alternatives to the
\par
dichotomous RK paradigm and thus support the underlying theoretical suggestion that there are
\par
additional states of subjective awareness accessible for study (McCabe, Geraci, Boman,
\par
Sensenig, \& Rhodes, 2011; see also Palmer, Brewer, McKinnon, \& Weber, 2010; (e.g.,
\par
Sauerland \& Sporer, 2009; Strong, 1913; Wright \& Sladden, 2003). A key question in the
\par
current experiments is whether the separated categories of Know and Familiar have any utility
\par
in standard episodic recognition memory paradigms, and indeed whether differences exist
\par
between Know and Familiar in the same way that differences exist between Remember and
\par
Know. In particular, the separation of Know and Familiar subjective experiences is pertinent to
\par
the issue of whether items assigned to Know can be recognised with accurate source. Research
\par
comparing subjective experience and confidence judgments with judgments of source is
\par
discussed in relation to Experiments 2 and 3 which include source judgments.
\par
\textbf{Comparing subjective experience and confidence judgments}
\par
Traditionally confidence was operationalized as a two-category scale of Sure-Unsure and
\par
proportion of items assigned to these two categories were compared against proportion assigned
\par
to Remember and Know. Gardiner and Java (1990) thus compared subjective experience and
\par
confidence to words and non-words and found that more Remember judgments were given toSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 11
\par
words while more Know judgments were given to non-words. This interaction between word
\par
type and response was not found when Sure-Unsure judgments were made. Similar patterns
\par
were demonstrated by by Rajaram (1993), Rajaram et al. (2002), and Tunney and Fernie (2007;
\par
with the addition of a Guess category). Overall, high confidence or `Sure' responses were made
\par
more often than Remember judgments, indicating more leniency in confidence compared to
\par
subjective experience judgments. Furthermore, patterns across experimental manipulations
\par
demonstrated that while judgments of confidence and subjective experience may be interrelated,
\par
the two judgment types are not ``experimentally interchangeable'' (Rajaram et al., 2002, p. 234).
\par
Remember-Know and confidence have also been compared using a larger scale to
\par
measure confidence and responses plotted using Receiver-Operating Characteristic (ROC)
\par
curves. The consistent finding from these studies has been that higher confidence is associated
\par
with Remember responses compared to Know responses (Rotello, Macmillan, \& Reeder, 2004;
\par
Rotello, Macmillan, Reeder, \& Wong,,2005; Slotnick, 2010; Wixted \& Stretch, 2004; Wixted \&
\par
Mickes, 2010; Yonelinas, 2001a; Yonelinas, Dobbins, Szymanski, Dhaliwal, \& King, 1996; see
\par
also Tulving, 1985). However, distribution of Remember and Know responses across
\par
confidence levels is influenced by how conservative the definitions of Remembering are
\par
(Rotello et al., 2005; Slotnick, 2010), suggesting that subjective experience and confidence are
\par
not equivalent measures. Whilst patterns of ROC curves are interesting for the single- versus
\par
dual-process debate, the manner in which confidence and subjective experience are
\par
operationalised in ROC studies means that participants' response distributions for the two
\par
cannot be directly compared as was done when Sure-Unsure were compared against Remember-
\par
Know.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 12
\par
In the current experiments, in order to directly compare participants' use of subjective
\par
experience and confidence judgments in a manner akin to the early studies of Gardiner and Java
\par
(1990) and Rajaram (1993), the four categories of subjective experience (Remember, Know,
\par
Familiar, and Guess) were operationalized alongside four levels of confidence (High, Medium,
\par
Low, and None). As we were interested in the influence of one judgment on another judgment,
\par
this operationalization allows the influence of one response on a subsequent response to be
\par
examined when the parameters of each judgment type are equal.
\par
\textbf{The influence of one judgment on another judgment}
\par
To date, no studies have explored the influence that confidence and subjective experience
\par
judgments may have on each other when the two judgments are reported post recognition. The
\par
traditional viewpoint on this subject is summarised by Holmes, Waters, and Rajaram (1998)
\par
who stated that ``\dots multiple judgments might affect performance in unforeseen ways'' (p. 1031)
\par
and Humphreys et al. (2003) who thought that ``When two judgments are made on the same
\par
trial, the knowledge that a second judgment will be required could distort the first judgment'' (p.
\par
806). This issue has also recently been considered by Bruno and Rutherford (2010):
\par
It is our belief that collecting confidence ratings when also R--K(--G)
\par
judgements are provided is problematic because these measures may not be
\par
independent of each other. R--K(--G) judgements may directly influence confidence
\par
ratings (e.g., `if I \emph{remember} an event, then I must assign a higher rating') and, vice
\par
versa, confidence ratings may exert an influence on the R--K(--G) judgement (e.g.,
\par
`if I give a high rating, then it must be \emph{remember'}). (italics in original; Bruno \&
\par
Rutherford, 2010, p. 127)SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 13
\par
These comments highlight that this belief has existed in the Remember-Know paradigm
\par
across at least the last two decades but has not yet been subject to empirical testing.
\par
In line with the traditional viewpoint of Holmes et al. (1998), previous experiments
\par
comparing subjective experience and confidence judgments have employed measures to ensure
\par
that the two judgment types did not influence one another. In the original subjective experience
\par
and confidence judgment comparisons by Gardiner and Java (1990) and Rajaram (1993),
\par
judgment type was compared across two separate experiments. Rajaram et al. (2002) later
\par
replicated these findings using a within-subjects design, but even in this design testing sessions
\par
employing the two types of judgments took place one week apart and subjective experience was
\par
always tested first, ``\dots to ensure that confidence judgments did not contaminate the remember-
\par
know judgments with a carryover effect to the second session'' (p. 230). Although the same
\par
participants were making both types of judgment, Rajaram et al. remained concerned about how
\par
one judgment might influence the other.
\par
\textbf{Reaction time data associated with confidence and subjective experience judgments}
\par
As with the influence of judgments on each other, reaction times to make post-
\par
recognition judgments have not before been tested experimentally. In two-step procedures,
\par
when examining reaction times to items recognised with differing levels of confidence or
\par
subjective experience the standard analysis is to compare recognition decision RTs for items
\par
categorised by their subsequent judgment category. In one-step procedures it is the judgment of
\par
subjective experience or confidence which is timed, but this judgment is made in conjunction
\par
with the recognition decision so the timings of the two decision processes are indistinguishable.
\par
However, reliable differences have been demonstrated for both judgment types using both these
\par
types of RT analysis.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 14
\par
Recognition is faster when confidence is higher2. Mandler and Boeck (1974) used a two-
\par
step Yes/No then 3-point confidence scale procedure and found recognition decisions to highest
\par
confidence items to be approximately 1200ms faster than recognition decisions to items given
\par
the lowest confidence ratings. Using a one-step 6-point confidence recognition scale this pattern
\par
was replicated by Ratcliff and Murdock (1976) who demonstrated a 1000ms (approx.)
\par
difference between highest and lowest confidence RTs. Recent evidence from neuroimaging
\par
has also produced similar findings. For example, Henson, Rugg, Shallice, and Dolan (2000)
\par
demonstrated an advantage in RT of approximately 600ms for high confidence compared to low
\par
confidence recognition judgments.
\par
Recognition RTs are also faster for Remember items than for Know items (Dewhurst \&
\par
Conway, 1994; Dewhurst, Hitch, \& Barry, 1998, Dewhurst, Holmes, Brandt, \& Dean, 2006;
\par
Henson, Rugg, Shallice, Josephs, \& Dolan, 1999; Vilberg \& Rugg, 2007; Wixted \& Stretch,
\par
2004). Dewhurst et al. (2006) demonstrated this finding to be true when both one-step and two-
\par
step procedures are used, and also found reliable differences in recognition RTs when stimulus
\par
characteristics are varied; for example a larger RT advantage for Remember responses for words
\par
compared to non-words. However, differences in Remember and Know recognition RTs can be
\par
much smaller than those observed for confidence, for example Wixted and Stretch (2004)
\par
reported a difference of only 80ms to be reliable. In comparison, other researchers have found
\par
as large an RT advantage for Remember over Know responses as that obtained for high
\par
compared to low confidence; for example a 730ms advantage was reported by Dewhurst et al.
\par
(2006) and an 830ms advantage by Henson et al. (1999).
\par
Examining subjective experience and confidence together Rotello and Zeng (2008) found
\par
recognition decisions for Remember items to be faster than for Know items. However, whenSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 15
\par
Remember and Know responses were equated for confidence the RT advantage for Remember
\par
responses was reduced, leading Rotello and Zeng to conclude that Remember and Know
\par
responses result from a single underlying memory process. Further interpretations of RT data in
\par
terms of theory are reserved until the General Discussion.
\par
So why have judgment RTs not been explored before? One methodological reason is
\par
that the participant often has to wait till a prompt appears on the screen before they can make
\par
their judgment (Wilding \& Rugg, 1996). The assumption perhaps being that the participant
\par
would have decided on their response prior to the prompt and therefore no difference in RTs
\par
would be observed. In the current experiments this methodological issue was avoided through
\par
counterbalancing of judgment order and the inclusion of `catch' trials where participants were
\par
asked to make other non-critical judgments for the item. On these trials, the first judgment
\par
made by participants was one critical to the experiment (e.g., either confidence or subjective
\par
experience in Experiment 1); however, the second judgment was either a judgment of age of
\par
acquisition (AoA) or of pleasantness (rather than the other `critical' judgment of subjective
\par
experience, confidence, or source -- Experiments 2 and 3 only). As participants would not have
\par
known which judgment they would be required to make first post-recognition, and they certainly
\par
would not know what type of judgment would be coming second, the issue of pre-prompt
\par
decisions being made for judgments is reduced. Participants could not systematically predict
\par
which judgment they would be making.
\par
As the experiments presented here compared two judgments made post-recognition, a
\par
methodological reason for analysing judgment RTs in addition to recognition RTs classified by
\par
subsequent judgment is that both judgments made for each item are dependent on the same
\par
recognition decision and therefore this RT could not be used to compare judgment types acrossSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 16
\par
judgment order. A theoretical reason for analysing judgment RTs is that whilst recollection and
\par
high confidence have each been shown to increase the speed at which a separate recognition
\par
decision is made, research has not yet examined whether these can influence RT for decisions
\par
which are independent of recognition. This separation of recognition decision time and
\par
judgment decision time also enables comparison of judgment types when employed in identical
\par
paradigms as in the literature RTs vary between studies. Here RT to make subjective experience
\par
and confidence judgments can be directly compared for the first time. It is predicted that
\par
confidence judgments will be made more quickly than judgments of experience as it is
\par
suggested that the consideration of whether anything is recollected about the encoding of the
\par
item will take longer than simply thinking about the confidence associated with recognition. RT
\par
for judgments is also interesting from an influence point of view. In addition to examining
\par
whether making a Remember judgment always leads to a secondary judgment of high
\par
confidence, for example (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010), RTs for judgments can explore whether
\par
making a Remember judgment also influences the speed at which that later confidence judgment
\par
is made, and vice versa.
\par
The current experiments were designed to explore whether when two judgments were
\par
made post-recognition the response to one judgment influenced the other judgment. The
\par
examination of influence of subjective experience and confidence judgments on each other is
\par
entirely novel, as is examination of RTs to make judgments that occur after, and are independent
\par
of, recognition. The three experiments presented here are identical in procedure except that the
\par
two post-recognition judgments differ across experiments. In Experiment 1, confidence
\par
judgments are compared with judgments of subjective experience.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 17
\par
\textbf{Experiment 1}
\par
In this experiment participants studied medium-frequency words and at test made an Old-
\par
New recognition judgment followed by two post-recognition judgments. The two critical
\par
judgments made in this experiment were confidence and subjective experience. For both
\par
judgment types four response options were provided: Remember, Know, Familiar, and Guess
\par
for subjective experience judgments, and 3 (High), 2 (Medium), 1 (Low), and 0 (None) for
\par
confidence judgments. The order in which participants made these judgments was
\par
counterbalanced across items.
\par
On the basis of previous findings it was predicted that participants would use confidence
\par
judgments more leniently than subjective experience judgments resulting in a greater proportion
\par
of High confidence responses than Remember responses (Gardiner \& Java, 1990; Rajaram,
\par
1993; Rajaram et al., 2002; Tunney \& Fernie, 200). However, as previous comparisons of
\par
subjective experience and confidence have not utilised four categories of each judgment type
\par
specific predictions comparing other categories of subjective experience and confidence are not
\par
made. It is assumed that the two judgment types are not ``experimentally interchangeable''
\par
(Rajaram et al., 2002, p. 234) and to examine this, distribution patterns, accuracy, and RT data
\par
for the four categories of subjective experience and confidence were explored. If confidence
\par
and subjective experience are independent then it would be expected that a fully
\par
counterbalanced design would reveal dissociations between the two types of judgment on these
\par
key dependent variables.
\par
With regard to the influence of one judgment on the other, the order of judgments was
\par
examined. It is possible that Bruno and Rutherford's (2010) suggestion is correct and that after
\par
making a Remember judgment participants will be more inclined to make a judgment of HighSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 18
\par
confidence, for example; but comparing the order of judgments allows the reverse pattern to
\par
also be explored -- whether more Remember judgments are made after a judgment of High
\par
confidence. Influence is also able to be explored in the RT analysis. As it is considered that
\par
subjective experience gives rise to confidence, not the other way around (Gardiner, 2001), it is
\par
suggested that confidence judgments that are made following a judgment of subjective
\par
experience may be speeded as assessment of recollection and familiarity has already occurred
\par
when the subjective experience judgment was made.
\par
\textbf{Method}
\par
\emph{Participants and Design}
\par
Participants were 44 undergraduate students (38 female), mean age 19.25 years (range 18
\par
to 23), from the University of Leeds, who received either participant credit or \pounds 5 for taking part.
\par
Participants were tested in groups of between three and fourteen at individual PCs. Data from
\par
two further participants could not be analysed due to computer problems during testing. The
\par
experiment employed a within-subjects design and for the study and test phases all instructions
\par
and stimuli were presented, and data collected, using E-Prime version 1.2.
\par
\emph{Materials}
\par
Information concerning materials and procedures are presented here in detail for all
\par
experiments in this paper. Critical methodological differences are then discussed for
\par
Experiments 2 and 3. The target words were 56 medium-frequency words3 (mean familiarity
\par
rating of 424; range 350-480) limited to between five and eight letters in length. Targets were
\par
matched with 56 words to be used as lure items in the recognition test, and 16 filler words. In
\par
total participants studied 64 words (56 targets plus 8 fillers, 4 shown at the start and 4 at the end
\par
of the study phase) and performed the recognition test on 128 words4. This 128 included the 8SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 19
\par
studied fillers and a further 8 lure fillers; all fillers were shown at the start of the test phase to
\par
acquaint the participants with the procedures for making recognition decisions and judgments.
\par
All fillers were excluded from analysis.
\par
Participants made four types of judgment in this experiment: subjective experience
\par
judgments (Remember, Know, Familiar, Guess), confidence judgments, age-of-acquisition
\par
(AoA) judgments, and pleasantness judgments. Participants were always required to make
\par
either a subjective experience judgment or a confidence judgment first, followed by another type
\par
of judgment (of the three not already made). The critical trials were those where participants
\par
made a combination of a subjective experience judgment and a confidence judgment, either
\par
subjective experience then confidence or confidence then subjective experience. The AoA and
\par
pleasantness trials were included in an attempt to avoid participants guessing the manipulation
\par
of interest and so that participants were not able to predict what judgment would follow the first.
\par
Assigned to the 64 studied words (56 targets, 8 fillers) were 16 trials of each judgment pairing,
\par
either: subjective experience + confidence, confidence + subjective experience, subjective
\par
experience + AoA/pleasantness (eight of each), and confidence + AoA/pleasantness (eight of
\par
each). Words were randomly assigned to judgment pairings, though it was ensured that filler
\par
words were always followed by a non-critical judgment pairing (involving AoA or
\par
pleasantness). Lure words were assigned to judgment pairings in an identical manner to target
\par
words. AoA and pleasantness responses were not analysed.
\par
Four word lists were utilised. Two sets of 64 words were counterbalanced as lure and
\par
target lists across participants. Two versions of each were constructed where each word was
\par
matched with a different judgment pairing and which also had differing inter-trial-intervals (ITI)SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 20
\par
and inter-judgment-intervals (IJI) for each word in the test phase. Allocation of participants to
\par
list was random.
\par
\emph{Procedure}
\par
In the study phase, target words were presented in random order individually in the
\par
centre of the computer screen. Participants were instructed that they should study the words and
\par
that later they would undergo an Old-New recognition test. It was emphasized that it was a long
\par
list of words and that participants should concentrate on the screen throughout. Participants
\par
studied each word for four seconds separated by a fixation point shown for 750 milliseconds.
\par
The eight filler words were shown in random order at the start and the end of the target word
\par
list. Prior to undergoing the recognition test, participants completed pattern comparison and
\par
letter comparison speed of processing tasks (e.g., Salthouse, 1991). Although not formally
\par
timed, participants took approximately five minutes to complete this.
\par
In the test phase, participants were instructed that for each word recognised as Old they
\par
would then be asked to make two judgments from the list of four judgments. The four
\par
judgments were then explained on individual screens (with full definitions of Remember (R),
\par
Know (K), Familiar (F), and Guess (G) provided on paper; see Table 1). Each explanation
\par
screen included an example of the scale that the participant would use for each type of
\par
judgment. The subjective experience judgment scale consisted of four response boxes labelled
\par
(from left to right) R, K, F, and G, with the question `What is your EXPERIENCE of
\par
recognising this word?'. The confidence scale consisted of four boxes labelled (from left to
\par
right) 0, 1, 2, 3, with 0 accompanied by the label `Not confident at all' and 3 accompanied by
\par
`Extremely confident', and the question `How CONFIDENT are you that you correctly
\par
recognised this word?'. For ease of description in discussing analyses, these numericalSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 21
\par
confidence levels are translated into verbal labels: 3 = High, 2 = Medium, 1 = Low, 0 = None.
\par
The labelling of the subjective experience scale `R, K, F, G' from left to right and the
\par
confidence scale `0, 1, 2, 3' from left to right was purposefully done to ensure that on trials
\par
involving both these judgments, if assigning the item to both Remember and High confidence
\par
for example, participants were not moving the mouse to exactly the same position to make both
\par
responses. Between judgments the mouse pointer was repositioned to the centre of the screen.
\par
The AoA judgment scale consisted of four boxes labelled 0-4, 5-8, 9-12, and 13+, and the
\par
question `What AGE were you when you first learnt this word?'. Finally the pleasantness scale
\par
consisted of the question `How PLEASANT is this word?' and four boxes labelled 1, 2, 3, and
\par
4, with 1 accompanied by the label `Not pleasant' and 4 accompanied by `Very pleasant'. For
\par
each judgment type, the response boxes were the same size, shape and positioned identically.
\par
Commencing with the 16 filler words, participants then underwent the recognition test.
\par
At the start of a trial a fixation point was shown for 750ms. Each word was then presented in
\par
the centre of the screen with the cues `New' and `Old' presented below and towards the left and
\par
right of the screen respectively, reflecting the number keys which participants were required to
\par
use to make their Old-New judgment: 1 for New, 2 for Old. Participants were instructed to
\par
press these keys with their left hand. Reminders for which numbers corresponded to Old and
\par
New were shown on all trials. When participants indicated that they recognised a word as Old
\par
the first judgment screen was shown. The word re-appeared in the centre of the screen with the
\par
response boxes and judgment question below. The mouse pointer appeared in the centre of the
\par
screen and participants made all judgments using the mouse to click on one of the response
\par
boxes. Speed and accuracy of responses were emphasised in the instructions for the test phase
\par
and response times for all recognition decisions and judgments were recorded. In between theSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 22
\par
two judgment screens and prior to a new trial commencing a blank screen was shown as an IJI
\par
or ITI (for a duration which ranged from 250 to 1250ms). Every twelve trials during the test
\par
phase participants were allowed to take a break if they were fatigued. A `take a break' slide
\par
appeared and remained until the participant pressed the spacebar. After completion of the test
\par
phase participants completed a debrief questionnaire to ensure that they had understood the
\par
different types of judgments made; no participants were excluded on the basis of their
\par
responses.
\par
\textbf{Results and Discussion}
\par
Memory performance was examined, followed by the proportion of correct responses
\par
assigned to each of the confidence levels and subjective experience categories calculated using
\par
both \emph{a priori }and \emph{a posteriori }methods. The influence of First Judgment on Second Judgment
\par
was then explored using \emph{a priori }and \emph{a posteriori }methods. Reaction times for recognition
\par
decisions and judgments were analysed by confidence level or subjective experience category.
\par
Prior to analysis, data from any responses that had been made faster than 300ms or slower than
\par
8000ms were excluded from the dataset. Data from Guess and None responses was excluded
\par
from all ANOVA to avoid violating the assumption of independence in analysis of \emph{a priori}
\par
proportional data and to increase listwise N in analysis of RT and \emph{a posteriori }accuracy data
\par
(though data from Guess and None are included on figures). Greenhouse-Geisser values are
\par
reported when ANOVA did not meet assumptions of sphericity. The central findings from each
\par
analysis are summarised within the following sections and more substantive theoretical
\par
discussion is carried out in the General Discussion.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 23
\par
\emph{Memory measures}
\par
Memory performance measures are shown in Table 2. Overall recognition performance
\par
(\emph{M }= .54) was significantly higher than chance (zero), \emph{t}(43) = 17.86, \emph{p }{$<$} .001. As participants
\par
made FAs to only 8\% of lures, analysis of lure data was not performed.
\par
[Insert Table 2 about here]
\par
\emph{Subjective experience and confidence measures}
\par
The level of confidence or type of subjective experience participants had based their
\par
recognition decisions on was examined next. Firstly \emph{a priori }proportions were calculated -- the
\par
proportion of correct responses assigned to each of the confidence levels or subjective
\par
experience categories; see the upper panel of Figure 1. A 2(judgment type) x 2(judgment order)
\par
x 3(response category) within-subjects ANOVA demonstrated no main effect of judgment type,
\par
\emph{F}(1,43) = 1.48, \emph{p} = .23; or judgment order, \emph{F} {$<$} 1. The main effect of response category was
\par
significant, \emph{F}(1.55,66.55) = 41.34, \emph{p} {$<$} .001; and there was a significant interaction between
\par
judgment type and response category, \emph{F}(1.43,61.61) = 13.61, \emph{p} {$<$} .001. As shown in Figure 1,
\par
nearly 60\% of responses were assigned to High confidence, while just over 40\% of responses
\par
were assigned to Remember. Approximately equal proportions of responses were assigned to
\par
Know and Familiar, whilst slightly more responses were assigned to Medium compared to Low
\par
confidence.
\par
[Insert Figure 1 about here]
\par
There was no interaction between judgment order and response category \emph{F}(1.68,72.41) =
\par
1.61, \emph{p} = .21; or judgment type and judgment order, \emph{F }{$<$} 1; and the three-way interaction was
\par
not significant, \emph{F }{$<$} 1. As there was no main effect of judgment order, judgment order did not
\par
interact with response category, and there was no three-way interaction, further analysis wasSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 24
\par
conducted with data for First and Second Judgments combined. Confidence and subjective
\par
experience do not appear to differ as a function of which judgment type is made first.
\par
To examine the significant interaction between judgment type and response category,
\par
separate 3(response category) ANOVAs for subjective experience and confidence were
\par
conducted with responses aggregated across judgment order. Both ANOVAs demonstrated
\par
significant main effects of response category: subjective experience, \emph{F}(1.63,69.94) = 7.77, \emph{p} =
\par
.002; and confidence, \emph{F}(1.35,58.10) = 75.64, \emph{p} {$<$} .001. For subjective experience, comparisons
\par
between the different response categories demonstrated no significant difference in the
\par
proportion of responses assigned to Know and Familiar, \emph{t }{$<$} 1. All other comparisons were
\par
significant, all at least \emph{p} {$<$} .007; more responses were assigned to Remember than to Know or
\par
Familiar. For confidence judgments all comparisons were significantly different, all at least \emph{p} {$<$}
\par
.006. More responses were assigned to High compared to Medium or Low confidence, and
\par
more were assigned to Medium than to Low confidence. Comparing across judgment type,
\par
more responses were assigned to High confidence than to Remember, \emph{t}(43) = 4.87, \emph{p} {$<$} .001, and
\par
fewer responses were assigned to Low confidence than were assigned to Familiar, \emph{t}(43) = 6.35,
\par
\emph{p} {$<$} .001.
\par
The observed patterns suggest that while order does not influence the relationship
\par
between one judgment type and another, there are not straightforward mappings between
\par
confidence and subjective experience judgments. For instance, Remember judgments do not
\par
merely reflect the highest level of confidence; more items were assigned to High confidence
\par
than to Remember.
\par
To explore the level of accuracy associated with each response category \emph{a posteriori}
\par
probabilities were calculated. These probabilities were based on First Judgments only as forSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 25
\par
each item both First and Second Judgments were conditional on the same recognition response.
\par
Analysis was conducted separately for subjective experience and confidence judgments as the
\par
main comparison of interest was accuracy of response categories within judgment type.
\par
Separate 3(response category) ANOVAs demonstrated significant main effects of
\par
response category for subjective experience judgments, \emph{F}(1.60,62.54) = 23.79, \emph{p} {$<$} .001; and
\par
confidence judgments, \emph{F}(1.57,59.54) = 11.26, \emph{p} {$<$} .001; data shown in the top panel of Figure 2.
\par
For subjective experience judgments, no significant difference was found in the accuracy of
\par
responses assigned to Remember or Know, \emph{t }{$<$} 1, and both Remember and Know responses were
\par
significantly more likely to be correct than were responses assigned to Familiar, both \emph{p} {$<$} .001.
\par
Contrastingly, for confidence judgments, responses assigned to High confidence were
\par
significantly more likely to have been correct than responses assigned to Medium or Low
\par
confidence, both at least \emph{p} = .001; but Medium confidence responses were no more accurate
\par
than Low confidence responses, \emph{t}(38) = 1.36, \emph{p} = .18.
\par
[Insert Figure 2 about here]
\par
In sum, patterns of distributions of responses and accuracy of responses revealed that
\par
participants did not use confidence and subjective experience judgments in the same way.
\par
Participants were more lenient in their use of confidence judgments than subjective experience
\par
judgments and more responses were assigned to High confidence than to Remember. This fits
\par
with previous findings (Gardiner \& Java, 1990; Rajaram, 1993; Rajaram et al., 2002; Tunney \&
\par
Fernie, 2007) but demonstrates this without the inclusion of a manipulation such as a word/non-
\par
word comparison or masked priming. In the current experiment, confidence and subjective
\par
experience judgments produced different patterns on identical stimuli. Accuracy analysis
\par
further demonstrates the different ways participants used the two judgments. A linearSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 26
\par
relationship was demonstrated between confidence and accuracy, whereas the relationship was
\par
not linear for subjective experience judgments as Know recognition decisions were just as
\par
accurate as Remember recognition decisions. This latter finding replicates Dewhurst et al.
\par
(2009) and Sauerland \& Sporer (2009). With regards to the order in which the judgments were
\par
made, the \emph{a priori }analysis provides initial evidence that the order of the two judgment types did
\par
not influence distribution patterns across response categories.
\par
\emph{The influence of one judgment on the other}
\par
To fully explore whether making two judgments per item led to one judgment
\par
influencing the other judgment, conditional probabilities were calculated on correct recognition
\par
decisions in both temporal orders. Firstly, the proportion of items assigned to Remember,
\par
Know, Familiar, and Guess at Second Judgment was calculated split by which confidence level
\par
the item had been assigned to at First judgment; i.e., \emph{`of the items assigned to High confidence at}
\par
\emph{First judgment, what proportion were then assigned to each of the categories of subjective}
\par
\emph{experience at Second Judgment?'. }As shown in the upper panel of Figure 3, if an item had been
\par
assigned to High confidence at First Judgment then .61 of these items were then assigned to
\par
Remember at Second Judgment, .36 were assigned to Know, and .03 were assigned to Familiar.
\par
Contrastingly, if an item was assigned to Medium confidence at First Judgment then at Second
\par
Judgment .24 were assigned to Remember, .29 to Know, and .47 to Familiar; and so on.
\par
These proportions were also calculated in the opposite direction. In the counterbalanced
\par
design, participants either judged confidence followed by subjective experience, or the order of
\par
these two judgments was reversed. Data were next examined by calculating the proportion of
\par
items assigned to Remember, Know, Familiar, and Guess at First Judgment split by which
\par
confidence level the item had later been assigned to at Second judgment; i.e., \emph{`of the items that}SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 27
\par
\emph{were assigned to High confidence at Second Judgment, what proportion of these had come from}
\par
\emph{a Remember First Judgment?'. }Mean proportions are shown in the lower panel of Figure 3.
\par
Comparing the upper and lower panels of Figure 3 it is evident that the order in which
\par
judgments were made did not influence the relationship between confidence and subjective
\par
experience; the similarities across the two panels are plainly evident. For example, in the lower
\par
panel, if an item had been assigned to High confidence at Second Judgment then .64 of these
\par
items had previously been assigned to Remember at First Judgment, compared to .61 when the
\par
judgments were in the opposing order as shown in the upper panel. Comparing across panels,
\par
within each level of confidence the patterns of means are paralleled for each category of
\par
subjective experience.
