Separate 2(judgment order) x 3(response category) ANOVAs for items assigned to each
\par
of the confidence levels compared the distribution of subjective experience responses at each
\par
confidence level across the panels of Figure 3; i.e., how correct recognition responses that were
\par
assigned to High confidence were distributed across subjective experience categories whether
\par
judgment order was confidence then subjective experience or the reverse. At each level of
\par
confidence, ANOVA demonstrated no main effect of judgment order, all \emph{F }{$<$} 1; a significant
\par
main effect of response category: High, \emph{F}(1.08,45.37) = 39.36, \emph{p} {$<$} .001, Medium, \emph{F}(2,58) =
\par
2.60, \emph{p} = .083 -- though this effect only approached significance, and Low, \emph{F}(1.22,30.60) =
\par
90.48, \emph{p} {$<$} .001; and no interaction between judgment order and response category, all \emph{F }{$<$} 1.
