confidence ratings to Remember, Know, and Familiar items demonstrated a significant main
\par
effect of response category, \emph{F}(2,76) = 101.41, \emph{p} {$<$} .001. Items assigned to Remember (\emph{M }=
\par
2.75, \emph{SD }= .44) or Know (\emph{M }= 2.65, \emph{SD }= .38) were both given higher confidence ratings than
\par
items assigned to Familiar (\emph{M }= 1.52, \emph{SD }= .51): \emph{t}(38) = 12.41, \emph{p} {$<$} .001 and \emph{t}(38) = 12.73, \emph{p} {$<$}
\par
.001 respectively. However, there was no significant difference between the confidence ratings
\par
given to Remember or Know items, \emph{t }{$<$} 1. Though not included in ANOVA, mean confidence
\par
for Guess items was 0.81 (\emph{SD }= .57).
\par
In sum, exploration of the influence of one judgment type on the other further
\par
demonstrated that participants did not simply map the four levels of confidence onto the four
\par
categories of subjective experience. For example, over 80\% of Remember responses were also
\par
assigned to High confidence, but when proportions were calculated based on confidence
\par
response, only approximately 60\% of items which were assigned to High confidence were alsoSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 31
\par
Remembered. The two main points to take from this analysis are that participants did not
\par
simply assign all Remember items to High confidence, Know items to Medium confidence, and
\par
Familiar items to Low confidence and vice versa; however, the patterns obtained for assignment
\par
of responses were matched across judgment order. While all items that were given Remember
\par
responses at First Judgment were not assigned to High confidence at Second judgment, the
\par
patterns obtained in that judgment order matched the patterns of responding when the order of
\par
judgments was reversed. Whilst subjective experience and confidence judgments are no doubt
\par
related, concerns such as those voiced by Bruno and Rutherford (2010) regarding the influence
\par
of one judgment on the other do not have credence. Here responses at Second Judgment were
\par
not influenced by what response had been made at First Judgment.
\par
Regarding categories of subjective experience, analysis of mean confidence demonstrates
\par
that items that were assigned to Remember and Know did not differ in terms of confidence;
\par
adding to the previous finding that Remember and Know did not differ in terms of accuracy.
\par
However, both accuracy and confidence ratings differentiated Know from Familiar responses.
\par
The issue of the separation of Know and Familiar subjective experiences is returned to in the
\par
General Discussion.
\par
\emph{Reaction times for recognition decisions}
\par
As discussed earlier, the typical form of analysis to explore reaction time is to analyse
\par
RTs for recognition decisions split by which subjective experience category the item was later
\par
assigned to. In the current experiment this analysis was performed on correct recognition RTs
\par
classified by the confidence or subjective experience response category the item was then
\par
assigned to at First Judgment. Mean correct recognition decision RTs are shown in the upper
\par
panel of Figure 5.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 32
