patterns are matched across the two judgment types.
\par
In sum, recognition RT analysis demonstrated a case where confidence and subjective
\par
experience responses did not differ. There was no difference in speed of recognition when items
\par
were later categorised as Remember or Know, and recognition of these items was faster than
\par
items later assigned to Familiar or Guess. Similarly, recognition of items later classed as High
\par
or Medium confidence was equally fast and was faster than for items assigned to Low or None.
\par
Taken together with the findings regarding accuracy, where a significant difference betweenSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 33
\par
High and Medium confidence responses was demonstrated, this again highlights that subjective
\par
experience and confidence judgments behave in different ways: High and Medium confidence
\par
recognition decisions are differentiated by accuracy but not RT, while Remember and Know
\par
