\par
\emph{Reaction times for subjective experience and confidence judgments}
\par
A novel approach to RT analysis here was how quickly participants made their actual
\par
judgments of confidence and subjective experience. Firstly, the overall mean RTs for
\par
confidence and subjective experience judgments to correct recognition decisions were compared
\par
irrespective of response category; data shown in Table 3. A 2(judgment type) x 2(judgment
\par
order) ANOVA demonstrated a significant main effect of judgment type,\emph{ F}(1,43) = 25.51, \emph{p} {$<$}
\par
.001; confidence judgments were made more quickly than judgments of subjective experience.
\par
The main effect of judgment order approached significance, \emph{F}(1,43) = 3.04, \emph{p} = .09, as for both
\par
judgment types Second Judgments were slightly faster than First Judgments. The interaction
\par
between judgment type and judgment order was not significant, \emph{F }{$<$} 1.
\par
[Insert Table 3 about here]
\par
Further analysis examined whether differences in RT were observed for different
\par
response categories within judgment types. As there was no effect of judgment order in the 2x2
\par
ANOVA, data were aggregated across order. Mean judgment RTs split by response category
\par
are shown in the lower panel of Figure 5. Separate 3(response category) ANOVAs for
\par
confidence and subjective experience revealed a significant main effect of response for
\par
confidence judgments, \emph{F}(1.25,50.06) = 6.61, \emph{p} = .009; but no main effect of response for
\par
subjective experience, \emph{F }{$<$} 1. As shown in Figure 5, time to make subjective experienceSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 34
\par
judgments was stable at around 2000ms, whilst for confidence judgments speed increased as
\par
confidence increased.
\par
For confidence, High confidence judgments were made significantly faster than both
\par
Medium and Low confidence judgments, at least \emph{p} = .003. The difference in speed between
\par
Medium and Low confidence judgments was not significant, \emph{t}(40) = 1.34, \emph{p} = .19. For
\par
subjective experience, there were no significant differences between the time to make
\par
Remember, Know, or Familiar judgments, all at least \emph{p }{$>$} .26.The novel analysis of comparing
\par
RT to make judgments independent of recognition RT confirmed the prediction that, on the
\par
whole, confidence judgments would be made more quickly than judgments of subjective
\par
experience. It is suggested that it is simply easier to assess memory strength than whether or not
\par
you can recollect details from the study episode and this is demonstrated via reaction time. No
\par
interaction with judgment order was demonstrated however: confidence judgments were not
\par
speeded when made following a judgment of recollective experience.
\par
Contrasting patterns of RT were demonstrated within judgment types. Confidence
\par
judgments were made more quickly the more confident they were -- paralleling the speed
\par
advantage for High confidence recognition decisions -- but, as predicted, no differences in RT
\par
were demonstrated for subjective experience judgments. It is suggested that this is related to
\par
recollection being a threshold process (e.g., Yonelinas \& Parks, 2007). Though Remembered
\par
items have quicker recognition decision RTs, when making the subjective experience judgment
\par
itself, if no recollection of the study episode comes to mind search is terminated and a different
\par
subjective experience response is made. The time to make a judgment of any category of
\par
subjective experience is therefore related to the time spent undertaking additional metacognitive
\par
processing. This appears to be quite a long process compared to making a confidence judgment.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 35
\par
So why might judgments of subjective experience take so long? If making judgments
\par
which require recalling information from the encoding phase is a slow and effortful process
\par
involving retrieval reaching the threshold of activation for recollection (e.g., Yonelinas \& Parks,
\par
2007) or mental time travel (e.g., Tulving, 1985), it might be expected that making a judgment
\par
of source will take just as long as making a judgment of subjective experience. On this basis,
\par
Experiments 2 and 3 were designed with the aim of following up the slower reaction times for
\par
subjective experience judgments demonstrated in Experiment 1. However, designing these two
\par
experiments also enabled the examination of a number of previously un-researched relationships
\par
such as the relationship between source accuracy and confidence and source judgments and
\par
Remember, Know, Familiar and Guess subjective experiences.
\par
\textbf{Experiments 2 and 3}
\par
\textbf{Comparing subjective experience and source judgments}
\par
In contrast to confidence and subjective experience judgments which cannot be
\par
objectively verified, source memory judgments can be objectively scored as correct or incorrect.
\par
Many researchers have therefore combined judgments of source with confidence and/or
\par
subjective experience in attempts to tap both subjective and objective measures of recollection.
\par
The central theoretical concern that has arisen from research comparing judgments of source
\par
and subjective experience is whether accurate source should be able to be retrieved from
\par
memory when an item is assigned to Know. Previous research has produced mixed results
\par
regarding the accuracy of source for Know items. Some studies have demonstrated source
\par
accuracy to only be at chance levels for Know items, which reflects the interpretation of Know
\par
responses being recognition unaccompanied by any contextual details from encoding (Dewhurst
\par
\& Hitch, 1999; Dudukovic \& Knowlton, 2006; Perfect, Mayes, Downes, \& Van Eijk, 1996,SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 36
\par
except for Experiment 3). However, other studies have found source accuracy to be above
\par
chance for items assigned to Know (Conway \& Dewhurst, 1995b; Hicks, Marsh, \& Ritschel,
\par
2002; Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002; Meiser \& Sattler, 2007; Starns \& Hicks, 2005). This suggests
\par
that in some cases enough contextual details from the study phase are retrieved at recognition
\par
for source to be judged correctly but not enough details are recollected for the item to be
\par
assigned to Remember.
\par
In the above studies there were a number of methodological differences that are relevant
\par
to the current experiments. Five of the eight studies used a standard Old-New recognition
\par
paradigm with judgments of source and/or subjective experience being made to only items
\par
judged Old, whereas three used a one-step procedure by including a `New' category with the
\par
first judgment made. Perfect et al. (1996) and Starns and Hicks (2005) asked participants to
\par
make a Remember-Know-New judgment followed by a source judgment and Hicks et al. (2002)
\par
had participants make a Left source-Right source-New judgment followed by a judgment of
\par
subjective experience. These methodological differences do not map onto the differences in
\par
findings however; the five studies which found Know items to have above chance source
\par
accuracy are not the five that incorporated a separate Old-New judgment. While one-step and
\par
two-step procedures have been shown to influence patterns of responding for Remember-Know
\par
judgments (cf., Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010; Eldridge et al., 2002; Hicks \& Marsh, 1999; see
\par
General Introduction) and source judgments (Dodson \& Johnson, 1993; Marsh \& Hicks, 1998)
\par
individually, the above studies that employed both types of judgment do not show any clear
\par
patterns regarding judgment order.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 37
\par
\textbf{Comparing source and confidence judgments}
\par
Confidence and source judgments have not been directly compared in the same way as
\par
confidence and subjective experience: \emph{do these judgments produce the same patterns of}
\par
\emph{responding? }(Gardiner \& Java, 1990; Rajaram, 1993; Rajaram et al., 2002), or in the same way
\par
as source and subjective experience have: \emph{can source for Know judgments be accurate?}
\par
(Conway \& Dewhurst, 1995b; Hicks et al., 2002; Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002; Meiser \& Sattler,
\par
2007; Perfect et al., 1996; Starns \& Hicks, 2005). Instead, studies have typically combined the
\par
two judgments into a source-confidence judgment. For example, in four experiments Yonelinas
\par
(1999) compared recognition-confidence and source-confidence judgments. These were both
\par
measured on 6-point scales from `\emph{1 = confident new'} to `\emph{6 = confident old'}, and from \emph{`1 = sure}
\par
\emph{it was spoken by the female voice', }to \emph{`6 = sure it was spoken by the male voice'. }Results
\par
demonstrated that the higher the recognition-confidence or source-confidence judgment the
\par
higher the recognition or source accuracy. In addition, the ROC curves differed for recognition-
\par
confidence and source-confidence and the shapes of the curves obtained fit with the prediction
\par
that recognition confidence can rely on both familiarity and recollection while source
\par
confidence relies primarily on recollection (Yonelinas, 1999).
\par
Other researchers have used similar approaches, combining source-confidence measures
\par
with recognition-confidence ratings (and judgments of subjective experience) and obtained
\par
similar findings with recognition-confidence ratings being higher for accurate, and higher
\par
confidence, source judgments (e.g., Rotello et al., 2005; Slotnick, 2010). However, the shapes
\par
of their ROC curves led these authors to suggest different interpretations of recollection and
\par
familiarity. Although these findings are relevant to the ongoing debates surrounding
\par
recollection and familiarity processes, the current experiments operationalize and examineSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 38
\par
source, confidence, and subjective experience judgments in a different manner not directly
\par
comparable to these ROC studies.
\par
In relation to the subjective experience and confidence judgments made in Experiments 2
\par
and 3 it is predicted that distribution patterns, accuracy, and RT will parallel those observed in
\par
Experiment 1. Distribution patterns, accuracy, and RT for these two judgment types are also
\par
then able to be explored split by whether the accompanying source judgment is correct or
\par
incorrect. As an accurate judgment of source requires retrieval of contextual information from
\par
the study episode it is predicted that correct source items will be associated with higher levels of
\par
confidence and a greater proportion of Remember responses than incorrect source items. In line
\par
with the majority of previous research it is also predicted that while source accuracy will be
\par
greatest for items assigned to Remember, a large proportion of items assigned to Know will also
\par
be accompanied by accurate source judgments (Conway \& Dewhurst, 1995b; Hicks et al., 2002;
\par
Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002; Meiser \& Sattler, 2007; Starns \& Hicks, 2005). Furthermore, the use of
\par
separate categories of Know and Familiar in the current experiments permits the accuracy of
\par
source judgments for these two subjective experiences to be compared. If source accuracy
\par
differs for Know and Familiar this will add to the validity of separating these two categories of
\par
subjective experience.
\par
\textbf{The influence of one judgment on another}
\par
As with the influence of confidence and subjective experience judgments on each other,
\par
to date no direct manipulation of judgment order for source and subjective experience
\par
judgments has been published. As discussed earlier, experiments comparing source accuracy
\par
and subjective experience have employed a variety of different procedures and findings have
\par
been mixed (cf. Dewhurst \& Hitch, 1999; Perfect et al., 1996). With regard to the order ofSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 39
\par
source and subjective experience judgments when made after Old-New recognition all five
\par
studies which used this procedure asked for subjective experience first and then source (though
\par
in Conway and Dewhurst (1995b) participants actually made separate RK judgments for their
\par
recognition decision and their source judgment). Although no published study has manipulated
\par
judgment order for source and subjective experience judgments, one Master's dissertation from
\par
Jason Hicks' lab has examined this manipulation.
\par
Martin (2007, \emph{unpublished thesis}) manipulated order of source and subjective experience
\par
judgments between participants. At study, words were presented either on the left or the right of
\par
the computer screen and at test half the participants made a Remember-Know-New judgment5
\par
followed by a Left-Right source judgment, and the other half made a Left-Right-New judgment
\par
followed by Remember-Know. Many of the general patterns of results replicated previous
\par
findings. Source accuracy to Remember items was higher than source accuracy for Know
\par
items; and source accuracy for Know items was higher than chance (Conway \& Dewhurst,
\par
1995b; Hicks et al., 2002; Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002; Meiser \& Sattler, 2007; Starns \& Hicks,
\par
2005). However, findings regarding the effect of judgment order were mixed. Participants who
\par
made subjective experience judgments first gave more Remember responses to hits than
\par
participants who made this judgment following a source judgment, though this result was only
\par
marginal. For source accuracy, no main effect of judgment order was observed when source
\par
accuracy was analysed collapsed across subjective experience, though puzzlingly, when
\par
response category (Remember-Know) was included in analysis a main effect was reported with
\par
higher source accuracy when source was the first judgment made. Additionally, the discussion
\par
states that the source-first group showed better source memory for Remember items than the
\par
subjective experience-first group, however an interaction between judgment order andSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 40
\par
subjective experience nor any separate analysis for Remember items was reported. These
\par
discrepancies are not explained and it cannot be known which of these opposing findings may
\par
be an error in analysis, or an error in reporting. While no study has explored the influence of
\par
source and confidence judgments on each other, and these very mixed findings from an
\par
unpublished dissertation should be considered with caution, it is of interest that the influence of
\par
one judgment on the other has begun to be explored.
\par
The influence of one judgment on the other is again of key interest in Experiments 2 and
\par
3. While no effects of judgment order were obtained in Experiment 1, it is suggested that the
\par
objective nature of source judgments could lead them to have more influence over subsequent
\par
judgments. Perhaps when a source judgment is made first and one element of source context is
\par
retrieved this may influence the participant to make a Remember or High confidence response
\par
somewhat more leniently as was demonstrated, albeit only marginally, by Martin (2007). For
\par
example, if `only' the required Left/Right source information is retrieved the item may still be
\par
assigned to Remember even if recognition is not accompanied by any feelings of mental time
\par
travel or recollection of thoughts that came to mind during study which would have normally be
\par
required for the item to exceed that individual's Remember threshold. Additionally, the
\par
accuracy of source judgments could be influenced by whether they precede or follow a
\par
judgment of confidence or subjective experience as was suggested by some of Martin's (2007)
\par
data. To examine these suggestions patterns of judgment distribution are again explored by
\par
judgment order.
\par
\textbf{Reaction time for judgments of source}
\par
In addition to high confidence and recollection speeding recognition decisions, some
\par
research has also shown recognition to be faster for items when source is accurate compared toSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 41
\par
inaccurate. Using a one-step source procedure where participants categorised items as either
\par
`Old - mentally imaged at study', or `Old - read backwards at study', or `New', Kahn, Davachi,
\par
and Wagner (2004) found item + source recognition (Old + correct source) decisions were
\par
approximately 250ms faster than item only recognition (Old + incorrect source), but only for
\par
items which had been mentally imaged at study. Similar results were obtained by Lundstrom,
\par
Ingvar, and Petersson (2005) who also used a one-step procedure. However, opposing results
\par
have been demonstrated using a two-step procedure. Wilding and Rugg (1996) presented items
\par
in male or female voices at study and correct recognition responses were categorised by whether
\par
the subsequent judgment of source was correct. No differences in recognition RTs for correct
\par
and incorrect source items were found.
\par
In the current two experiments, analysis of RTs to make post-recognition judgments
\par
again allows completely novel analysis to be performed: the comparison of how long it takes to
\par
make judgments of source, confidence, and subjective experience. As source judgments require
\par
assessment of retrieved contextual information it is predicted that RT for accurate source
\par
judgments will be approximately equal to those for subjective experience, which were slower
\par
than confidence judgments in Experiment 1. Whether inaccurate source judgment RTs or their
\par
prior recognition decision RTs will be slower than those for accurate source judgments will also
\par
be explored as, as discussed above, some previous research on source recognition RTs has
\par
demonstrated differences while other research has not (Kahn et al., 2004; Lundstrom et al.,
\par
2005; Wilding \& Rugg, 1996).
\par
In Experiments 2 and 3, influence is also able to be explored in the RT analysis by
\par
examining whether the subjective experience category or confidence level an item was assigned
\par
to differentiated how long it took to make an accurate source judgment. For example, it couldSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 42
\par
be suggested that accurate judgments of source accompanied by High confidence or
\par
Remembering may be made more quickly than accurate judgments of source where confidence
\par
is not so high or the recognition is based on Familiarity. Final analysis in these experiments
\par
focuses on this novel method of exploring judgment RTs.
\par
\textbf{The current experiments}
\par
The materials and basic procedures employed in Experiments 2 and 3 were identical to
\par
those in Experiment 1: Participants studied medium-frequency words and at test made an Old-
\par
New recognition judgment followed by two post-recognition judgments. However, in order that
\par
retrieval of source could be tested at recognition, words were presented for study on either the
\par
left or the right of the screen with an encoding instruction to associate words on the left with one
\par
person and words on the right with another person (after Yonelinas, 1999). The two critical
\par
judgments made post-recognition were source and subjective experience in Experiment 2 and
\par
source and confidence in Experiment 3. Subjective experience and confidence judgments were
\par
made using the same four response options as in Experiment 1 and for source judgments
\par
participants selected whether they thought the word had been studied on the Left or the Right.
\par
The order in which participants made the two critical judgments was counterbalanced across
\par
items.
\par
\textbf{Methods}
\par
\emph{Participants and Designs}
\par
Thirty-two undergraduate students (30 female) participated in Experiment 2, mean age
\par
18.88 years (range 18 to 21). Thirty-four undergraduate students (32 female) participated in
\par
Experiment 3, mean age 18.62 years (range 18 to 20). All participants were from the University
\par
of Leeds and received Participant Pool Credits for taking part. Participants were tested inSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 43
\par
groups of between five and thirteen at individual PCs. In Experiment 2, data from two
\par
participants was excluded from analysis as they did not follow experimental instructions. Both
\par
experiments employed within-subjects designs and for the study and test phases all instructions
\par
and stimuli were presented, and data collected, using E-Prime version 1.2.
\par
\emph{Materials and Procedures}
\par
The materials and procedures for Experiments 2 and 3 were identical to that of
\par
Experiment 1 except changes due to the source manipulation. In both experiments, in the study
\par
phase target words were presented individually to either the left or the right of the computer
\par
screen (placement randomised). Participants were instructed that as well as trying to remember
\par
all the words, they should also try to remember on which side of the screen the word was shown.
\par
To improve performance participants were instructed to associate words on the left with one
\par
person and words shown on the right with another person (Yonelinas, 1999). Participants were
\par
told they could associate the words with anyone, e.g., celebrities/family/friends, and were asked
\par
to write the names of their chosen people on their response booklet (to check they followed this
\par
instruction correctly). All other aspects of the study and distracter phases were identical to those
\par
of Experiment 1.
\par
In the test phases of both experiments participants were instructed that for each word
\par
recognised as Old they would then be asked to make two judgments from the list of four
\par
judgments. The four judgments were then explained on individual screens. The subjective
\par
experience (Experiment 2 only), confidence (Experiment 3 only), AoA, and pleasantness
\par
judgments and instructions were identical to those in Experiment 1. For source judgments the
\par
screen consisted of two boxes labelled `Left' and `Right' with the question `Which side of the
\par
screen was this word shown on?' below. These two response boxes were the same size andSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 44
\par
shape as the four response boxes required for the subjective experience and confidence
\par
judgments. For each source judgment the two response boxes were positioned below the re-
\par
presented word, equidistant from the centre of the screen.
\par
Participants underwent the recognition test in the same manner as in Experiment 1. Each
\par
word was presented in the centre of the screen with the cues `New' and `Old' presented below
\par
and towards the left and right of the screen respectively, reflecting the number keys which
\par
participants were required to use to make their Old-New judgment: 1 for New, 2 for Old. When
\par
participants indicated that they recognised a word as Old the first judgment screen was shown.
\par
In Experiment 2, participants were always required to make either a source judgment or a
\par
subjective experience judgment first, followed by another type of judgment (of the three not
\par
already made). The critical trials were those where participants made a combination of a source
\par
judgment and a subjective experience judgment, either source then subjective experience or
\par
subjective experience then source. In Experiment 3, participants were always required to make
\par
either a source judgment or a confidence judgment first, followed by another type of judgment.
\par
The critical trials were those where participants made either source then confidence or
\par
confidence then source.
\par
\textbf{Results and Discussion}
\par
Analysis was conducted in the same manner as in Experiment 1 with the addition of
\par
comparisons split by source accuracy. Memory performance was examined, followed by overall
\par
source accuracy and the proportion of correct responses assigned to confidence levels and
\par
subjective experience categories using both \emph{a priori }and \emph{a posteriori }methods. The influence of
\par
First Judgment on Second Judgment was then explored. Reaction times for recognition
\par
decisions and judgments were analysed by source accuracy, confidence level, or subjectiveSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 45
\par
experience category. Prior to analysis, data from any responses which had been made faster
\par
than 300ms or slower than 8000ms were excluded from the dataset. Data from Guess and None
\par
responses was excluded from all ANOVA to avoid violating the assumption of independence in
\par
analysis of \emph{a priori }proportional data and to increase listwise N in analysis of RT and \emph{a}
\par
\emph{posteriori }accuracy data (though data are included on figures). Greenhouse-Geisser values are
\par
reported when ANOVA did not meet assumptions of sphericity. The central findings from each
\par
analysis are summarised within the following sections and more substantive theoretical
\par
discussion is reserved until the General Discussion. Exploratory statistical analysis comparing
\par
experiments is carried out where appropriate.
\par
\emph{Memory measures}
\par
Memory performance measures for Experiments 2 and 3 are shown in Table 2. In both
\par
experiments memory performance was higher than in Experiment 1. The source encoding
\par
manipulation appears to have increased participants' encoding proficiency leading to higher
\par
performance. Participants made FAs to under 10\% of lure items and therefore further analysis
\par
of FA data was not performed.
