\emph{Summary of influence data}
\par
No influence of judgment order was demonstrated in Experiments 2 and 3. Patterns of
\par
responses for conditional probabilities were identical whether source had been judged before or
\par
after subjective experience or confidence. This does not fit with the findings of Martin (2007)
\par
who reported that source judgments were more accurate when performed first and that higher
\par
levels of Remember were demonstrated when subjective experience was judged second. One
\par
possible explanation is that the procedure used by Martin (2007) conflated the first judgment
\par
participants made with the recognition judgment. At test half Martin's participants made a
\par
Remember-Know-New judgment followed by a Left-Right source judgment, and the other half
\par
made a Left-Right-New judgment followed by Remember-Know. However, one-step and two-
\par
step procedures have been demonstrated to produce different patterns of responding for both
\par
subjective experience (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010; Eldridge et al., 2002; Hicks \& Marsh, 1999)
\par
and source judgments (Dodson \& Johnson, 1993; Marsh \& Hicks, 1998). It is suggested that
\par
using a one-step procedure for his first judgment led to the results reported by Martin (2007) and
\par
that when recognition decisions and judgments are recorded separately, as in the current study,
\par
judgments of source, confidence, and subjective experience, do not influence each other.
\par
The conditional probabilities for subjective experience and confidence judgments
\par
separated by source accuracy did reveal very different relationships between confidence and
\par
source, and subjective experience and source. In Experiment 2, subjective experience varied
\par
systematically for both correct and incorrect source items. The greatest proportion of items was
\par
assigned to Remember when source was correct whereas the greatest proportion of items wereSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 55
\par
assigned to Familiar when source was incorrect. In contrast, for confidence judgments
\par
(Experiment 3), whilst accurate source items were primarily retrieved with the highest level of
\par
confidence, for items that received incorrect source judgments the confidence accompanying
\par
retrieval did not vary systematically. This difference in the relationship between confidence and
\par
source and subjective experience and source is returned to in the General Discussion.
\par
When conditional probabilities were calculated in the opposing direction, in addition to
\par
Remembered items, both Know and Familiar items were associated with above-chance source
\par
accuracy. This fits with previous research that has demonstrated that accurate source can
\par
accompany retrieval of items assigned to Know (Conway \& Dewhurst, 1995b; Hicks et al.,
\par
2002; Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002; Meiser \& Sattler, 2007; Starns \& Hicks, 2005). While it may
\par
appear that the finding of above-chance source accuracy for Familiar items does not fit with this
\par
literature, it should be noted that previous studies have conflated Know and Familiar into one
\par
judgment. The separation of Know and Familiar is returned to in the General Discussion. For
\par
confidence judgments, source accuracy was not able to be calculated for the different levels of
\par
confidence as participants had assigned over 70\% of items to High confidence. However, as
\par
predicted, mean confidence ratings for accurate source items were significantly higher than for
\par
inaccurate source items (Rotello et al., 2005; Slotnick, 2010; Yonelinas, 1999).
\par
\emph{Reaction times for recognition decisions}
\par
Correct recognition RTs were first examined classified by accuracy of source judgment
\par
(at First Judgment). Separate 2(source accuracy) ANOVAs for recognition RTs for
\par
Experiments 2 and 3 produced different results. In Experiment 2, the difference in RTs only
\par
approached significance, \emph{F}(1,26) = 2.55, \emph{p }= .12; correct source recognition decisions were
\par
marginally quicker (\emph{M }= 1465ms, \emph{SD }= 406ms) than incorrect source recognition decisions (\emph{M }=SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 56
\par
1644ms, \emph{SD }= 698). However, in Experiment 3 the difference in RTs was significant, \emph{F}(1,29) =
\par
5.73, \emph{p }= .023; correct source recognition decisions were significantly quicker (\emph{M }= 1640ms, \emph{SD}
\par
= 584ms) than incorrect source recognition decisions (\emph{M }= 1852ms, \emph{SD }= 765ms). As there
\par
seemed to be no influence of subjective experience on source in Experiment 2, one might
\par
assume that the lack of an effect in Experiment 2 was due to a lack of power. Since
\par
Experiments 2 and 3 were identical in terms of memory performance, source accuracy, stimuli,
\par
and all elements of methodology (except other critical post-recognition judgment), it is possible
\par
to increase power for this analysis by combining the tasks. A 2(source accuracy) ANOVA
\par
demonstrated that, aggregated across experiment, correct recognition decisions followed by
\par
correct source judgments (\emph{M }= 1557ms, \emph{SD }= 511ms) were significantly faster than those
\par
followed by incorrect source judgments (\emph{M }= 1753ms, \emph{SD }= 735ms), \emph{F}(1,56) = 7.87, \emph{p }= .007.
\par
Thus it could tentatively be concluded that RTs are faster for recognition decisions where source
\par
judgments are ultimately correct. This conclusion fits with some previous findings (Kahn et al.,
\par
2004; Lundstrom et al., 2005) but not others (Wilding \& Rugg, 1996).
\par
Correct recognition RTs were next examined classified by the confidence or subjective
\par
experience response category the item was assigned to at First Judgment; data shown in the
\par
upper panel of Figure 8. For Experiment 2, a 3(response category) ANOVA for recognition
\par
RTs categorised by later subjective experience demonstrated a significant main effect of
\par
response category, \emph{F}(1.37,30.10) = 4.90, \emph{p }= .025. Correct recognition decisions for items later
\par
categorised as Remember or Know were made significantly faster than decisions for items later
\par
categorised as Familiar, both at least \emph{p} = .031. There was no difference in speed of correct
\par
recognition if later categorised as Remember or Know, \emph{t }{$<$} 1. For Experiment 3, a 3(response
\par
category) ANOVA demonstrated a significant main effect of response category, \emph{F}(1.49,34.28) =SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 57
\par
10.95, \emph{p }= .001. Correct recognition decisions for items later categorised as High or Medium
\par
confidence were significantly faster than decisions for items later categorised as Low
\par
confidence, both at least, \emph{p} = .004. There was no difference in the speed of correct recognition
\par
if later categorised as High or Medium confidence, \emph{t}(23) = 1.04, \emph{p} = .31. These findings for
\par
speed of recognition decision categorised by later subjective experience or confidence replicated
\par
those obtained in Experiment 1.
\par
