\emph{Reaction times for subjective experience, confidence, and source judgments}
\par
These two experiments were again interested in how quickly participants had made their
\par
post-recognition judgments; data shown in Table 3. Firstly, source judgment RTs split by
\par
accuracy and judgment order were compared across experiments. Experiment was included as a
\par
factor in order to examine whether RTs for source were consistent across experiments or
\par
whether source was speeded when participants were also required to report subjective
\par
experience or confidence. A 2(experiment) x 2(source accuracy) x 2(judgment order) ANOVA
\par
demonstrated no main effect of experiment, \emph{F }{$<$} 1; a significant main effect of source accuracy,
\par
\emph{F}(1,46) = 26.49, \emph{p }{$<$} .001; but no main effect of judgment order, \emph{F }{$<$} 1; and no significant
\par
interactions, all at least \emph{p }{$>$} .30. Correct source judgments were faster than incorrect source
\par
judgments in both experiments, both \emph{p }{$<$} .002. In sum, there were no differences in RT to make
\par
source judgments whether they were made as a First or Second judgment; and correct source
\par
judgments were made more quickly than incorrect source judgments. Taken together with the
\par
recognition RT data these results show a clear picture: when accurate source information does
\par
not come to mind when an item is presented RTs are slower for both the initial recognition
\par
response and subsequent source judgment.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 58
\par
The next analysis of interest was comparisons across judgment type. Table 3 includes
\par
mean time to make confidence and subjective experience judgments in Experiments 2 and 3.
\par
These RTs were compared against RTs for correct source judgments6. For Experiment 2, a
\par
2(judgment type) x 2(judgment order) ANOVA comparing subjective experience and source
\par
RTs demonstrated a significant main effect of judgment type, \emph{F}(1,31) = 17.17, \emph{p }{$<$} .001, no
\par
main effect of judgment order, \emph{F }{$<$} 1, and no interaction between the two, \emph{F }{$<$} 1. For
\par
Experiment 3, a 2(judgment type) x 2(judgment order) ANOVA comparing confidence and
\par
source judgment RTs demonstrated a significant main effect of judgment type, \emph{F}(1,33) = 18.26,
\par
\emph{p }{$<$} .001, no main effect of judgment order, \emph{F}(1,33) = 1.75, \emph{p }= .20, and no interaction between
\par
the two, \emph{F }{$<$} 1. Source judgments were made significantly faster than both confidence
\par
judgments and subjective experience judgments and order of judgment did not influence time to
\par
make judgment. Mean RTs for confidence and subjective experience judgments in these
\par
experiments parallel those in Experiment 1.
\par
The finding that source judgments were made more quickly than both confidence and
\par
subjective experience judgments was not predicted. It was thought that RTs for source
\par
judgments would be similar to those for subjective experience judgments as both require
\par
assessment of what contextual information is able to be retrieved from memory. A possible
\par
explanation for this finding is that in these experiments source judgments were able to be made
\par
quickly due to the ease of the judgment. Source judgments were only 2AFC (compared to some
\par
experimental designs where multiple types of source information are tested at once, e.g., Meiser
\par
\& Br\"oder, 2002), and the encoding manipulation whereby participants were instructed to
\par
associate words on the left with one person and words on the right with another person led to
\par
improved recognition memory and source judgments being correct over 80\% of the time. If theSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 59
\par
task had been more difficult, source judgments may require more processing time and RTs may
\par
be slower.
\par
Further analysis examined whether differences in RT were observed for different
\par
response categories within the confidence and subjective experience judgment types. As the
\par
effect of order in the 2x2 ANOVA had not reached significance, data were aggregated across
\par
judgment order. Mean judgment RTs split by response category are shown in the lower panel of
\par
Figure 8. Separate 3(response category) ANOVAs for confidence and subjective experience
\par
