\textbf{General Discussion}
\par
Comparing source, confidence, and subjective experience judgments when the order of
\par
judgments was counterbalanced post-recognition, three experiments demonstrated no
\par
differences in patterns of judgment, relationships with source, or reaction times across judgmentSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 62
\par
order. Therefore it appears that the fear of contamination of one judgment by the other was
\par
unfounded (Bruno \& Rutherford, 2010; Holmes et al., 1998; Martin, 2007; Rajaram et al.,
\par
2002). Alternatively, it could be argued that all judgments were confounded by the judgment
\par
that accompanied them. Comparisons across experiments suggest that this is not the case.
\par
Experiment 1 compared subjective experience judgments with confidence judgments in a
\par
within-subjects design with judgment order counterbalanced across items. Conversely,
\par
Experiments 2 and 3 only employed one of these judgment types and compared it against
\par
judgments of source. The patterns for subjective experience and confidence judgments obtained
\par
in these latter two experiments replicate those obtained when subjective experience and
\par
confidence judgments were made together (Experiment 1). More specifically, if subjective
\par
experience was influenced by being made alongside confidence judgments, then this influence
\par
must be identical to that exerted when subjective experience judgments are accompanied by
\par
source judgments, as the distribution of subjective experience responses and accuracy of these
\par
responses were the same in Experiments 1 and 2. This comparison of patterns alone strongly
\par
supports the assertion that making two judgments together does not lead to contamination of one
\par
judgment by the other.
\par
The current experiments were the first to compare four categories of subjective
\par
experience: Remember, Know, Familiar, and Guess, against four levels of confidence, and
\par
combine both these measures with judgments of source. Although the order of post-recognition
\par
judgments demonstrated no influence of one judgment type on another, distribution of responses
\par
across subjective experience categories and confidence levels, their associated source accuracy,
\par
and their reaction times, revealed hitherto unknown relationships between these different
\par
measures.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 63
\par
\textbf{Comparing subjective experience and confidence}
\par
Both distributions and accuracy of responses demonstrated that participants did not use
\par
confidence and subjective experience judgments in the same way. Participants were more
\par
lenient in their use of confidence judgments than subjective experience judgments: more
\par
responses were assigned to High confidence than to Remember. This fits with previous findings
\par
(Gardiner \& Java, 1990; Rajaram, 1993; Rajaram et al., 2002; Tunney \& Fernie, 2007), but the
\par
current experiments were the first to demonstrate this by contrasting the four categories of
\par
subjective experience against four levels of confidence. Accuracy analysis demonstrated that
\par
Medium confidence responses were less accurate than High confidence responses, whereas
\par
Know responses were as accurate as Remember responses. This latter finding replicates
\par
Dewhurst et al. (2009) and Sauerland and Sporer (2009) but the differentiation across
\par
confidence and subjective experience categories had not been shown before. In Experiment 1,
\par
mean confidence ratings also demonstrated no differences between Known and Remembered
\par
items.
\par
Distribution of judgments conditional on previous response and which type of judgment
\par
had been made first or second further demonstrated that participants used subjective experience
\par
categories and confidence levels in different ways, while also supporting the conclusion that the
\par
order in which judgments were made did not influence response patterns. For example, there
\par
were no differences in the proportion of High confidence items assigned to Remember whether
\par
judgment order was confidence then subjective experience or subjective experience then
\par
confidence. However, there was a difference when comparing the proportion of Remember
\par
responses assigned to High confidence with the proportion of High confidence responses that
\par
were assigned to Remember (cf. Figures 3 and 4). The four categories of the two different typesSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 64
\par
of judgment did not simply map onto each other. Subjective experience and confidence are
\par
therefore not ``experimentally interchangeable'' (Rajaram et al., 2002, p. 234), either holistically
\par
as judgment types, or when comparing individual categories such as Remember and High
\par
confidence. These patterns are considered in terms of different models of memory later in this
\par
discussion.
\par
Further dissociation of confidence and subjective experience is shown by the fact that the
\par
source encoding manipulation employed in Experiments 2 and 3 led to increased recognition
\par
performance and higher confidence in these experiments compared to Experiment 1, but
\par
patterns of subjective experience did not change. This finding runs contrary to previous
\par
manipulations which have shown that more elaborative or conceptual processing at encoding
\par
leads to more Remember responses. Such manipulations include deep versus shallow
\par
processing, generation or vocalization versus reading of items (Gardiner, 1988; Gregg \&
\par
Gardiner, 1991), orthographically distinctive versus orthographically common words (Rajaram,
\par
1998), repetition of items at study (Dewhurst \& Anderson, 1999), and intentional versus
\par
incidental learning (Macken \& Hampson, 1993). It is unclear why the source encoding
\par
manipulation employed in Experiments 2 and 3 led to increases in recognition performance and
\par
confidence but no increases in Remember responses. In interpreting this result, it is important to
\par
remember that this difference was observed across experiments, not within. But the fact that
\par
increases were shown in High confidence but not in Remember judgments supports the
\par
argument that the two processes are dissociable in some tasks and in response to some variables.
\par
To fully explore how subjective experience and confidence judgments respond to source
\par
encoding instruction manipulations, source encoding would need to be performed at study and
\par
both subjective experience and confidence judgments made at test. We are currently pursuingSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 65
\par
this line of enquiry. Though prior research has measured both subjective experience and
\par
confidence at test and included a source manipulation at encoding (e.g, Wixted \& Mickes,
\par
2010); to our knowledge, subjective experience and confidence judgments have not been
\par
operationalized in the same manner and been measured at the same time when a source
\par
manipulation has been included at encoding.
\par
\textbf{The separation of Know and Familiar}
\par
The current experiments were the first to employ the four categories of Remember,
\par
Know, Familiar, and Guess in simple word recognition. Previous research has only employed
\par
the separation of Know and Familiar with objectively more meaningful stimuli such as learning
\par
of rare word definitions, faces, and student course material (e.g., Conway et al., 1997; Dewhurst
\par
et al., 2009; Herbert \& Burt, 2001, 2003, 2004; Sauerland \& Sporer, 2009; Wright \& Sladden,
\par
2003). A key question for the current experiments was therefore: How would participants use
\par
the Know response in an episodic memory task with less meaningful material?
\par
In terms of similarities, the current experiments suggest that Know and Familiar
\par
experiences are similar in how often they are reported by participants. Experiments 1 and 2
\par
demonstrated very similar \emph{a priori }usage of Know and Familiar responses by participants;
\par
approximately 25\% of correct recognition responses were assigned each to Know and Familiar.
\par
However, this is where the similarity ends. Recognition decisions assigned to Know were
\par
demonstrated to be more accurate, more confident, faster, and have higher source accuracy than
\par
those assigned to Familiar. In fact, Know recognition did not differ from Remember recognition
\par
on all of the above factors except source accuracy. That there were no differences in accuracy
\par
or confidence between Remember and Know recognition responses when Know and Familiar
\par
are separated replicates the results of Dewhurst et al. (2009; and regarding accuracy SauerlandSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 66
\par
\& Sporer, 2009) using very different experimental stimuli. The current novel findings for
\par
source accuracy and RT add to these previous findings concerning differences between
\par
Remember, Know, and Familiar subjective experiences.
\par
These results firmly establish that participants are able to use separate Know and
\par
Familiar responses in episodic memory tasks. A subjective experience of `just knowing' that an
\par
item was presented previously without any associated recollective experience or feelings of
\par
familiarity was how a significant proportion of items were recognised in the current
\par
experiments. As suggested by Gardiner (2001) concerning the traditional Remember-Know
\par
paradigm, ``Other classifications of subjective conscious experiences of memory are
\par
undoubtedly possible and may even prove more useful, in the longer run, than this one'' (p.
\par
1360; see also McCabe et al., 2012). The separation of Know and Familiar is suggested to be
\par
one of these more useful classifications and one that may benefit future research in, for example,
\par
aging, neuropsychology, education, or identifying the neurological bases of memory retrieval.
\par
Indeed, as discussed previously, some researchers interested in the neural correlates of
\par
recollection and familiarity have already used modified versions of the Remember-Know
\par
categories. Montaldi et al. (2006) trained participants to distinguish between recollection and
\par
three levels of familiarity and showed that certain neural activation increased or decreased
\par
linearly as familiarity increased but hippocampal activity was not modulated by changes in
\par
familiarity: the hippocampus was only activated for items that were recollected (see also Diana,
\par
Yonelinas, \& Ranganath, 2010; Yonelinas et al., 2005). Use of the separated Know and
\par
Familiar categories -- or at least use of more than one category of non-recollection retrieval --
\par
may enable identification of separate patterns of neural activation for different subjective
\par
experiences, and thus perhaps aiding clarification of the opposing patterns of conclusions thatSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 67
\par
currently confuse the literature (cf. for example, Diana et al., 2010; Squire, Wixted, \& Clark,
\par
2007; Wais, 2008; Wais, Wixted, Hopkins, \& Squire 2006).
\par
\textbf{Source accuracy, subjective experience and confidence}
\par
Some previous studies have found source accuracy to only be at chance for Know items
\par
(Dewhurst \& Hitch, 1999; Dudukovic \& Knowlton, 2006; Perfect et al., 1996, except for
\par
Experiment 3) while other studies have shown source accuracy to be above chance for items
\par
assigned to Know (Conway \& Dewhurst, 1995b; Hicks et al., 2002; Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002;
\par
Meiser \& Sattler, 2007; Perfect et al., 1996 -- Experiment 3; Starns \& Hicks, 2005). In the
\par
current experiments source judgments were accurate over 80\% of the time overall, and
\par
Remember, Know, and Familiar recognition decisions were accompanied by above-chance
\par
source accuracy8
\par
. That Know responses were accompanied by accurate source supports the
\par
latter group of studies listed above. For a substantial proportion of items that were Known in
\par
Experiment 2, enough contextual details from the study phase were retrieved at recognition for
\par
source to be judged correctly but not enough details were recollected, or were experienced in the
\par
right way, for the item to be assigned to Remember. For participants this might mean that
\par
retrieval of source is more semantic in nature; for example, using the encoding manipulation in
\par
the current task a participant might have the thought ``I just know I associated \emph{`silence' }with
\par
George Clooney, so it must have been shown on the left, but I can't recall what association I
\par
made''. In this example, while source is accurate, subjective experience is Know rather than
\par
Remember. Importantly, source accuracy was the only factor that reliably differentiated
\par
Remember and Know responses. A similar dissociation was demonstrated by Wixted and
\par
Mickes (2010) who asked participants to make a Remember-Know judgment in conjunction
\par
with a 20-point confidence judgment and a judgment of source. They found that when only theSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 68
\par
highest confidence Remember and Know judgments were compared they were equally accurate
\par
in terms of recognition sensitivity; however they were accompanied by different levels of source
\par
accuracy.
\par
As regards the studies that previously found source accuracy for Know responses to only
\par
be at chance, it is suggested that methodological differences resulted in these findings. For
\par
example, Dudukovic and Knowlton (2006) only measured source in a re-test one week after
\par
initial testing. This delay led to a decrease in Remember responses and an increase in Know
\par
responses indicating an overall reduction in retrieval of contextual details. This forgetting of
\par
source information could have led to items being Known rather than Remembered and also
\par
explain why Know responses were not accompanied by accurate source judgments. Dewhurst
\par
and Hitch (1999) did not employ a delay but their participants only made a source judgment if
\par
they were confident about their recognition decision. They suggested that the low source
\par
accuracy for Know items may have been a result of the overall low response rate for source.
\par
For Perfect et al. (1996) it is the difficulty of the source judgment that is suggested to have led to
\par
at-chance source accuracy for Know items. Of their four experiments, the three that found
\par
chance levels of source accuracy for Know responses employed source manipulations that were
\par
much more complex (temporal order, undifferentiated lists, and sequential quadrant
\par
presentation) than that employed in their Experiment 3, which did find above-chance source
\par
accuracy for Know. Experiment 3 used simultaneous quadrant presentation which could have
\par
allowed participants to more easily create spatial associations between items thus aiding
\par
retrieval of location at test -- even if other elements of the association were not recollected and
\par
the experience of retrieval was one of Knowing rather than Remembering.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 69
\par
The current experiments were the first to separate Know and Familiar and record source
\par
judgments and thus source accuracy for Familiar responses had not been explored previously.
\par
The finding that Familiar responses were also accompanied by above-chance source accuracy
\par
suggests that feelings of familiarity at recognition can include retrieval of elements of the
\par
encoding episode that allow a correct source judgment to be made. As concluded by Hicks et al.
\par
(2002) it appears that judgments of source ``can be based on recollection but can also effectively
\par
use qualitative characteristics that lack clarity and sufficient amounts of details to give rise to
\par
the subjective feeling of remembering'' (p. 503). Utilising the four categories of Remember,
\par
Know, Familiar, and Guess, further research could examine whether Familiar (and/or Know)
\par
responses lose their accurate source when the source judgment is made more difficult by
\par
increasing the delay between study and test (Dudukovic \& Knowlton, 2006), requiring
\par
multidimensional source judgments ( Meiser \& Br\"oder, 2002), or eliminating the deep encoding
\par
instructions that were used in the present experiments. If overall source accuracy is reduced it
\par
might be expected that Familiar recognition would no longer have accurate source. For
\par
example, previous research has demonstrated that the specificity of the source judgment
\par
differentiates between whether Know responses have accurate source or not (in a traditional
\par
Remember-Know procedure). Meiser and Sattler (2007) found that specific source memory for
\par
the voice of an individual speaker was more accurate for Remember items than for Known
\par
items. However, partial source memory for the more global attribute of gender did not
\par
differentiate between Remember and Know items. Further work is required to explore the
\par
relationship between the difficulty and informativeness of source judgments, and their
\par
relationship with subjective experience.SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 70
\par
\textbf{Further relationships between source, subjective experience, and confidence}
\par
The relationship between source and confidence and subjective experience judgments
\par
was also able to be analysed in the opposite direction in the current experiments. Patterns of
\par
subjective experience and confidence responses split by the accuracy of the accompanying
\par
source judgment demonstrated further differences between how subjective experience and
\par
confidence judgments were used. For subjective experience responses, a clear relationship
\par
between source and subjective experience was demonstrated for both correct and incorrect
\par
source judgments. Correct retrieval of source was associated with recollection whilst incorrect
\par
retrieval of source was associated with familiarity. In contrast, patterns of confidence
\par
judgments were only reliable for correct source items. When source was correct, a greater
\par
proportion of items were assigned to High, compared to Medium, compared to Low confidence;
\par
but when source was incorrect, confidence judgments were evenly split across High, Medium,
\par
and Low confidence levels. While this is not incorrect of participants as confidence judgments
\par
were made for recognition of the item, not confidence in their source judgment, it shows that
\par
there was no relationship between recognition confidence and source when source was
\par
incorrect. Critically, it is suggested that judgments of subjective experience are more sensitive
\par
and more reflective of memory processes than judgments of confidence. In addition to
\par
judgments of confidence being more lenient than judgments of subjective experience,
\par
confidence did not vary systematically when items were recognised without retrieval of source.
\par
The current findings may best be interpreted in terms of recent conceptualisations of
\par
recollection as a continuous process (e.g., Mickes, Wais, \& Wixted, 2009; Onyper, Zhang, \&
\par
Howard, 2010; Parks \& Yonelinas, 2007; Rotello, Macmillan, \& Reeder, 2004; Slotnick, 2010;
\par
Slotnick \& Dodson, 2005; Wixted \& Mickes, 2010). With the separation of Know and FamiliarSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 71
\par
responses, the finding that Know and Remember responses differ only in terms of source
\par
accuracy while Remember, Know, and Familiar responses were all accompanied by retrieval of
\par
varying degrees of source accuracy is taken to support the idea that there are varying degrees of
\par
recollection. Crucially, it is not suggested that retrieval of source for Know and Familiar items
\par
was accompanied by recollective experience; instead the suggestion is that retrieval of source
\par
was experienced either as semantic knowledge (Know) or with feelings of familiarity (Familiar).
\par
Though recollection as a process might be continuous, it is suggested that the \emph{feeling }of
\par
Remembering is threshold. The phenomenological experience associated with retrieval that
\par
warrants a Remember response is what separates this Remember recognition from Know and
\par
Familiar recognition, even if that Know and Familiar recognition can include retrieval of enough
\par
contextual details to garner an accurate judgment of source. Evidence to support this viewpoint
\par
comes from examination of reaction time.
\par
\textbf{Reaction time for recognition}
\par
To our knowledge, the current experiments were the first to examine RTs using the four
\par
categories of Remember, Know, Familiar and Guess. Here, with the separation of Know and
\par
Familiar subjective experiences, recognition decisions for Know items were as fast as for
\par
Remember items, and both Remember and Know were faster than Familiar. These results are
\par
therefore interpreted as consistent with earlier studies that found Remember to be faster than
\par
Know, as in those studies Know and Familiar had not been separated (Dewhurst \& Conway,
\par
1994; Dewhurst et al., 1998, 2006; Henson et al., 1999; Stretch \& Wixted, 1998; Vilberg \&
\par
Rugg, 2007; Wixted \& Stretch, 2004). Though many studies have demonstrated RT differences
\par
for Remember and Know recognition decisions, many dual-process models predict that
\par
responses based on familiarity should be quicker than those based on recollection as familiaritySOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 72
\par
is considered to be rapid and automatic while recollection is thought to be slower and more
\par
effortful (e.g., Jacoby, 1991; Mandler, 1980; Yonelinas \& Jacoby, 1994, 1996). This
\par
inconsistency between the models' predictions and experimental findings led Yonelinas (2002)
\par
to suggest that Remember responses are faster than Know responses because instructions require
\par
participants to only respond Know if an item is `familiar and not recollected'. If Remember and
\par
Familiar subjective experiences are considered to directly map onto the processes of recollection
\par
and familiarity then Yonelinas's (2002) suggestion explains the current patterns -- Familiar
\par
recognition decisions are slower because instructions require the recollection process to be
\par
completed first. In addition, with the separation of Know and Familiar categories, Yonelinas's
\par
suggestion is also consistent with the present findings that Know and Remember decisions were
\par
equally fast. If Know is considered to be high confidence without recollection then whether the
\par
recollection process results in anything from encoding being recollected (Remember) or nothing
\par
being recollected (Know) this should take the same amount of time and recognition RTs for
\par
Remembering or Knowing should be equal, as demonstrated in the current experiments.
\par
However, Dewhurst et al. (2006) provided evidence against the suggestion that the speed
\par
advantage for Remember responses is an artefact of experimental instructions. In their
\par
experiment, faster recognition decisions for Remember items were demonstrated even when the
\par
judgment of subjective experience was decoupled from the recognition decision. Under these
\par
conditions, RK instructions could not influence the timing of recognition decisions and thus
\par
Dewhurst et al. concluded against the demand characteristics account proposed by Yonelinas
\par
(2002). Dewhurst et al. suggested that instead of directly reflecting the time-course of the
\par
\emph{processes} of recollection and familiarity, Remember and Know responses reflect the time taken
\par
to make recognition decisions based on \emph{the information which results from} recollection andSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 73
\par
familiarity processes. If a test item cues retrieval of contextual information from encoding such
\par
as thoughts, images, or associations, then recognition decisions can be made more quickly than
\par
if it requires additional processing to evaluate its familiarity relative to other items on the test
\par
(Conway \& Dewhurst, 1994; Dewhurst et al., 2006; Henson et al., 1999). For the present
\par
experiments, this explains the difference in speed of Remember and Familiar decisions but not
\par
the similarity in speed of Remember and Know decisions. Moreover, in Experiment 2 both
\par
Know and Familiar responses were accompanied by above-chance source accuracy
\par
demonstrating that some contextual information from encoding was retrieved for these items,
\par
even if this retrieval was not accompanied by a subjective feeling of Remembering.
\par
As Know and Remember were equally fast in terms of RT, and Remember, Know, and
\par
Familiar were all accompanied by above-chance source accuracy, it is suggested it is the
\par
\emph{subjective feeling} concerning recognition that is critically important in influencing the speed
\par
with which decisions are made. For Remember and Know responses, whether an item is
\par
retrieved with recollection of contextual information from encoding or high confidence without
\par
recollection (accompanied by `just known' contextual information or not) results in two
\par
different subjective experiences which are experienced equally rapidly and which result in fast
\par
recognition decisions. In contrast, when the subjective experience is one of Familiarity,
\par
additional processing regarding the source of this feeling of familiarity (and any accompanying
\par
contextual information retrieved) results in recognition decisions taking longer. The subjective
\par
feeling is related to the information in memory and in what manner context is retrieved, and
\par
assessment of all these elements of retrieval influences recognition decision RTs.
\par
For confidence judgments, patterns of recognition RTs categorised by later confidence
\par
judgment were similar to those for subjective experience judgments. Recognition decisions forSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 74
\par
High and Medium confidence items were faster than for Low and None. The general finding
\par
that the higher the confidence the faster the response replicates previous research (Henson et al.,
\par
2000; Mandler \& Boeck, 1974; Ratcliff \& Murdock, 1976). However, the similarity between
\par
the patterns for confidence and subjective experience RTs suggests that the categories within the
\par
two types of judgment are similar in terms of processing time while they were not the same in
\par
terms of distribution of responses or recognition accuracy.
\par
For source judgments, Experiments 2 and 3 did not find a consistent speed advantage for
\par
accurate source items but re-analysis of data combined across both experiments demonstrated
\par
that recognition was faster for items when the subsequent source judgment was correct. The
\par
differing findings across experiments mirrors previous research. Some studies found a
\par
difference between RT for incorrect and correct source items (e.g., Kahn et al., 2004; Lundstrom
\par
et al., 2005) and other studies did not (e.g., Wilding \& Rugg, 1996). From the combined
\par
analysis in the present experiments, it is tentatively concluded that RTs are faster for correct
\par
recognition decisions where source judgments are ultimately correct. The retrieval of source
\par
information at recognition speeds the recognition decision.
\par
Regarding interpretations of the present RT findings in terms of single- and dual-process
\par
accounts, Rotello and Zeng (2008) argued against such RT differences being support for dual-
\par
process theories by demonstrating that when Remember and Know responses were equated for
\par
confidence the RT advantage for Remember responses was reduced. In contrast to their
\par
findings, the current experiments showed that when Remember and Know responses are equated
\par
for accuracy (Experiment 2) or equated for both accuracy and confidence (Experiment 1), both
\par
Remember and Know responses are made more quickly than Familiar responses, which had
\par
lower levels of accuracy and confidence. These patterns do not support the conclusion thatSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 75
\par
subjective experience judgments are based on a single underlying memory processes and instead
\par
demonstrates that splitting Know into separate Know and Familiar categories can reveal
\par
important patterns of data otherwise obscured.
\par
\textbf{Reaction time to make judgments}
\par
These experiments were the first time that reaction times to make post-recognition
\par
judgments of confidence, source, and subjective experience have been compared. For
\par
confidence and subjective experience, mean judgment RTs in Experiments 2 and 3 paralleled
\par
those obtained in Experiment 1 and between-subjects comparisons demonstrated no significant
\par
differences in RTs across experiments, all \emph{t }{$<$} 1. The time it took participants to make
\par
judgments of subjective experience and confidence was equivalent across experiments.
\par
Comparisons across judgment types demonstrated that source judgments were quicker
\par
than confidence, which in turn were quicker than subjective experience. This result was not
\par
predicted. As both source and subjective experience judgments require assessment of what
\par
information can be retrieved regarding context at encoding it was predicted that both source and
\par
subjective experience judgments would be slower than judgments of confidence. One
\par
suggestion for the observed pattern is that judgments of subjective experience and confidence
\par
are both subjective types of assessment whereas source is more objective. Thus, confidence and
\par
subjective experience judgments may require greater metacognitive processing because of their
\par
non-objective nature. An alternative suggestion is that source judgments may have been so
\par
quick because only a one-dimensional source judgment was used and encoding proficiency had
\par
been improved due to the instructions provided (after Yonelinas, 1999). These manipulations
\par
led to source judgments being accurate over 80\% of the time and may have also influenced RTs.
\par
Future experiments could examine whether source judgments are slower when the sourceSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 76
\par
judgment is more difficult, or when the delay between encoding and retrieval is longer, as this
\par
might increase forgetting of source information. Previous research has shown that coarse source
\par
discriminations are faster than finer ones (Lindsay, 2008). For example, Johnson, Kounios, and
\par
Reeder (1994) analysed the time-course of item recognition and source memory and found that
\par
item recognition became accurate more quickly than source memory. However, multi-
\par
dimensional source experiments have not yet investigated the time course of source judgments.
\par
If this was explored in future research it might be expected that, in line with the findings of
\par
Meiser and Sattler (2007), RTs might differ for source judgments which require retrieval of
\par
specific (e.g., individual voice) and global (e.g., gender of voice) information.
\par
In the present experiments, in addition to recognition judgments being faster when a
\par
subsequent judgment of source was correct, source judgments themselves were also faster when
\par
source was correct. This finding is novel, though unsurprising. It also adds to the findings of
\par
Kahn et al. (2004) and Lundstrom et al. (2005) who used one-step methods where recognition
\par
and source were judged at the same time. The current findings show that when separate source
\par
and recognition judgments are made, both these types of judgment are faster when source is
\par
accurate. Critically, using the two-step method in the current experiments, the speed advantage
\par
for correct source judgments demonstrated a larger and more reliable difference than the speed
\par
advantage observed for recognition decisions later shown to have correct source. Moreover,
\par
further analysis revealed that RTs for source judgments were faster when accompanied by
\par
Remember or High confidence. Thus, the subjective experience or confidence associated with
\par
recognition of an item was related to how quickly a source judgment was made to that item.
\par
This novel finding is particularly interesting in terms of the relationship between source
\par
and subjective experience as for subjective experience judgments themselves no reliableSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 77
\par
relationship was demonstrated between category of subjective experience and speed of
\par
judgment. The subjective experience associated with recognition of an item did not influence
\par
RTs to make subjective experience judgments yet it did influence RTs for source judgments.
\par
This is surprising as source judgments were made significantly faster than subjective experience
\par
judgments. Though source judgments were made more quickly, within that quick response
\par
there was significant variation and this was associated with the subjective experience with
\par
which the item was recognised. It is suggested that this occurred because a judgment of source
\par
is a more objective one-dimensional type of judgment compared to a subjective experience
\par
judgment -- at least in these experiments. Thus, if the critical source information was recollected
\par
from encoding and this was experienced subjectively as Remembering then this led to speeding
\par
of the source judgment. This interplay between source and subjective experience fits with the
\par
suggestion made earlier that recognition decision RTs themselves are based on the relationship
\par
between the \emph{subjective feeling} of recognition and the manner in which contextual information
\par
accompanying them is retrieved.
\par
In contrast to the finding that source judgments were speeded by accompanying
\par
recollection, Remember responses themselves were not made significantly more quickly than
\par
other subjective experience responses. One explanation for this could be that all processing of
\par
subjective experience has already taken place at recognition and judgment RT simply reflects
\par
time taken to make a response. However, as subjective experience judgments took longer to
\par
make than recognition decisions (1794ms compared to 1560ms respectively9), this suggests that
\par
additional processing was undertaken at the time of making the judgment. Participants could
\par
have been checking that they did not in fact retrieve any contextual information; or checking the
\par
content of the contextual information they had retrieved -- was it accurate? MetacognitiveSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 78
\par
processing such as this would result in the patterns observed; as soon as processing ends a
\par
response is made, and RT for this response is not dependent on the outcome of the processing.
\par
This suggestion fits with the pattern of results obtained for confidence judgments where
\par
judgments were made more quickly as confidence increased. For these more inferential, less
\par
subjective judgments, it is suggested that the type of processing required is easier than that for
\par
subjective experience judgments. Although the subjective experience with which an item is
\par
recognised is suggested to be what gives rise to feelings of confidence (Gardiner, 2002; Tulving,
\par
1985), once subjective experience has been triggered by presentation of an item at test, the lack
\par
of further processing required post-recognition results in quicker judgments when confidence is
\par
higher.
\par
\textbf{Conclusions}
\par
The experiments presented here used novel experimental designs and measures to
\par
explore the differences, similarities, and reaction times of confidence, source, and subjective
\par
experience judgments. Results demonstrate that making multiple judgments does not influence
\par
those judgments; however participants use the three types of judgment in different ways --
\par
supporting the argument that there are differences between confidence and recollection.
\par
The current experiments have shown confidence and subjective experience judgments to
\par
dissociate in many different ways. Based on these dissociations, confidence is interpreted as an
\par
inferential assessment of subjective experiences whereas subjective experience judgments
\par
themselves are considered to be more reflective of underlying memory processes and
\par
experiential states. The current findings are therefore interpreted as supporting dual-process as
\par
opposed to single-process accounts of recognition. In agreement with Dewhurst et al. (2006),
\par
subjective experience responses in the current experiments are not considered to be directSOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 79
\par
reflections of the underlying processes of recollection and familiarity, rather they are considered
\par
to be assessments of the information provided by these processes \emph{plus }the experiential state
\par
which results. Although the present findings may be able to be interpreted using unidimensional
\par
SDT models, that Remember and Know responses were differentiated only by source accuracy
\par
while Know and Familiar responses were dissociated by many factors adds to evidence from
\par
other behavioural and brain imaging studies that recognition decisions associated with different
\par
types of subjective experience judgments differ in ways other than just confidence or memory
\par
strength (e.g., Dewhurst et al., 2006; Diana \& Ranganath, 2011).SOURCE, CONFIDENCE, AND SUBJECTIVE EXPERIENCE 80
