@article{wongLeveragingEnvironmentalInformation2013,
  title = {Leveraging Environmental Information Integration to Enable Environmental Management Capability and Performance},
  author = {Wong, C.W.Y.},
  year = 2013,
  journal = {Journal of Supply Chain Management},
  volume = {49},
  number = {2},
  pages = {114--136},
  doi = {10.1111/jscm.12005},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Export Date: 05 May 2024; Cited By: 111
\par
LEVERAGING ENVIRONMENTAL INFORMATION
\par
INTEGRATION TO ENABLE ENVIRONMENTAL
\par
MANAGEMENT CAPABILITY AND PERFORMANCE
\par
CHRISTINA W. Y. WONG
\par
The Hong Kong Polytechnic University
\par
Although environmental sustainability has emerged as an important orga-
\par
nizational capability to protect the environment and sustain businesses,
\par
there is little knowledge on how it is developed. This is of particular
\par
importance when environmental management no longer relies solely on
\par
an individual firm's efforts, but on its supply chain partners as well.
\par
Building on dynamic capabilities theory, environmental information inte-
\par
gration (EII) is defined as the organizational capacity of sharing informa-
\par
tion on environmental management with supply chain partners to
\par
facilitate coordination of environmental management practices. This study
\par
examines how EII contributes to environmental management capabilities
\par
in terms of corporate environmental innovativeness and adaptability. The
\par
research model is empirically tested using data collected from 230 firms.
\par
The findings show that supplier EII is insufficient in improving environ-
\par
mental management capabilities. Internal EII contributes to corporate
\par
environmental adaptability, while customer EII engenders both corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness and adaptability. This study suggests that
\par
strategic values of EII go beyond the sharing of environmental manage-
\par
ment information between supply chain partners, simultaneously contrib-
\par
uting to the environmental management capabilities of firms. This study
\par
contributes substantially to environmental management research by pro-
\par
viding empirical evidence on the specific dimensions of EII in supply
\par
chains that contribute to environmental management capabilities and
\par
business values.
\par
Keywords: environmental sustainability; environmental management; sustainable
\par
supply chain management; information integration; dynamic capabilities; financial
\par
performance; environmental performance
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Environmental protection is an important and
\par
timely organizational issue that affects long-term
\par
development of businesses (Madsen, 2009). While
\par
firms are actively engaging in practices, ranging from
\par
cleaner production (Rothenberg, Pil \& Maxwell, 2001;
\par
Yang, Hong \& Modi, 2011) to eco-product design
\par
(Toffel, 2004), there is growing attention to the signif-
\par
icance of supply chain collaboration in environmental
\par
Acknowledgments: The author is grateful to the Editor-in-Chief, the
\par
Associate Editor, and the four anonymous reviewers for their helpful
\par
comments on earlier versions of this manuscript. This research is
\par
partially supported by the Research Grants Council of Hong Kong
\par
Special Administrative Region, China (GRF PolyU 5500/10H).
\par
protection, such as closed-loop supply chains and
\par
reverse logistics (Parmigiani, Klassen \& Russo, 2011).
\par
As a result, many industry leaders, such as Nike,
\par
Hewlett-Packard and S.C. Johnson, share information
\par
with their supply chain partners in support of their
\par
efforts to eliminate the use of toxic materials, reduce
\par
energy consumption and avoid waste production
\par
throughout their supply chain processes. However, the
\par
Carbon Disclosure Project Supply Chain Report
\par
(2011) and Green Chemistry \& Commerce Council
\par
(2009) suggest that coordination of environmental
\par
management practices in a supply chain is fraught
\par
with challenges. Such challenges are induced by the
\par
complexity of supply chain communication and coor-
\par
dination, suggesting the importance of developing an
\par
114
\par
Volume 49, Number 2Environmental Information Integration
\par
information sharing mechanism to support environ-
\par
mental management beyond an individual firm. For
\par
example, PepsiCo shares information and integrates
\par
with its suppliers to successfully reduce their green-
\par
house gas emissions in the supply chain as a whole
\par
(ATKearney, 2011). Drawing in part on extant find-
\par
ings from both the information systems and supply
\par
chain management disciplines (Flynn, Huo \& Zhao,
\par
2010; Lee, 2000; Wong, Boon-itt \& Wong, 2011c;
\par
Wong, Lai \& Cheng, 2011a), this study explores the
\par
effects of environmental information integration
\par
established internally across business units and exter-
\par
nally with suppliers and customers on the environ-
\par
mental management capabilities of firms.
\par
Environmental information integration (EII) is
\par
defined as the information sharing infrastructure that
\par
supports environmental information exchange and
\par
coordination across business functions and partner
\par
firms (Almotairi \& Lumsden, 2009; Bajwa et al., 2008;
\par
Grover \& Saeed, 2007; Lai, Wong, Cheng \& Yeung,
\par
2006). While it differs from the traditional supply
\par
chain information integration that is confined to sup-
\par
porting such activities as shipment coordination, order
\par
fulfilment and so forth (Lai, Wong \& Cheng, 2010; Lai
\par
et al., 2006; Lau, Hui, Chan \& Wong, 2002; Wong, Lai
\par
\& Cheng, 2009a; Wong, Lai \& Ngai, 2009b), it also
\par
differs from vertical integration that is concerned with
\par
corporate ownership. EII reflects organizational elec-
\par
tronic connectivity with supply chain partners that
\par
enables firms to acquire and disseminate information
\par
to coordinate environmental management practices,
\par
ranging from eco-product design, asset recovery, com-
\par
ponents disassembly and recycling, to reuse, with the
\par
aim of mitigating the environmental impact of prod-
\par
ucts throughout their life cycle (Elliot, 2011). For
\par
example, by sharing information on product design
\par
and material composite with downstream customers
\par
(e.g., distributors and retailers), firms enable their cus-
\par
tomers' participation in such asset recovery tasks as
\par
returned product inspection and separation. On the
\par
other hand, information related to the condition and
\par
amount of retrieved components and materials enable
\par
upstream partners (e.g., component suppliers and
\par
manufacturers) to identify opportunities for product
\par
and process improvement, and plan for inventory.
\par
However, there is limited empirical evidence on
\par
how EII may contribute to the environmental man-
\par
agement capabilities of firms. This omission in the lit-
\par
erature is undesirable because there are few
\par
managerial insights into how firms may develop their
\par
environmental management capabilities through lever-
\par
aging their supply chain efforts in environmental pro-
\par
tection. The concept of environmental management
\par
capabilities is concerned with conserving natural capi-
\par
tal, whereby firms reduce their environmental impact
\par
by such means as reducing waste in operations, using
\par
renewable inputs, and continuously improving their
\par
operations to sustain yield with minimum adverse
\par
impact to the environment (Goodland, 1995; Klassen
\par
\& Whybark, 1999). It refers to the ability of firms to
\par
integrate environmental issues into business opera-
\par
tions (Lee \& Klassen, 2008) and is related to the orga-
\par
nizational characteristics and management system that
\par
facilitates and supports a firm's environmental protec-
\par
tion efforts and initiatives. Although there is little
\par
empirical evidence of its performance impact and
\par
knowledge on how it is developed, a few prior studies
\par
have provided anecdotal evidence and discussion on
\par
the potential performance impact of environmental
\par
management capabilities in terms of corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness and adaptability in
\par
mitigating environmental damage, preempting future
\par
environmental requirements and satisfying customer
\par
needs (Handfield, Sroufe \& Walton, 2005). The objec-
\par
tive of this study is to answer two critical research
\par
questions related to the development of environmen-
\par
tal management capabilities and its performance
\par
impact: (1) How does EII influence environmental
\par
management capabilities in terms of corporate envi-
\par
ronmental innovativeness and adaptability? and (2)
\par
What are the implications of developing environmen-
\par
tal management capabilities for financial and environ-
\par
mental performance? To answer these research
\par
questions, we draw on dynamic capabilities (DC) the-
\par
ory to examine the environmental management capa-
\par
bilities of firms in innovating new environmental
\par
practices and adapting to new market environmental
\par
demands and expectations, while EII facilitates envi-
\par
ronmental management capabilities by providing the
\par
relevant information.
\par
LITERATURE REVIEW
\par
Environmental Management Capabilities as
\par
Dynamic Capabilities
\par
Noting the limit of natural resources that requires
\par
economic and environmental goals to be pursued
\par
within these limits (Farrell \& Hart, 1998), firms
\par
require proactive problem solving and innovation to
\par
improve traditional, inefficient business processes and
\par
products (Ramus, 2001). Firms also need to be adap-
\par
tive to changing environmental requirements (e.g.,
\par
new environmental regulations) (Elliot, 2011; Milne,
\par
Kearins \& Walton, 2006). In line with organizational
\par
dynamic capabilities (DC) theory, which advocates
\par
the ability of firms to continually build and develop
\par
organizational practices to sustain business growth
\par
(Teece, Pisano \& Shuen, 1997), environmental man-
\par
agement capabilities is characterized by the capabili-
\par
ties of firms to innovate and adapt to changing
\par
market needs (Beske, 2012; Elliot, 2011). It extends
\par
beyond corporate strategic goals (Farrell \& Hart, 1998;
\par
April 2013
\par
1745493x, 2013, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jscm.12005 by Universit\"ats- Und, Wiley Online Library on [15/08/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License115Journal of Supply Chain Management
\par
Parris \& Kates, 2003), environmental protection initia-
\par
tives (Jacobs, Singhal \& Subramanian, 2010) and
\par
environmental management practices (Matos \& Hall,
\par
2007).
\par
Dynamic capabilities theory suggests that firms that
\par
are responsive and rapid in product innovation, cou-
\par
pled with the capabilities to effectively coordinate and
\par
redeploy internal and external competencies, would
\par
improve performance (Teece et al., 1997). It considers
\par
the capacity of firms to integrate, build and renew
\par
internal and external competencies to adapt to chang-
\par
ing market needs (Sirmon, Hitt \& Ireland, 2007). DC
\par
theory, in line with the corporate social responsibility
\par
literature, provides a theoretical grounding for the
\par
conceptualization of environmental management
\par
capabilities, which is a key component of the environ-
\par
mental sustainability of firms in attending and
\par
responding to changing market and societal needs
\par
(Ackerman \& Bauer, 1976). Environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities reflect firms' ability to develop inno-
\par
vative practices while being adaptive to market needs
\par
(Klassen \& Whybark, 1999; Quak \& de Koster, 2007),
\par
to address the growing concern of environmental
\par
expectations while improving environmental and
\par
financial performance (Kleindorfer, Singhal \& van
\par
Wassenhove, 2005).
\par
Based on DC theory, corporate environmental innova-
\par
tiveness is concerned with the development and adop-
\par
tion of new environmental management practices to
\par
improve the efficiency and effectiveness of firms' envi-
\par
ronmental protection efforts (Fong \& Chang, 2012;
\par
Geffen \& Rothenberg, 2000; Theyel, 2000). It is
\par
related to the ability of firms to make investments
\par
and seize opportunities in developing new environ-
\par
mentally friendly products or adopting new pollution
\par
prevention practices and technologies. Corporate envi-
\par
ronmental innovativeness requires supply chain efforts
\par
to improve end-to-end processes between a focal firm
\par
and its suppliers and customers (Lee, 2000) and to
\par
improve resource visibility and information sharing to
\par
facilitate innovation (Geffen \& Rothenberg, 2000).
\par
On the other hand, corporate environmental adaptability
\par
is concerned with firms' responsiveness and flexibility
\par
in responding to new environmental requirements
\par
and needs while sustaining economic growth (Milne
\par
et al., 2006). It reflects the ability of firms to support
\par
the restructuring and reengineering of business pro-
\par
cesses to comply with new environmental principles
\par
and market demands (e.g., use of alternative energy).
\par
In the context of supply chain management, adapt-
\par
ability is considered a critical capability of firms in
\par
coping with the changing market needs (Lee, 2004).
\par
Thus, corporate environmental adaptability is an
\par
important environmental management capability to
\par
flexibly address future changes in environmental regu-
\par
lations and market expectations (Colby, 1991).
\par
According to DC theory, these characteristics of envi-
\par
ronmental management capabilities are crucial for
\par
firms to continually integrate new resources (e.g.,
\par
environmental technologies, practices or information)
\par
to the existing ones to innovate, while adapting to
\par
changing or evolving market needs. The triple bottom
\par
line literature suggests that environmental sustainabil-
\par
ity is related to a balance of economic, environmental
\par
and social goals (Carter \& Rogers, 2008). To achieve
\par
these goals in a supply chain, according to the
\par
resource-based view of a firm, firms need enabling
\par
processes to acquire the relevant information and
\par
facilitate the development of environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities (Barney, 2012; Priem \& Swink,
\par
2012). In line with this view, DC theory presumes the
\par
existence of two critical aspects: (1) the input of new
\par
resources that can be applied to modify existing orga-
\par
nizational practices and (2) the capabilities of chang-
\par
ing existing organizational practices (Winter, 2000).
\par
The development of such capabilities requires acquisi-
\par
tions of new information to understand market needs
\par
(Dillman, 2000) and collective efforts in managing
\par
cross-functional business activities to address the mar-
\par
ket needs (Fawcett, Wallin, Allred, Fawcett \& Magnan,
\par
2011). As such, information is considered an impor-
\par
tant resource input that enables firms to build and
\par
make necessary adjustments to their existing environ-
\par
mental knowledge and practices (Ravichandran \&
\par
Lertwongsatien, 2005). EII serves as a strategically sig-
\par
nificant mechanism to facilitate information sharing
\par
amongst functions and supply chain partners (Frohlich
\par
\& Westbrook, 2001). The environmental management
\par
capabilities of firms is considered as the second critical
\par
aspect of DC, which reflects the ability of firms to
\par
modify and change their existing practices to improve
\par
their environmental and economic performance.
\par
Environmental Information Integration
\par
The conceptualization of EII revolves around the
\par
fundamental concept of the intra- and inter-organiza-
\par
tional systems infrastructure to facilitate timely, accu-
\par
rate and standardized data exchange across
\par
organization functions (Truman, 2000). In line with
\par
the notion of supply chain integration, EII provides
\par
the electronic linkages of internal and external func-
\par
tions to share environmental information and coordi-
\par
nate environmental management practices (e.g.,
\par
return product collection, disassembly, recycling). It is
\par
concerned with managing the environmental impacts
\par
of business activities ranging from product develop-
\par
ment, production, delivery and consumption, to post-
\par
disposal disposition of products (Linton, Klassen \&
\par
Jayaraman, 2007; Pagell \& Wu, 2009). As such, EII
\par
enables the coordination of environmental protection
\par
responsibilities and tasks among supply chain
\par
partners.
\par
116
\par
Volume 49, Number 2
\par
1745493x, 2013, 2, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/jscm.12005 by Universit\"ats- Und, Wiley Online Library on [15/08/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons LicenseEnvironmental Information Integration
\par
From the technical perspective, EII is concerned with
\par
the electronic interconnectedness and linkages devel-
\par
oped between partner firms to share logically compati-
\par
ble data (Bernstein \& Haas, 2008). EII supports
\par
communication and sharing of such environmental
\par
management information as solid waste generation
\par
and carbon emission among the associated partners.
\par
It reflects the extent to which partner firms and func-
\par
tions are interconnected to allow timely and accurate
\par
environmental information sharing across organiza-
\par
tional functions (Goodhue, Wybo \& Kirsch, 1992).
\par
From the coordination perspective, EII is concerned
\par
with inter- and intra-organizational environmental
\par
management practices, for example, the collection of
\par
returned products and separation of reusable parts,
\par
being streamlined and coupled by information sys-
\par
tems (Truman, 2000; Wong et al., 2009a,b). It enables
\par
coordination of inter-dependency and allocation of
\par
responsibility, ranging from end-of-life product collec-
\par
tion and disassembly, to material recycling amongst
\par
partners to achieve environmental goals (e.g., reduce
\par
disposal to landfill) at a low cost (Rao \& Holt, 2005).
\par
In line with DC theory, these perspectives highlight
\par
the importance of EII in enabling supply chain part-
\par
ners to collaborate in environmental management
\par
through information sharing to plan and improve
\par
decision making, with the result of having better coor-
\par
dination and organization in their environmental
\par
management practices.
\par
Dimensions of Environmental Information
\par
Integration
\par
A firm is an open social and economic system,
\par
where it is influenced by external forces and condi-
\par
tioned by internal processes (Astley \& Van de Ven,
\par
1983). Such a classical view of organization advocates
\par
the need to distinguish between internal and external
\par
EII and is in line with the conceptualization of supply
\par
chain integration, namely internal, supplier and cus-
\par
tomer integration (Flynn et al., 2010; Frohlich \&
\par
Westbrook, 2001; Wong et al., 2011c). Supply chain
\par
integration is concerned with the extent to which (i.e.
\par
the degree of integration) a focal firm collaborates
\par
with supply chain partners and facilitates data flow
\par
across its intra- and inter-organizational processes.
\par
Adopting this line of thought, EII is divided into three
\par
dimensions: namely internal EII, customer EII and
\par
supplier EII.
\par
Internal EII supports the coordination of internal
\par
processes to achieve corporate environmental goals. It
\par
is concerned with the extent to which a firm config-
\par
ures its communication and information sharing
\par
infrastructure across intra-organizational functions to
\par
facilitate organizational efforts in environmental man-
\par
agement (Lai, Wong \& Cheng, 2008). On the other
\par
hand, external EII includes supplier and customer EII
\par
to take into account the differences between upstream
\par
and downstream supply chains. Customer and sup-
\par
plier EII refer to the extent to which electronic link-
\par
ages are developed across inter-organizational
\par
processes, which are structured to facilitate the perfor-
\par
mance of environmental management, for which each
\par
partner is responsible.
\par
Customer EII is concerned with the information
\par
sharing infrastructure of firms sharing environmental
\par
information with customers (e.g., new eco-product
\par
introduction), while acquiring information of new
\par
customer requirements and expectations (e.g., new
\par
environmental standards and regulations). Such EII
\par
helps identify business and environmental manage-
\par
ment opportunities (Priem \& Swink, 2012), while
\par
informing and educating customers about alternative
\par
products and practices. Customer EII can therefore be
\par
useful in engaging customers in participating in
\par
environmental protection (e.g., returning end-of-life
\par
products).
\par
On the other hand, supplier EII is concerned with
\par
the information sharing infrastructure that enables
\par
information sharing between a focal firm and its sup-
\par
pliers to facilitate upstream supply chain operations
\par
on such aspects as materials selection and treatment
\par
of manufacturing by-products. Supplier EII enables
\par
product and process improvement by sharing infor-
\par
mation related to their material wastage, new alterna-
\par
tive materials and manufacturing technologies, and so
\par
forth. It enables supply chain partners to align their
\par
environmental objectives while collaboratively engag-
\par
ing in environmental practices to reduce their adverse
\par
environmental impact.
\par
Synthesis of the Extant Literature
\par
Upon conducting an extensive literature review, we
\par
summarized the seminal works on environmental
\par
management capabilities in Table 1 and observed the
\par
following limitations in the literature. First, the defini-
\par
tion and conceptualization of environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities varies across studies. While some
\par
studies take organizational environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities as the accountability and control of
\par
natural resources consumption (Elliot, 2011; Linton
\par
et al., 2007; Parmigiani et al., 2011), some consider
\par
environmental management capabilities as business
\par
practices (Montabon, Sroufe \& Narasimhan, 2007;
\par
Wu \& Pagell, 2011). A few studies have provided
\par
empirical evidence that is confined to specific prac-
\par
tices, such as announcing corporate environmental
\par
initiatives (Jacobs et al., 2010) and incorporating life-
\par
cycle assessment into product design (Matos \& Hall,
\par
2007). This study responds to the call for empirically
\par
investigating environmental management capabilities
\par
as an organizational ability to achieve sustained com-
\par
petitive advantage (Hart, 1995).
