\par
Second, although prior studies acknowledge the
\par
importance of supply chain efforts in environmental
\par
protection (e.g., Handfield et al., 2005; Matos \& Hall,
\par
2007; Quak \& de Koster, 2007), they focus on the
\par
environmental practices at various supply chain stages
\par
(e.g., product design and procurement stage) and their
\par
performance impact, neglecting how the supply chain
\par
collective efforts are developed. While information
\par
integration facilitates coordination of cross-firm activi-
\par
ties and enables joint efforts in environmental protec-
\par
tion, this study is novel in advancing knowledge on
\par
how firms may develop their environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities by forming EII across internal func-
\par
tions and with supply chain partners (Kleindorfer
\par
et al., 2005; Wong, Lai, Cheng \& Lun, 2011b). Such
\par
an approach is in line with the process-based perspec-
\par
tive of corporate social responsibility that suggests that
\par
environmental issues are related to organizational
\par
capacity in meeting market and societal needs (Acker-
\par
man \& Bauer, 1976). To cope with such needs, firms
\par
require enabling processes, such as EII, that provide
\par
environmental information and a coordination mech-
\par
anism (Wong et al., 2011a).
\par
Third, prior studies have examined the use of such
\par
IS applications as Environmental ERP software, GaBi
\par
and SimaPro to measure, manage and report environ-
\par
mental data (Denzer, Shimak \& Russell, 1995; Gun-
\par
ther, 1997). While these systems are largely confined
\par
to applications of individual organizations, little is
\par
known about how the inter-organizational environ-
\par
mental management efforts on such activities as green
\par
procurement (Carter \& Carter, 1998), take-back activi-
\par
ties (Atasu, Van Wassenhove \& Sarvary, 2009; Lai \&
\par
Wong, 2012; Lai, Wong \& Cheng, 2012; Lun, Lai,
\par
Wong, Ng \& Cheng, 2011) and remanufacturing
\par
(Webster \& Mitra, 2007) are coordinated. This omis-
\par
sion in the literature is undesirable for the theoretical
\par
and managerial development of environmental man-
\par
agement, as managers lack the knowledge on how to
\par
develop environmental management capabilities by
\par
leveraging their supply chain environmental manage-
\par
ment efforts. In particular, Vachon and Klassen
\par
(2007) found that the investments made in pollution
\par
control and prevention technologies tend to increase
\par
as a focal firm integrates more with its suppliers and
\par
customers. It is therefore important to reveal the
\par
dimensions of EII that contribute to environmental
\par
management capabilities and the performance impacts
\par
of environmental management capabilities.
\par
HYPOTHESES
\par
Effects of EII on Environmental Management
\par
Capabilities
\par
Internal EII facilitates communication and informa-
\par
tion sharing and promotes cross-functional coopera-
\par
tion (Mukhopadhyay, Kekre \& Kalathur, 1995). It
\par
facilitates new eco-product development by integrating
\par
cross-functional efforts in sourcing, product design,
\par
production, disassembly and so forth, to ensure the
\par
product has little adverse environmental impact
\par
throughout its life cycle. Internal EII lowers the silos
\par
across functions and enables access of information
\par
and knowledge that is critical to developing new
\par
products and environmental management practices
\par
that are difficult to complete under the effort of a
\par
function in isolation. According to DC theory, such
\par
EII fuels innovative activities by providing additional
\par
information and knowledge to generate new ideas
\par
and facilitating firms in their development and adop-
\par
tion of new environmental management practices and
\par
technologies. While facilitating internal process coor-
\par
dination, internal EII improves flexibility of business
\par
functions in responding to changing market demands,
\par
expectations and regulatory requirements as it facilities
\par
cross-functional adjustments and changes in practice.
\par
H1: Internal EII is positively associated with envi-
\par
ronmental management capabilities in terms
\par
of (a) corporate environmental innovativeness
\par
and (b) corporate environmental adaptability.
\par
By facilitating information sharing with suppliers,
\par
supplier EII encourages supplier participation in the
\par
stages of product design, production and asset recov-
\par
ery, with the objective of minimizing product life-cycle
\par
costs to the environment (Hart, 1995; Wong, Lai, Lun
\par
\& Cheng, 2012). Supplier EII provides firms with infor-
\par
mation and knowledge related to alternative choices of
\par
materials, designs for material waste reduction and
\par
reuse of scrap and retrieved components to reduce
\par
material consumption and disposal to landfill (Carter
\par
\& Carter, 1998; Pagell, Wu \& Wasserman, 2010). In
\par
addition, supplier EII supports the sharing of environ-
\par
mental performance, such as the amount of emission,
\par
waste water drainage and solid waste disposal in opera-
\par
tions, enabling firms to identify areas of improvement
\par
in a supply chain. For example, in addition to innovat-
\par
ing technologies that increase fuel efficiency when
\par
vehicles are in use, Honda Motor Co. tracks emission
\par
and environmental impacts of its suppliers' production
\par
of components and vehicle assembly to monitor and
\par
control their environmental impacts throughout its
\par
supply chain (Anonymous, 2012; Bardelline, 2011).
\par
Another example is the motorcycle manufacturing
\par
industry in Japan that establishes a voluntary recycling
\par
system by utilizing their existing information systems
\par
networks to facilitate such processes as product collec-
\par
tion, disassembly, component inspection, recycling
\par
and so forth among partners in a motorcycle supply
\par
chain (Staff, 2004). Based on DC theory, supplier EII
\par
provides sources of information to facilitate innovation
\par
of new eco-products and environmental management
