\par
As customer EII supports information sharing and
\par
communication with customers, it provides market
\par
intelligence and new information that enable firms to
\par
identify such opportunities as new eco-product devel-
\par
opment and environmental technology adoption.
\par
Customer EII provides information that improves the
\par
understanding of market needs, which in turn reduces
\par
uncertainties in eco-product design, asset recovery
\par
planning and reverse logistics scheduling. Such infor-
\par
mation integration enhances organizational adaptabil-
\par
ity to market needs by providing updated market
\par
information (Ettle \& Reza, 1992), thus improving
\par
firms' responsiveness to new market demands and
\par
unanticipated changes in environmental requirements
\par
(Flynn et al., 2010). It also helps preempt the loss of
\par
investment in new product and process development
\par
due to failure in meeting market needs. Equally, cus-
\par
tomer EII assists customers in identifying environmen-
\par
tally friendly alternatives, while providing them with
\par
information to engage and participate in environmen-
\par
tal protection activities. For example, DuPont, a major
\par
supplier of chemicals and materials, integrates with its
\par
customers and is involved in the early stages of product
\par
development, allowing it to deliver solutions that help
\par
its customers satisfy market needs (Guide \& Li, 2010).
\par
Such involvement also enables DuPont to learn about
\par
new market needs, which are beneficial to its new
\par
product development and innovation. This mutual
\par
exchange of information about market requirements
\par
and environmental best practices helps develop a col-
\par
laborative nature of value creation with customers,
\par
helping firms to identify market opportunities while
\par
meeting the market needs to sustain businesses.
\par
H3: Customer EII is positively associated with
\par
environmental management capabilities in
\par
terms of (a) corporate environmental innova-
\par
tiveness and (b) corporate environmental
\par
adaptability.
\par
Environmental Management Capabilities and
\par
Performance
\par
Environmental management capabilities often corre-
\par
spond to the concept of the triple bottom line, which
\par
is concerned with the balance of the well-being of
\par
people and the planet, as well as viable organizational
\par
economic benefits (Kleindorfer et al., 2005). In the
\par
context of supply chain management, environmental
\par
management capabilities require the efforts and ability
\par
of firms to manage the uncertainties in a supply
\par
chain, ranging from sourcing decision to demand
\par
forecast, which have different financial and environ-
\par
mental impacts (Carter \& Rogers, 2008). Corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness pertains to organiza-
\par
tional and supply chain-wide efforts to initiate,
\par
develop, introduce, diffuse and implement novel envi-
\par
ronmental management practices (Roy, Sivakumar \&
\par
Wilkinson, 2004). Such advanced development of
\par
environmental management capabilities often goes
\par
beyond environmental requirements and standards.
\par
Corporate environmental innovativeness enables firms
\par
to preempt and anticipate changes in environmental
\par
requirements and market expectations, avoiding a lack
\par
of compliance with environmental regulation that
\par
may lead to consequential legal fines and customer
\par
boycott. The case of Procter \& Gamble's invention of
\par
Tide concentrated detergent is an exemplar of a suc-
\par
cessful innovation in environmental product develop-
\par
ment. Such effort of Procter \& Gamble enabled it to
\par
cope with Walmart's new packaging reduction request
\par
in 2008 (GreenerDesign Staff, 2008), which otherwise
\par
may have stopped Tide from shelving its products in
\par
one of the biggest retail outlets in North America.
\par
Corporate environmental innovativeness protects
\par
investments in product and process development,
\par
while reducing environmental impacts by eliminating
\par
the use of hazardous materials, the consumption of
\par
new resources and raw materials, and the disposal to
\par
landfills by taking into account of the environmental
\par
impacts of products throughout their life cycle.
\par
H4: Corporate environmental innovativeness is
\par
positively associated with (a) financial perfor-
\par
mance and (b) environmental performance.
\par
The degree of flexibility and responsiveness in
\par
responding to new market demands constitute firms'
\par
environmental adaptability. It maintains the agility in
\par
firms' environmental practices to adjust to new envi-
\par
ronmental requirements and to take advantage of
\par
increasing knowledge about environmental protection.
\par
Having such capabilities enable firms to reduce waste
\par
of efforts and lead time in responding to new envi-
\par
ronmental requirements and commercializing new
\par
products, contributing to the triple bottom line by
\par
improving return on investment, as well as reducing
\par
waste and disposal (Carter \& Dresner, 2001). As such,
\par
corporate environmental adaptability is valuable for
\par
firms in adapting to market changes and gaining
\par
investment payback in a short period of time. Such
