\par
METHODOLOGY
\par
Sample and Data Collection
\par
We selected a random sample of 1,000 firms partici-
\par
pating in voluntary environmental management
\par
schemes of environmental excellence and green label
\par
schemes in Hong Kong to serve as our empirical set-
\par
ting for the following reasons. First, firms' participa-
\par
tion in these environmental management schemes
\par
reflects their commitment to environmental protec-
\par
tion. These firms conduct and report their environ-
\par
mental management practices and results voluntarily.
\par
As one of our research questions is to examine EII as
\par
antecedents of environmental management capabili-
\par
ties in terms of corporate environmental innovative-
\par
ness and adaptability, and their performance impact,
\par
this sample is appropriate for providing the relevant
\par
information due to the initiatives taken in environ-
\par
mental protection. Second, these groups consist of
\par
firms in different industries, which offer different
\par
products and services, enabling us to improve the gen-
\par
eralizability of the findings of firms operating in dif-
\par
ferent supply chains with different EII arrangements.
\par
Third, our sample aims at achieving environmental
\par
protection goals through resource conservation, waste
\par
and carbon footprint reduction, improvement in air
\par
quality, and product development with environmental
\par
attributes. Such coverage of environmental initiatives
\par
provides information about organizational efforts in
\par
these aspects by collaborating with their supply chain
\par
partners.
\par
Following the procedures recommended by Gerbing
\par
and Anderson (1988), we first conducted interviews
\par
with 10 senior managers in the areas of environmen-
\par
tal, supply chain and information systems manage-
\par
ment in industries including manufacturing,
\par
construction and retailing to understand industry
\par
practices. These interviews revealed that environmen-
\par
tal management is prevalent and vital to businesses,
\par
and the participation of supply chain partners is
\par
important to its success. Many firms are working
\par
closely with their supply chain partners to reduce their
\par
environmental impact, while encouraging innovation
\par
and change to embed environmental management
\par
into business activities. Second, we developed a pool
\par
of measurement items from the literature and
\par
amended the items systematically to reflect organiza-
\par
tional efforts in environmental protection. Third, we
\par
pre-tested the survey items with five managers and five
\par
academics in the field to assess the clarity, relevancy
\par
and comprehensiveness of the measurement scales.
\par
We refined the measurement based on their feedback
\par
and conducted a pilot test with 40 managers. We then
\par
conducted an exploratory factor analysis to purify the
\par
scales, and on the basis of the pilot test results, we
\par
refined and finalized the questionnaire.
\par
A survey package containing the questionnaire, a
\par
self-addressed prepaid reply envelop and a cover letter
\par
explaining the purpose of this research (assuring the
\par
answers of respondents are reported in aggregate and
\par
their company details are kept confidential) was
\par
mailed to each of them (Dillman, 2000). We made
\par
follow-up telephone calls 5 days after the first mailing
\par
to seek acknowledgment of the receipt of the survey
\par
package and to stress the importance of their response
\par
in this research. Two weeks after the follow-up calls,
\par
we sent another survey package to the nonrespon-
\par
dents. We then sent another survey package to the
\par
nonrespondents 3 weeks after the second mailing.
\par
Our data collection was concluded 3 weeks after the
\par
final mailing with a total of 238 returned question-
\par
naires. We eliminated eight returns due to significant
\par
missing data. The resulting response rate is 23 percent,
\par
which is comparable to other survey-based environ-
\par
mental management studies (Ramus \& Steger, 2000).
\par
Measurement Development
\par
We operationalized the constructs using the reflec-
\par
tive measures based on the guidelines recommended
\par
by Jarvis, MacKenzie and Podsakoff (2003). We
\par
adopted measurements that have been employed pre-
\par
viously to improve the reliability and validity of the
\par
measures. A five-point Likert scale was used for all the
\par
measures, where a higher value indicates a higher
\par
level of integration and environmental management
\par
capabilities, or better performance.
\par
Environmental Information Integration. We
\par
adopted the measure of internal information integra-
\par
tion from previous studies (Gold, Malhotra \& Segars,
\par
2001; Rai, Patnayakuni \& Seth, 2006). The scale cap-
\par
tures the extent to which firms encourage and provide
\par
infrastructure to facilitate employees' access to envi-
\par
ronmental management information. Similarly, we
\par
adopted the measurement for customer and supplier
\par
information integration from the literature (Frohlich
\par
\& Westbrook, 2001; Rai et al., 2006). These two con-
\par
structs reflect the relationships between a focal firm
\par
and its customers and suppliers in the collaboration
\par
and sharing of environmental information. They
\par
measure the extent to which a focal firm has devel-
\par
oped mechanisms and infrastructures to exchange
\par
environmental information. The respondents were
