\par
Environmental Management Capabilities. For the
\par
environmental management capabilities measures, we
\par
developed the measure of corporate environmental
\par
innovativeness based on Wang and Ahmed (2004).
\par
The scale assesses the innovativeness of firms in devel-
\par
oping environmentally responsible products and ser-
\par
vices and adopting new environmental management
\par
practices to sustain the growth of businesses with low
\par
levels of environmental impact. On the other hand,
\par
we developed the measure of corporate environmen-
\par
tal adaptability based on Gold et al. (2001). The mea-
\par
sure was rephrased systematically to reflect the extent
\par
to which firms have flexibility to respond to new and
\par
changing market demands and environmental
\par
requirements. The respondents were asked to indicate
\par
the frequency of occurrence of corporate environmen-
\par
tal innovativeness and adaptability on a five-point
\par
Likert scale with 1= almost never to 5= almost
\par
always.
\par
Performance. For the performance variables, we
\par
adapted the measure of financial and environmental
\par
performance from the study by Montabon
\par
et al.(2007). The measure assesses financial perfor-
\par
mance with regard to returns on investment, market
\par
share growth, profit growth and profit growth rate gen-
\par
erated from environmentally friendly products relative
\par
to their industry's average on a five-point Likert scale
\par
with 1= significantly below average to 5= signifi-
\par
cantly above average. The environmental performance
\par
measure evaluates the extent to which firms reduce
\par
their environmental impact through a reduction in
\par
hazardous materials, resource consumption, green-
\par
house gas emission, waste disposal and waste water
\par
drainage.
\par
Control Variables. We included firm size (i.e.
\par
number of employees) and primary product/service
\par
type (i.e., multiple industry categories) as control vari-
\par
ables. Larger firms tend to be more resourceful in sup-
\par
porting the development of information integration
\par
(Zhu, 2004), while product type affects the coordina-
\par
tion amongst supply chain partners (Fisher, 1997)
\par
due to differences in market demands and environ-
\par
mental requirements. The model analyses with the
\par
control variables are discussed in Appendix 1.
\par
ANALYSES AND RESULTS
\par
Sample Profiles
\par
To ensure the qualification of the respondents, we
\par
collected information about their knowledge and
\par
involvement in environmental management in their
\par
organizations, and the number of years they have
\par
worked in their organizations. The informants indi-
\par
cated average to high (3.8 of a five-point scale) level
\par
of involvement and knowledge about environmental
\par
management, and 56.5 percent of informants have
\par
worked in their organizations for more than 5 years.
\par
Their positions include CEO, COO, Director and
\par
Manager of Environmental Department. Table 2 sum-
\par
marizes the respondents' profiles in terms of industry,
\par
firm size and annual sales.
\par
