Measurement Validation
\par
We performed confirmatory factor analysis (CFA)
\par
using AMOS 18.0 to assess the psychometric properties
\par
of the factor structures. Following Gerbing and Ander-
\par
son (1988), we used the maximum likelihood estima-
\par
tion with covariance to test measurement scales. We
\par
assessed their reliability and validity with an over-
\par
all confirmatory measurement model, where each
\par
measurement item loads onto its respective latent con-
\par
structs. All factor loadings were statistically significant
\par
(p {$<$} 0.01). The composite reliability of each construct
\par
exceeded the recommended 0.70 threshold, ranging
\par
from 0.87 to 0.94. As shown in Table 4, the average
\par
variance extracted (AVE) from each construct was
\par
greater than the threshold of 0.5 (Fornell \& Larcker,
\par
1981), indicating strong convergent validity. Table 4
\par
summarizes the item loading, and the Cronbach's
\par
alpha, composite reliability and AVE of each construct.
\par
We assessed discriminant validity by conducting chi-
\par
square difference tests between nested CFA models for
\par
all pairs of constructs. We compared the chi-square
\par
between the unconstrained model (i.e., the model
\par
with two constructs that vary freely) and the con-
\par
strained model (i.e., the model with the correlations
\par
between two constructs constrained to 1) (Bagozzi,
\par
Yi \& Phillips, 1991). Table 5 summarizes the chi-
\par
square different test results and the chi-square of
\par
the constrained and unconstrained models. The sig-
\par
nificant chi-square differences between all pairs of
\par
constructs suggest discriminant validity of our mea-
\par
surement.
