\par
tiveness (b= 0.14, p {$>$} 0.05), providing support for
\par
H1b but not H1a. These results suggest that firms with
\par
a higher level of customer EII achieve a higher level of
\par
environmental management capabilities, in innovating
\par
environmental management practices and products
\par
and adapting to new market needs.
\par
The two key functions of environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities, corporate environmental innova-
\par
tiveness and adaptability, have a significant and
\par
positive impact on both business and environmental
\par
performance. As shown in Figure 1, corporate envi-
\par
ronmental innovativeness is positively and signifi-
\par
cantly associated with financial performance
\par
