DISCUSSION
\par
Major Findings and Theoretical Implications
\par
First, customer EII engenders environmental man-
\par
agement capabilities of firms in terms of corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness and adaptability. The
\par
findings reveal that customer EII contributes to the
\par
environmental management capabilities of firms, in
\par
particular through effective information sharing and
\par
communication with customers. Such EII improves
\par
the ability of firms to innovate new environmen-
\par
tal management products and practices to tackle
\par
environmental problems, while being adaptable and
\par
responsive to new environmental demands and
\par
requirements. This finding is consistent with the
\par
theoretical conjectures of DC theory, where customer
\par
EII provides new information and knowledge that
\par
integrate with the existing environmental management
\par
practices, enabling firms to manage the changing
\par
environmental requirements. This provides insight
\par
into the information integration and environmental
\par
management literature, by suggesting the usefulness
\par
of integrating with customers and probing into cus-
\par
tomer information to improve the environmental
\par
management capabilities of firms (Elliot, 2011). In
\par
addition, from the perspective of supply chain man-
\par
agement, the importance of customer EII suggests that
