\par
environmental management capabilities stem from
\par
market intelligence that is collected through sharing
\par
information and communication with customers
\par
(Tate, Ellram \& Kirchoff, 2010). This is in line with
\par
the literature that emphasizes the importance of
\par
acquiring from customers the latest market informa-
\par
tion and requirements (Frohlich \& Westbrook, 2001).
\par
The study results show that customer EII plays the
\par
strongest role among the three types of EII. The
\par
importance of customer EII can be attributed to rising
\par
market expectations and demands on environmental
\par
protection.
\par
Second, internal EII significantly contributes to cor-
\par
porate environmental adaptability, but not corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness. The study findings
\par
reveal that internal EII is critical for firms to respond
\par
quickly to new environmental requirements, but is
\par
not significantly related to the ability of firms to inno-
\par
vate new environmental products and practices. That
\par
is, according to DC theory, internal EII provides an
\par
information sharing infrastructure, which improves
\par
cooperation across functions and the capabilities of
\par
firms to adapt to changes in organizational processes.
\par
There was no evidence that such EII contributed to
\par
the ability of firms to innovate new environmental
\par
products and practices. This finding advances the
\par
knowledge of DC theory concerning information inte-
\par
gration. Although internal EII can improve the effec-
\par
tiveness of business functions in responding to new
\par
challenges, new market intelligence, such as customer
\par
expectations and market trends, collected from cus-
\par
tomer EII, is important in supporting corporate envi-
\par
ronmental innovativeness. In other words, internal EII
\par
is insufficient to support innovation for environmen-
\par
tal management. This is consistent with prior DC
\par
studies that indicate the importance of having infor-
\par
mation acquisition routines to enable knowledge crea-
\par
tion and innovation processes (Helfat, 1997).
\par
Third, supplier EII does not contribute to corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness and adaptability.
\par
Although DC theory advocates that EII can be benefi-
\par
cial to rendering organizational performance advan-
\par
tage by providing information sharing capacity, the
\par
theory is less clear about what specific type of EII may
\par
lead to environmental management capabilities in the
\par
context of supply chain management. The study find-
\par
ings suggest that supplier EII is not significantly asso-
\par
ciated with environmental innovativeness and
\par
adaptability. Although supplier EII enables efficient
\par
coordination with suppliers, it fails to support innova-
\par
tion and agile adaptations on environmental pro-
\par
cesses. Further interviews with some of the
\par
respondents suggest that although their suppliers are
\par
important in providing information related to sustain-
\par
able sources of materials and components, their sup-
\par
pliers rarely play a strategic role in new product and
\par
process development for environmental protection.
\par
This lack of strategic positioning of supplier EII hin-
\par
ders its contribution to corporate environmental inno-
\par
vativeness and adaptability. Firms rely on themselves
\par
to design their new environmental management prac-
\par
tices, to ensure the new environmental management
\par
practices are compatible with their existing practices
\par
and processes, and to avoid disruption to their opera-
\par
tions. This results in a lack of recognition of the bene-
\par
fits of establishing information sharing infrastructure
\par
with suppliers. On the contrary, customer EII has sig-
\par
nificant influence on environmental management
\par
capabilities, as firms are often market oriented,
\par
addressing market needs by acquiring information
