\par
Fourth, corporate environmental innovativeness and
\par
adaptability are positively associated with business
\par
and environmental performance. Consistent with DC
\par
theory, the study findings suggest that innovativeness
\par
and adaptability of firms in environmental manage-
\par
ment are critical organizational capabilities that are
\par
valuable to business and environmental performance.
\par
While environmental management capabilities
\par
improves firms' ability to develop environmental
\par
products and practices that may preempt new and
\par
future environmental regulations and requirements
\par
(Handfield, Walton, Seegers \& Melnyk, 1997; Hand-
\par
field et al., 2005), it is also important for firms to
\par
sustain businesses by capturing additional market
\par
share, increasing returns on investment, and so forth.
\par
This study provides empirical evidence concerning
\par
environmental sustainability, which shows that envi-
\par
ronmental management capabilities, in terms of cor-
\par
porate environmental innovativeness and adaptability,
\par
are important to business and environmental perfor-
\par
mance improvement.
\par
Managerial Implications
\par
This paper offers several implications for managers.
\par
First, our findings show that customer EII is critical
\par
for environmental management capabilities. Firms are
\par
recommended to strengthen their customer EII to
\par
achieve a seamless information flow to develop their
\par
environmental management capabilities. Even when
\par
facing limited resources, it is important that managers
\par
allocate resources to develop customer EII that pro-
\par
vides an information sharing mechanism to facilitate
\par
firms in acquiring the latest market information for
\par
product or process improvement.
\par
Second, managers should bear in mind that internal
\par
EII is important for firms in improving corporate envi-
\par
ronmental adaptability to the changing environmental
\par
demands and requirements. Firms facing uncertainties
\par
in coping with environmental management require-
\par
ments in their marketplace should proactively develop
\par
internal and customer EII to increase flexibility, so as
\par
to respond quickly to new environmental standards
\par
and customer expectations.
\par
Third, managers should note the importance of
\par
environmental management capabilities in achieving
\par
both business and environmental performance. The
\par
capabilities of firms in innovating environmentally
\par
responsible practices and products and adapting to
\par
changing environmental requirements not only play a
\par
role in the reduction in resource consumption, waste
\par
and disposal, but also enable firms to improve their
\par
returns on investment, profit and market share.
\par
Limitations and Directions for Future Research
\par
This study has some limitations, and they are dis-
\par
cussed below with avenues for future research. First,
\par
this study attempts to advance understanding of the
\par
roles of EII in contributing to environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities in the context of supply chain man-
\par
agement. Although internal, supplier and customer EII
\par
are key factors that affect environmental operations in
\par
a supply chain, future studies may consider other
\par
supply chain factors, such as information technology
\par
adoption, and supply chain relational aspects and
\par
structures, which may affect the ability of firms to
\par
achieve environmental management capabilities. Also,
\par
the finding of the insignificant impact of supplier EII
\par
implies that there might be mediating variables, such
\par
as strategic partnerships with suppliers for environ-
\par
mental management, that enable firms to reap the
\par
benefits of supplier EII. Future studies may consider
\par
investigating the mediating factors that may facilitate
\par
or impede the relationship between supplier EII and
\par
environmental management capabilities. For example,
\par
supplier EII may contribute indirectly to corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness through internal EII.
\par
Second, two dimensions of environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities are considered in this study based
\par
on DC theory and the corporate social responsibility
\par
literature. Other organizational capabilities, such as
\par
stakeholder integration and continuous operations
\par
improvement, may be included in the study to
\par
explore environmental management capabilities from
\par
another theoretical perspective. Third, the insignificant
\par
impact of internal EII on corporate environmental
\par
innovativeness may imply the existence of precursors.
\par
An extension of this study may include such internal
\par
EII precursors as supplier EII, customer EII and con-
\par
sultation with stakeholders (e.g., communities and
\par
NGOs). Fourth, although we have carefully assessed
\par
the possibility of common method bias by using mul-
\par
tiple methods, future studies may collect more objec-
\par
tive data to further minimize the potential for this
\par
bias. Lastly, the cross-sectional design of this study
\par
did not provide empirical data to examine causal rela-
\par
tionships. Also, DC theory implies that capabilities
\par
change. The measurement of environmental manage-
\par
ment capabilities in terms of corporate environmental
\par
innovativeness and adaptability provides an indirect
\par
measure that could not assess the change in the
\par
underlying capabilities over time. A future longitudi-
\par
nal study would be desirable to complement this
\par
research study by revealing the evolution of the asso-
\par
ciations between the dimensions of EII, corporate
\par
environmental innovativeness and adaptability, and
\par
business and environmental performance.
