\par
QIONG WU and CHUNYAN MIAO, Nanyang Technological University
\par
Literature in psychology has shown that curiosity is the intrinsic motivation for exploration, learning, and
\par
creativity. Various forms of computational curiosity have been developed to provide artificial beings with
\par
desirable functions, such as detecting and adapting to novel inputs, making decisions related to aesthetics,
\par
and achieving pedagogical purposes. This article reviews existing models of computational curiosity in light
\par
of psychological theories that are beneficial to building models of human cognition and designing human-like
\par
agents. We first study theories in psychology to shed light on the underpinnings of human curiosity, where
\par
a two-step process is proposed to serve as a general model for analyzing curiosity. Subsequently, existing
\par
models of computational curiosity are reviewed under the proposed framework. We conclude the review
\par
by identifying 4 key research issues in computational curiosity and 10 important research areas where
\par
computational curiosity could bring significant impact.
\par
Categories and Subject Descriptors: I.2.0 [Artificial Intelligence]: General---Cognitive simulation; I.2.6
\par
[Artificial Intelligence]: Learning; I.2.9 [Artificial Intelligence]: Robotics; I.2.11 [Artificial Intelli-
\par
gence]: Distributed Artificial Intelligence---Intelligent agents
\par
General Terms: Theory, Model, Application
\par
Additional Key Words and Phrases: Human curiosity, computational curiosity, curious agents, curious robots,
\par
curious learning
\par
ACM Reference Format:
\par
Wu, Q. and Miao, C. 2013. Curiosity: From psychology to computation. ACM Comput. Surv. 46, 2, Article 18
\par
(November 2013), 26 pages.
\par
DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1145/2543581.2543585
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
What is curiosity? You are caught in a state of curiosity when you can't wait to read for
\par
the answer. Curiosity has been consistently recognized as a critical motivation that is
\par
associated with exploratory behaviors such as exploration, investigation, and learning
\par
[Kashdan et al. 2004]. It has been identified as a driving force for child development,
\par
scientific research, and educational achievement [Loewenstein 1994]. According to
\par
Kashdan [2010], curiosity benefits human beings at two levels: the individual level
\par
and the social level. At the individual level, curiosity is associated with individual
\par
growth, as an ``innate love of learning and of knowledge...without the lure of any profit''
\par
[Loewenstein 1994]. At the social level, curiosity is an ingredient for enhancing inter-
\par
personal relationships through infusing energy and passion into social interactions.
\par
Computational forms of curiosity have been developed to endow machines with de-
\par
sirable functions. For example, a curious design agent can arrange art exhibits to
\par
elicit the curiosity of their viewers and provide an aesthetically pleasing experience
\par
[Saunders and Gero 2001], a curious exploratory agent achieves higher learning
