\par
efficiency in unknown environments [Macedo and Cardoso 2005], and a curious learn-
\par
ing companion is able to detect interesting learning topics and artfully direct students'
\par
attention to them [Wu et al. 2012]. In this work, we attempt to offer a review on com-
\par
putational curiosity, with the aim to analyze the current research development and
\par
envision future trends.
\par
From a machine learning perspective, curiosity has been proposed as algorithmic
\par
principles to focus learning on novel and learnable regularities in contrast to irregular
\par
noises [Schmidhuber 1991a; Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004; Pang et al. 2009; Karaoguz
\par
et al. 2011]. These principles make machines ``explorative'' and allow machines to
\par
have ``the desire to improve the model's knowledge about the world'' [Schmidhuber
\par
1991c, 2006]. These principles have shown success in speeding up learning and building
\par
unsupervised developmental robotics [Schmidhuber 2006; Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004].
\par
Human beings, unfortunately, are often more complex than what a machine learning
\par
model can describe. Most machine learning algorithms are strictly utilitarian. The ma-
\par
chine always remains enthusiastic to learn and mostly assumes that there is something
\par
to be learned. On the other hand, a person may behave less rationally. For example,
\par
a person may lack the will to learn even when ample opportunities for learning exist.
\par
Alternatively, a person may be curious about a stimulus but realize that little can
\par
be learned from it due to a lack of knowledge or adverse environments (e.g., infor-
\par
mation blocked by a firewall). In order to provide a more complete model of human
\par
cognition and design artificial companions that can elicit curiosity (e.g., for pedagogical
\par
purposes), we believe that it is beneficial to go back to the research in psychology to
\par
understand how human curiosity can be aroused.
\par
Research in psychology suggests that curiosity can be externally stimulated by vari-
\par
ous factors, such as novelty, conflict, uncertainty, and complexity [Berlyne 1960]. Each
\par
of these factors characterizes a different condition where curiosity can potentially be
\par
elicited. For example, novelty is induced by something new, whereas surprisingness
\par
occurs when an expectation based on previous knowledge is violated by the actual
\par
outcome. Several curiosity stimulating factors have been considered in computational
\par
curiosity. Saunders and Gero [2001] modeled novelty in their curious design agent;
\par
Macedo and Cardoso [2005] incorporated novelty, surprise, and uncertainty into their
\par
explorative agent; and Wu et al. [2012] considered novelty, uncertainty, conflict, and
\par
complexity in their curious pedagogical agent. The explicit consideration of different
\par
curiosity stimulating factors makes the curious agents more human-like, more effi-
\par
cient in exploration [Macedo and Cardoso 2005], and more reactive in collaborative
\par
learning environments [Wu et al. 2012]. Hence, we believe that the explicit consider-
\par
ation of various curiosity stimulating factors can enrich the current understanding of
\par
computational curiosity and open up new directions for future development.
\par
In this article, we attempt to answer the following research questions:
\par
(1) What is the current common understanding on human curiosity in psychology?
\par
(2) What is the current research progress on computational curiosity?
\par
(3) What are the potential research directions for computational curiosity?
\par
The article is organized as follows. Section 2 provides a brief review on psycholog-
\par
ical studies of human curiosity and offers some fundamental guidelines for modeling
\par
computational curiosity in humanoid agents. Along this review of psychology, we pro-
\par
pose a two-step process of curiosity arousal that serves as a psychological viewpoint
\par
to study its computational implementations. Subsequently, in Section 3, we review
\par
existing computational models of curiosity in view of the two-step model and create
\par
taxonomy for them. Four key research issues regarding the theoretical models of compu-
\par
tational curiosity are summarized in Section 4. In Section 5, the review is concluded by
\par
