\par
pointing out 10 important research areas where computational curiosity can be infused
\par
to achieve significant innovations.
\par
\textbf{2. A BRIEF REVIEW ON HUMAN CURIOSITY}
\par
Research on human curiosity has attracted scientists' interest since back in the 1960s,
\par
when researchers attempted to uncover the underpinnings of curiosity [Loewenstein
\par
1994]. The effort continues to date with researchers focusing on interpreting rela-
\par
tionships between curiosity and human well-being [Kashdan 2010]. In this section, we
\par
briefly review psychological studies on human curiosity while answering two questions:
\par
(1) what are the underpinnings of curiosity? and (2) what is its arousal mechanism?
\par
Knowledge from psychological research on curiosity can provide insight into modeling
\par
curiosity computationally.
\par
\textbf{2.1. Underpinnings of Curiosity}
\par
In human beings, curiosity is closely related to cognition, emotion, and behaviors. It
\par
underlines human cognitive development, aesthetic appreciation, and interpersonal re-
\par
lationships. In this section, we review the literature in psychology on the underpinning
\par
of curiosity and the benefits of curiosity for human beings.
\par
2.1.1. Categories of Curiosity. Most psychologists believe that curiosity is an intrin-
\par
sic motivation driving the cognitive development of both humans and animals alike.
\par
Berlyne [1960] categorized curiosity along two spectrums: (1) from perceptual curiosity
\par
to epistemic curiosity and (2) from specific curiosity to diversive curiosity. Perceptual
\par
curiosity, which resides in the lower level of cognition, stems from the senses of both
\par
animals and humans (e.g., senses of touch, vision, taste, etc.). It is defined as ``a drive
\par
that is aroused by novel stimuli and reduced by continued exposure to these stimuli''
\par
[Loewenstein 1994]. Epistemic curiosity, referred to as ``an appetite for knowledge,'' is
\par
related to the higher level of cognition and believed to be a distinctive human feature.
\par
Whereas perceptual curiosity and epistemic curiosity are defined along the lines of
\par
``lower'' and ``higher'' levels of cognition, specific curiosity and diversive curiosity are
\par
distinguished by the possibility of curiosity having a ``direction.'' Specific curiosity is
\par
aroused by a particular piece of information. Diversive curiosity is a general drive to
\par
seek information with no specific direction and is predominantly employed to relieve
\par
boredom.
\par
Berlyne [1960]'s cognitive account for curiosity has been a theoretical foundation
\par
for the majority of recent studies. Litman and Spielberger [2003] agreed with Berlyne
\par
[1960] that there is a salient difference between diversive curiosity and specific curios-
\par
ity, and conducted experimental analysis to provide scales for measuring both concepts.
\par
They further concluded that diversive curiosity and specific curiosity, as well as percep-
\par
tual curiosity and epistemic curiosity, are ``substantially correlated.'' Spielberger and
\par
Starr [1994] associated diversive curiosity with ``low-level'' stimuli and specific curios-
\par
ity with ``high-level'' stimuli. However, Schmitt and Lahroodi [2008] disagreed with the
\par
notion that curiosity can be diversive. They argued that curiosity can only be specific
\par
toward its possessor and objects that cause the curiosity. Instead of diversive curiosity,
\par
they referred to the generic desire for knowledge as ``inquisitiveness.''
\par
Nevertheless, a general consensus among the psychologists points to the close re-
\par
lationship between curiosity and cognition as a drive to explore novel stimuli or an
\par
appetite for knowledge.
\par
2.1.2. Curiosity-Related Emotions. Curiosity is related to emotional constructs. In an
\par
early account of curiosity by James [1950], curiosity is viewed as an instinctual or emo-
\par
tional response closely related to fear. He believed that curiosity motivates organisms
\par
to actively explore their environment, whereas fear tends to turn the organisms away
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:4 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
from the risks induced by unfamiliarity. Berlyne [1960]'s branch of research, referred to
\par
as ``drive theory'' by Loewenstein [1994], is based on the assumption that curiosity pro-
\par
duces an unpleasant sensation that is reduced by exploratory behavior. Loewenstein
\par
believed that rather than serving a purposive end, the primary objective of satisfying
\par
one's curiosity is to induce pleasure.
\par
Wundt [1874] introduced the theory of ``optimal level of stimulation,'' which serves
\par
as a general rule postulating the relationships between stimulus intensity and hedonic
\par
tone. Based on this, Berlyne [1960] proposed that there is a need of ``intermediate
\par
arousal potential'' for curiosity to be aroused. This theory demonstrates that too little
\par
stimulation can result in boredom or inaction, whereas too much stimulation may
\par
result in aversion or withdrawal. Only when the level of stimulation is optimal and
\par
pleasurable can exploratory behavior occur.
\par
From the preceding discussion, it can be seen that curiosity is closely related to
\par
emotional constructs such as fear, pleasure, boredom and anxiety. The decision on
\par
whether to explore or to avoid a stimulus is driven by emotional comfort and results in
\par
behaviors that regulate emotional states.
\par
2.1.3. Curiosity-Related Behaviors. The most salient expression of curiosity is through
\par
exploratory behaviors, by which curiosity can be satisfied. Berlyne [1960] defined two
\par
levels of exploratory behaviors, one associated with the perceptual level of curiosity and
\par
the other associated with the epistemic level of curiosity. At each level, the exploratory
\par
behaviors can take many forms.
\par
At the perceptual level of curiosity, Berlyne divided exploratory behaviors into three
\par
categories according to the nature of responses. He referred to the exploratory behav-
\par
iors as orienting responses if they consist of changes in posture, orientations of sensory
\par
organs, or states of sensory organs. The second category of exploratory behaviors is as-
\par
sociated with locomotion, such as approaching or withdrawing from the stimuli. When
\par
an exploratory behavior causes changes in external objects, through manipulation or
\par
otherwise, it is called an investigatory response.
\par
At the epistemic level of curiosity, Berlyne also defined three categories of exploratory
\par
behaviors. The first category is observation, which places the subject in contact with
\par
external situations that can nourish the pertinent learning process. The second cat-
\par
egory is thinking, which refers to ``productive'' and ``creative'' thinking rather than
\par
``reproductive thinking'' that only calls up memories to determine how problems should
\par
be handled. The last category is consultation, which exposes an individual to verbal
\par
stimuli issued from other individuals, including questioning and reading, and so forth.
\par
Through exploratory behaviors, cognitive growth is achieved by creative thinking
\par
and emotional states are regulated toward a feeling of pleasure.
\par
2.1.4. Benefits of Curiosity. Current research indicates that curiosity can contribute to
\par
human well-being at two distinct levels: the individual level and the social level.
\par
At the individual level, curiosity provides an emotional motivation for self-
\par
development. It is the driving force for child development as well as an important
\par
spur for educational achievement [Loewenstein 1994]. In addition, literature in psy-
\par
chology indicates a close relation between curiosity and aesthetics, humor, and fun.
\par
According to Berlyne [1960], these behaviors have common motivational factors and
\par
are reinforced by common sources of reward.
\par
At the social level, curiosity can enhance interpersonal relationships. By studying
\par
the role of curiosity in conversations, Kashdan et al. [2011] suggested that curiosity can
\par
build social bonds by promoting behaviors such as engagement, responsiveness, and
\par
flexibility. These are desirable behaviors for developing interpersonal relationships
\par
and building intimacy. Their findings indicate that curiosity is uniquely related to the
\par
development of interpersonal closeness with strangers [Kashdan et al. 2004].
\par
