\par
\textbf{2.2. Arousal of Curiosity}
\par
In this section, we review the literature in psychology on the arousal mechanism of
\par
curiosity and provide insight into possible ways of implementing curiosity in artificial
\par
beings.
\par
According to Loewenstein [1994], existing psychological theories on human curiosity
\par
can be divided into three categories: incongruity theory, competence theory, and drive
\par
theory. Incongruity theory holds on to the idea that curiosity is evoked by violation of
\par
expectations [Hebb 1949]. Competence theory views curiosity as an intrinsic motivation
\par
to master one's environment [White 1959]. Drive theory believes in the existence of
\par
a curious drive, either primary (homeostatic generated in a similar way as hunger)
\par
or secondary (externally generated by stimuli). As commented by Loewenstein, the
\par
incongruity theory and the competence theory suffer from the same deficiency that
\par
both fail to offer a comprehensive account of curiosity. Hence, in this work, we focus on
\par
the drive theory and adopt Berlyne [1960]'s interpretation of curiosity.
\par
According to Berlyne [1960], traditional psychological research concentrated on prob-
\par
lems of response selection, which studies what response humans will make to one
\par
standard stimulus at a time. However, curiosity deals with a different problem from
\par
response selection, which is referred to as stimulus selection. Stimulus selection dis-
\par
cusses when several conspicuous stimuli are introduced at once, to which stimulus
\par
humans will respond. Berlyne [1960] has conducted extensive experimental studies to
\par
understand the process of stimulus selection and discovered a set of collative variables
\par
that govern this process.
\par
2.2.1. Collative Variables. Collative variables, according to Berlyne [1960], refer to the
\par
external factors that govern various forms of stimulus selection. There are four major
\par
collative variables: novelty, uncertainty, conflict, and complexity. Berlyne [1960] named
\par
these factors collative variables because the evaluation of each variable involves an
\par
analysis of similarities and differences between elements in a stimulus pattern. In the
\par
following part of this section, the major collative variables are reviewed.
\par
Novelty denotes something new. In accordance with the time when a stimulus has
\par
been experienced by an organism, novelty can be divided into short-term, long-term, and
\par
complete novelty. A stimulus can either be completely new to an organism, or it could
\par
have been experienced within just the past few minutes. In the former case, it is called
\par
complete novelty, and in the latter case, it is called short-term novelty. The intermediate
\par
case where a stimulus has not been experienced for a period of time (usually days) is
\par
called long-term novelty. Based on whether a stimulus possesses qualities that has
\par
been perceived before, it is classified into absolute novelty or relative novelty. If the
\par
stimulus does not have any previously perceived quality, then it is absolute novelty;
\par
otherwise, it is relative novelty.
\par
Based on these observations, Berlyne [1960] introduced three criteria to measure
\par
novelty: novelty is inversely related to (1) how often the stimuli have been experienced
\par
before, (2) how recently the stimuli have been experienced, and (3) how similar the
\par
stimuli are to previously experienced ones.
\par
Novelty is often accompanied by other properties, each of which may have differ-
\par
ent influence on exploratory behaviors. Berlyne [1960] listed them as supplementary
\par
variables to novelty, which include change, surprisingness, and incongruity. A change
\par
of the stimulus in question may induce some priority in exploratory directions. Sur-
\par
prisingness arises when there is a stimulus that induces an expectation and a later
\par
stimulus that contradicts the expectation. Incongruity is somewhat different from sur-
\par
prisingness (e.g., the statement that the Earth is round is a surprise to people who
\par
are accustomed to the concept that the Earth is flat) and indicates an expectation that
\par
is not met by the same stimulus (e.g., a person who is used to receiving gifts on his
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:6 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
birthday before may find the experience incongruent when nobody remembered to send
\par
him gifts on his birthday this year).
\par
Uncertainty arises when an organism has difficulty selecting a response to a stimulus.
\par
Berlyne [1960] adopted information theory to quantify uncertainty. He proposed to
\par
measure the uncertainty caused by a stimulus with the following steps: (1) draw up a
\par
list of stimuli that might occur (as a response to the stimulus in question), (2) partition
\par
them into classes, and (3) assign a probability to each class. The probability pi of each
\par
class denotes the competing strength of each possible response, and Shannon's entropy
\par
H= -
\par
n
\par
i=1( pi \texttimes{} log2( pi )) denotes the degree of uncertainty.
\par
Conflict occurs when a stimulus arouses two or more incompatible responses in
\par
an organism. A response can be incompatible with one another in several ways. First,
\par
some responses may be innately antagonist to each other. For example, no organism can
\par
move forward and backward at the same time. Second, other responses may initially be
\par
capable of performing together but become incompatible through learning. For example,
\par
we seldom frown when shaking hands. The third reason for incompatible responses may
\par
be attributed to the limitation of an organism's ability to multitask. For example, it
\par
would be considered as an outstanding ability for a person to read two books at the
\par
same time.
\par
Berlyne [1960] proposed four criteria for measuring conflict. Conflict is positively
\par
related to (1) the nearness to equality in the strengths of competing responses, (2) the
\par
absolute strengths of competing responses, (3) the number of competing responses, and
\par
(4) the degree of incompatibility between competing responses.
\par
Complexity roughly refers to the variety or diversity in a stimulus pattern. The three
\par
most obvious properties that determine the degree of complexity are (1) the number of
\par
distinguishable elements in a stimulus, (2) the dissimilarity between these elements,
\par
and (3) the degree to which several elements are perceived and responded to as a unit.
\par
All of the collative variables are interrelated. Berlyne postulated that novel stimuli
\par
can lead to conflict when a new pattern of stimulus can be sufficiently similar to
\par
several familiar stimulus patterns, which may induce many incompatible responses. In
\par
addition, novelty has a connection with complexity. For example, a stimulus with short-
\par
term novelty will have a higher degree of temporal complexity than purely repetitive
\par
patterns. Alternatively, a higher degree of synchronous complexity may induce a higher
\par
degree of relative novelty. Moreover, complexity is also associated with uncertainty and
\par
conflict. For example, complex patterns with more parts can assume a larger amount
\par
of alternative forms, and the degree of uncertainty can be affected by the number of
\par
classes in these alternative forms; each subelement of a complex figure may induce
\par
different responses that are incompatible with each other, leading to a higher level of
\par
conflict.
\par
In summary, collative variables are properties in a stimulus that describe curios-
\par
ity stimulating conditions. According to Berlyne, they are all eminently quantitative
\par
properties that exist plainly in varying degrees. The quantitative property of collative
\par
variables makes them potential candidates for measuring the stimulation level of cu-
\par
riosity. Most of the computational models of curiosity have considered at least one of
\par
the collative variables for the determination of stimulation level (a review of the same
\par
will be provided in Section 3).
\par
2.2.2. Intermediate ArousalPotential. The existence of a stimulus does not necessarily re-
\par
sult in curiosity. The arousal of curiosity depends on the appropriate level of stimulation
\par
that can be induced by a stimulus.
\par
In the 1870s, Wundt [1874] introduced the concept of ``optimal level of stimulation''
\par
and postulated an inverted ``U-shape'' relationship between the stimulation level and
\par
the hedonic value, referred to as the Wundt curve (Figure 1). It is a general rule
