\par
\textbf{3. COMPUTATIONAL MODELS OF CURIOSITY}
\par
In this section, we review and analyze the existing computational models of curiosity
\par
from the perspective of the proposed two-step model. A general appraisal process of
\par
computational curiosity is summarized in Figure 2. The input stimuli are data samples
\par
that are perceived by agents and can trigger the appraisal process of computational
\par
curiosity. In Step 1, the appraisal process first evaluates the level of stimulation in the
\par
stimuli based on the collative variables considered in each model. Some models adopt
\par
a single collative variable to determine the stimulation value(e.g., Saunders and Gero
\par
[2001]), whereas others aggregate multiple collative variables to derive the stimulation
\par
value (e.g., Macedo and Cardoso [2005] and Wu et al. [2012]).
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:8 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
In Step 2, the level of curiosity is evaluated through a mapping from the stimulation
\par
value to the curiosity value. Some models follow the principle of intermediate arousal
\par
potential by explicitly simulating the Wundt curve, which represents a nonlinear map-
\par
ping from stimulation to curiosity (e.g., Saunders and Gero [2001] and Merrick et al.
\par
[2008]). These models accommodate both AoB and AoA in their stimulus selection ap-
\par
proaches. Other models simply use the stimulation value as the curiosity value (e.g.,
\par
Schmidhuber [1991b], Oudeyer and Kaplan [2004], and Macedo and Cardoso [2001])
\par
or assume a positive correlation between the stimulation level and the curiosity level
\par
(e.g., Wu et al. [2012]). Some of the models that directly use the stimulation value as
\par
the curiosity value consider the principle of intermediate arousal potential when de-
\par
termining the stimulation value (e.g., Schmidhuber [1991b] and Oudeyer and Kaplan
\par
[2004]), which also accommodate both AoB and AoA. The rest of the models that as-
\par
sume positive correlations between stimulation and curiosity only support AoB but not
\par
AoA because high stimulation can lead agents into anxiety (e.g., Macedo and Cardoso
\par
[2001] and Wu et al. [2012]).
\par
A taxonomy for the existing models of computational curiosity is provided in Table I.
\par
The models are classified into six categories according to the collative variables used
\par
for the evaluation of stimulation in Step 1. These six categories include novelty, sur-
\par
prise, uncertainty, conflict, change, and complexity. Within each category, the models
\par
are again classified into two subcategories, according to whether the principle of inter-
\par
mediate arousal potential is followed in the models. The first subcategory of models
\par
follows this principle and supports both AoB and AoA in stimulus selection, whereas
\par
the second subcategory only supports AoB.
\par
\textbf{3.1. Models Based on Novelty}
\par
As mentioned previously, Berlyne [1960] associated three criteria with the evaluation
\par
of novelty: the degree of novelty is inversely correlated to (1) how often the stimuli have
\par
been experienced before (frequency), (2) how recently the stimuli have been experienced
\par
(recency), and (3) how similar the stimuli are to previously experienced ones (similarity).
\par
From this description, it can be observed that the appraisal of novelty is time dependent
\par
and involves a comparison between the current stimuli and previous experiences.
\par
3.1.1. Similarity Based Methods. To explore the possibility of machines capable of gener-
\par
ating creativity, Saunders and Gero [2001] developed a computational model of curiosity
\par
for intelligent design agents, focusing on the appraisal of novelty. The goal of such an
\par
agent is to evaluate the interestingness of creative design patterns based on individual
\par
experiences, where curiosity is the key for the evaluation of interestingness and the
\par
selection of design patterns [Saunders and Gero 2001]. In their model, each creative
\par
design pattern (generated by a design generator) can be considered as a stimulus, and
\par
the stimuli experienced in the past are stored in a ``conceptual design space.'' This
\par
conceptual design space is modeled by a Self-Organizing Map (SOM), representing the
\par
forms that the agent has experienced often enough to learn.
\par
If we view their model from the perspective of the two-step model, in Step 1, the
\par
evaluation of stimulation is governed by the degree of novelty in a stimulus. The
\par
evaluation of novelty is achieved by comparing the stimulus (a creative design pattern)
\par
with past experiences (the SOM representations) and is defined by the complement of
\par
the typicality measure. This implementation is in line with Berlyne's third criteria for
\par
measuring novelty: novelty is inversely proportional to similarity (typicality).
\par
In Step 2, Saunders and Gero adopted the full principle of intermediate arousal
\par
potential and explicitly modeled the Wundt curve as a nonlinear function.This function
