reward to update the policy {$\pi$} that maps states to actions. This curiosity reward can
\par
eventually lead to creative behavior, because each new interesting event can result
\par
in a perturbation of the existing behavioral patterns and form reward signals for the
\par
learning process.
\par
For reconfigurable robots [Merrick and Huntington 2008], the role of curiosity is to
\par
direct the robots' attention to reconfigurations in their structures. This is an important
\par
skill for robots to learn new behaviors in response to structural changes. Similar to the
\par
implementation of curiosity for NPCs, an event can be considered as a stimulus and
\par
defined as the change between the current sensation and the previous one. Following
\par
a similar algorithm used for curious NPCs, curiosity reward is generated for robots.
\par
With this curiosity reward, the robots are self-motivated to explore changes in their
\par
structures and develop new behaviors.
\par
In the application domain of exploring unknown environments (e.g., planetary ex-
\par
ploration, map-building of interiors), Macedo and Cardoso [1999] proposed a model
\par
of curiosity for intelligent agents to simulate human-like exploratory behavior. Along
\par
their research, they gradually introduced novelty, surprise, and uncertainty into their
\par
computational model of curiosity. Here, we first look at their model of novelty.
\par
Macedo and Cardoso's model relies on graph-based mental representations of objects.
\par
Each object can be regarded as a stimulus. In Step 1, the level of novelty regarding a
\par
stimulus (object) is measured based on the error correcting code theory of Hamming.
\par
Three steps are considered: (1) representing each graph (describing an object) in a com-
\par
mon shape matrix, (2) extracting the numerical code from the matrix representation
\par
of each graph, and (3) computing the Hamming Distance. Here, novelty is defined as
\par
the minimum Hamming Distance from the stimulus to the ones that have been expe-
\par
rienced before. Novelty determines the stimulation value. In Step 2, the stimulation
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:11
\par
value is directly used as the curiosity value. The system only supports AoB because a
\par
higher level of curiosity increases the chance of an object to be explored.
\par
For both models by Saunders and Gero [2001] and Macedo and Cardoso [1999],
\par
regardless of the implementation---for instance, atypicality in SOM and hamming dis-
\par
tance in graph-based mental representations--- the model of novelty reflects a compar-
\par
ison between the current stimuli and previous experiences (in other words, similarity).
\par
3.1.2. Time and Similarity Based Methods. In some later models of curiosity, the factor
\par
of time, as another dimension in addition to similarity, is considered for measuring
\par
novelty.
\par
Ogino et al. [2006] addressed lexical acquisition problems for robots using compu-
\par
tational curiosity to associate visual features of observed objects with the labels (for
\par
objects) that are uttered by a caregiver. In their work, each object can be interpreted
\par
as a stimulus for the robot. Visual features of each object are represented by an SOM.
\par
In Step 1, stimulation value is determined by novelty. The novelty of each object is
