\par
The curious companion maintains two sources of information: world knowledge,
\par
which is defined by experts and embedded in the virtual world, and user knowledge,
\par
which is updated by users in real time. Both types of knowledge are represented by
\par
Concept Maps (CMs). In the CM for world knowledge, each concept is embodied by a
\par
virtual object, and each relationship between concepts specifies a knowledge point that
\par
can be learned by users through interactions with the virtual objects. The CM for user
\par
knowledge represents the human learner's current understanding.
\par
Here, each virtual object embedded with learning contents can be considered as a
\par
stimulus. In Step 1, novelty of a stimulus is determined by three factors: frequency,
\par
recency, and similarity. Frequency (how many times a virtual object has been met in a
\par
time period) and recency (how recently a virtual object has been met) are time-related
\par
variables that can be retrieved from the user's behavior history. Similarity is an inte-
\par
ger determined by the number of knowledge points learned by the learner correctly,
\par
through the comparison between world knowledge and user knowledge. Novelty is cal-
\par
culated based on a fuzzy combination of all three factors using a Fuzzy Cognitive Map
\par
(FCM) based approach. In Step 2, a linear relationship is assumed between stimulation
\par
and curiosity, and the curiosity value is positively proportional to the level of novelty
\par
(specified by the weight of FCM). The combination of novelty and the other three colla-
\par
tive variables (which will be introduced in subsequent sections) determines the final
\par
level of curiosity. The agent will select the virtual object with the highest curiosity
\par
value for the user to explore, which only supports AoB in stimulus selection.
\par
\textbf{3.2. Models Based on Surprise}
\par
Two interpretations for surprise exist in the literature of computational curiosity. The
\par
first one interprets surprise as the difference between an expectation and the real
\par
outcome. Prediction error matches well with this interpretation and has been utilized
\par
in many curiosity models to measure the level of surprise [Schmidhuber 1991a; Barto
\par
et al. 2004; Schmidhuber 1999; U \textasciibreve{} gur et al. 2007]. The second interpretation describes
\par
surprise as the degree of not expecting something. Storck et al. [1995] modeled this
\par
type of surprise using the information gain before and after an observation, whereas
\par
Macedo and Cardoso [2001] proposed another measure using improbability.
\par
3.2.1. Prediction-Error Based Methods. Schmidhuber [1991a] introduced artificial curios-
\par
ity into model-building control systems. The goal of such a control system is to learn the
\par
input-output mapping in a noisy environment. Curiosity is infused to give the control
\par
system an intrinsic desire to improve the model's knowledge about the world. This is
\par
realized by introducing an additional reinforcement unit on top of the controller, which
\par
rewards actions that cause high prediction errors. The prediction error inherently mea-
\par
sures the degree of mismatch between belief and reality, which is in line with the first
\par
interpretation of surprise. In Step 1, the stimulation value is determined by surprise
\par
(prediction error), and it is directly used as the curiosity value (in Step 2) to form de-
\par
cisions (on rewarding the system). This mechanism (rewarding the system with high
\par
surprise/curiosity) encourages certain past actions to be carried out again in order to
\par
repeat situations similar to the mismatched ones. Hence, the system will always direct
\par
its attention toward something that is unknown and therefore avoid boredom. This
\par
working mechanism only supports AoB in stimulus selection. Barto's theory of intrin-
\par
sically motivated reinforcement learning is implemented based on a similar principle
\par
[Barto et al. 2004; Singh et al. 2004].
\par
In a later variation, Schmidhuber [1999, 2002] worked on exploring the space of
\par
general algorithms that can automatically create predictable internal abstractions of
\par
complex spatiotemporal events. In this model, both AoB and AoA are accommodated.
\par
Curiosity is interpreted as the ability of the system to focus on interesting things by
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:13
\par
losing interest in the overly predictable (boring) or the overly unpredictable (anxiety
\par
inducing) aspects of the world. To achieve this goal, the system is realized by two
\par
intrinsically motivated agents playing zero-sum games. The two agents can bet in
\par
advance on whether a prediction is true or false. If they bet on different outcomes, the
\par
system will check who is right. The winner gets rewarded by receiving the other's bids,
\par
whereas the loser loses its bids due to surprise (error in prediction). Hence, both agents
\par
are motivated to lure the opponent into agreeing on computation sequences that will
\par
surprise the other one. However, a surprised module will eventually adapt and in turn
\par
will cause the other agent to lose a source of reward. In this way, the system as a whole
\par
is motivated to shift the exploration focus and reveal the unknown yet predictable
\par
regularities.
\par
Another example of prediction-error based implementation of surprise is given by
\par
U \textasciibreve{} gur et al. [2007]. In situations where a robot physically interacts with the environ-
\par
ment to explore and learn, assuming the traditional reinforcement learning method
\par
is applied, the robot may require a large number of interactions to learn even simple
\par
tasks. This could eventually damage the robot. To address this problem, U \textasciibreve{} gur adopted
\par
a Support Vector Machine (SVM) to learn the perception and action mapping in the
\par
robot, where curiosity is introduced to select interesting training data for SVM to re-
\par
duce the total number of training data required. In Step 1, the stimulation level is
\par
determined by surprise, which is measured by a sample's distance to the hyperplane
\par
(which separates two classes) in the feature space. In Step 2, the stimulation value
\par
is directly used as curiosity value for decision making. The system supports AoB be-
\par
cause it can be driven away from boredom: only if the distance is smaller than a fixed
\par
threshold (curiosity value is high) is the sample considered interesting and sent for
\par
learning.
\par
3.2.2. Probability Based Methods. An extension of Schmidhuber's curiosity principle to
\par
nondeterministic environments was done by Storck et al. [1995]. The goal of their
\par
system is to learn the model of a nondeterministic Markov environment, where each
\par
state-action pair (S(t),a(t)) may result in different next states (S(t+1)) probabilistically.
\par
The goal of introducing curiosity into this learning system is to actively search for
\par
interesting training examples that can maximize expected learning improvement. In
\par
Step 1, the stimulation value is determined by surprise, which reflects ``the degree of
\par
not expecting something.'' They adopt a probabilistic way of measuring surprise, which
\par
is the Kullback-Leibler distance between the agent's belief distribution before and after
\par
making an observation. This value of information gain reflects the learnability of the
\par
exploration space. Exploration areas where little information gain can be achieved
\par
are either too predictable (well learned) or too unpredictable (inherently unlearnable).
\par
Hence, the information gain (surprise) accommodates both AoB and AoA in stimulus
\par
selection. In Step 2, the surprise value is directly used as the curiosity value, which acts
\par
as rewards for the reinforcement learning of state-action pairs. In this way, curiosity
\par
drives the system away from the exploration space where little information gain can
\par
be achieved---that is, areas that are either too boring or anxiety inducing.
\par
Macedo and Cardoso [1999, 2001, 2004, 2005], mentioned in Section 3.1.1, also mod-
\par
eled human forms of surprise in artificial agents. Surprise serves as an intrinsic drive to
\par
direct the agent's exploration in an unknown environment. Their model relies on graph-
\par
based mental representations of objects, where each object is considered as a stimulus.
\par
The surprise level is defined by the degree of not expecting a stimulus (object) and
\par
is implemented as the improbability of existence of the stimulus. The improbability
\par
is evaluated based on probability theory: the degree of expecting an event X is given
\par
by its probability P(X), and accordingly, the improbability of X, denoted by 1- P(X),
\par
defines the degree of not expecting X (surprise). In the case of an object Objk comprising
