\textbf{3.3. Models Based on Change}
\par
In robot sensory-motor learning, Karaoguz et al. [2011] proposed a model of curiosity
\par
to direct the robot's attention to changes in the environment. The aim of the model is
\par
not to measure the amount (or level) of changes in a stimulus, but rather to focus on
\par
how to react when changes occur. Hence, the system does not provide evaluation for the
\par
level of stimulation or the level of curiosity (induced by change) but offers mechanisms
\par
to redirect its attention to changes.
\par
For the robot, the focus of attention is determined by a Gaussian distribution that
\par
governs the sampling of training examples over the laser motor space. The center of the
\par
Gaussian distribution is determined by the mean value of the last N samples added to
\par
the mapping, and it directs the attention of the robot to areas where new samples have
\par
been recently added. The spread of Gaussian distribution is related to the performance
\par
(success rate) of the mapping by
\par
{$\sigma$}(t,sr )= {$\sigma$}0 + kbtb + kf (tf- sr )2
\par
, (8)
\par
where {$\sigma$}0 is the baseline, tb is the number of timesteps since the last new sample was
\par
added, kb is the boredom coefficient, tf is the failure threshold, sr is the success rate,
\par
and kf is the failure coefficient.
\par
It can be seen from Equation (8) that the attention spread is inversely correlated
\par
to the success rate. For example, the attention spread is wide when the success rate
\par
is low due to a high value of kf (tf- sr )2. This model can successfully redirect the
\par
robot's attention toward changes, because when a change happens, tb will be reset
\par
to 0 and the high past success rate of that region will result in a narrow Gaussian
\par
distribution of samples. Newly added links in unlearnable areas (noisy region) will fail
\par
on retest, which can drive the success rate for that region below the failure threshold
\par
tf and increase the width of sampling distribution, redirecting the system away from
\par
that region. Hence, the system has a drive toward regions where existing mappings
\par
are not making accurate predictions (AoB) and a drive away from regions where no
\par
improvements can be obtained (AoA).
\par
From the perspective of machine learning, one can argue that this model is a special
\par
case of earlier models based on predictors [Schmidhuber 1991a, 1991b], because a pre-
\par
dictor always predicts that the next observation is similar to the current observation,
\par
which indicates no change. Once change is present, the predictor makes an error and
\par
updates its knowledge about the world.
\par
\textbf{3.4. Models Based on Uncertainty}
\par
Uncertainty arises when there is no clear response to a stimulus [Berlyne 1960]. The
\par
entropy in information theory has been proposed to measure the degree of uncer-
\par
tainty in a stimulus. This measure has also been employed very often in computational
\par
implementations to realize uncertainty-based curiosity [Macedo and Cardoso 2005].
\par
However, when a stimulus is difficult to classify, entropy can hardly be used. In a
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:15
\par
virtual learning environment, Wu et al. [2012] proposed a rough measure of uncer-
\par
tainty in a stimulus (learning object) by counting the number of uncertain elements.
\par
3.4.1. Information-Theory Based Methods. To improve an agent's exploration in unknown
\par
environments, Macedo and Cardoso [2005] introduced a measure of uncertainty, on
\par
top of novelty [Macedo and Cardoso 1999, 2001, 2004], into their model of curiosity.
\par
Based on the same system setup as presented in Section 3.1.1, Macedo and Cardoso
\par
[2005] argued that the desire to know or learn an object can be induced by both novelty
\par
and uncertainty---that is, objects with at least some parts that are not yet known.
\par
Each object (stimulus) can contain known parts (without uncertainty) and uncertain
\par
parts. The known parts of an object are used to measure novelty by Hamming Distance
\par
(introduced in Section 3.1.1), whereas uncertainty is measured by the entropy of all
\par
uncertain parts, including analogical and propositional descriptions of the physical
\par
structure, and functions of the object. In Step 1, the stimulation value is determined by
\par
the aggregation of novelty and uncertainty. In Step 2, the stimulation value is directly
\par
used as curiosity value. The system adopts AoB in stimulus selection and chooses
\par
objects with highest curiosity value to explore.
\par
3.4.2. Similarity Based Methods. As mentioned in Section 3.1.2, Wu et al. [2012] also
\par
considered uncertainty in their model of curiosity. For the virtual learning companion,
\par
uncertainty arises when it cannot verify a knowledge point (given by the user) with
\par
the expert knowledge. Hence, in Step 1, uncertainty is an integer calculated by using
\par
the world knowledge (expert knowledge) as reference and counting the number of
\par
knowledge points that are present in the user knowledge but are not present in the
\par
world knowledge. In Step 2, they assumed a positive correlation between stimulation
\par
and curiosity. The level of curiosity is set to be positively proportional to uncertainty
\par
(specified by the weight in the FCM-based approach).
\par
\textbf{3.5. Models Based on Conflict}
\par
Conflict arises when a stimulus tends to induce two or more incompatible responses
\par
in an organism [Berlyne 1960]. For the virtual learning companion, Wu et al. [2012]
\par
defined conflict as the incompatibility between the human learner's understanding and
\par
the expert knowledge embedded in the virtual world. If a relationship from concept Ci
\par
to concept C j in the user knowledge has a different label with the relationship from
\par
Ci to C j in the world knowledge, then the user's understanding of this relationship
\par
is in conflict with that of the expert. Hence, the measurement of conflict involves a
\par
comparison between the user knowledge and the world knowledge and is an integer
\par
calculated by counting the number of knowledge points having conflicting labels in
\par
both knowledge bases. In Step 1, the level of conflict in a virtual object (stimulus) is
\par
defined by the total number of conflicting relationships. In Step 2, the level of curiosity
\par
is positively correlated to conflict (specified by the weight in the FCM-based approach).
\par
\textbf{3.6. Models Based on Complexity}
\par
In machine learning models of curiosity, complexity has been associated with the pre-
\par
dictive power of predictive systems or compressive power of data compression systems
\par
[Li and Vitanyi 2008; Schmidhuber 1991b, 2006; Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004]. In virtual
\par
learning environments, complexity has been interpreted as how difficult a topic is to a
\par
student [Wu et al. 2012].
\par
3.6.1. Prediction-Improvement Based Methods. In Section 3.2.1, we introduced Schmidhu-
\par
ber's early ideas on implementing curiosity by rewarding the system proportionally to
\par
surprise---that is, prediction error [Schmidhuber 1991a]. However, this implementa-
\par
tion only succeeded in guiding the system to avoid boredom (i.e., well-learned area) but
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:16 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
not anxiety (i.e., inherently unpredictable area caused by noise). Later, Schmidhuber
\par
refined the model to accommodate both AoB and AoA. He defined curiosity as a simple
\par
principle: to learn a mapping from actions to the expectation of future performance
\par
improvement [Schmidhuber 1991b, 1991c]. Instead of pure prediction error, reward
\par
is now generated according to the controller's prediction improvement (i.e., change in
\par
prediction error). In Step 1, the complexity of data to be learned by the system (e.g., fa-
\par
miliar data that are easy to learn or noises that are too difficult to learn) is determined
\par
by prediction improvement, which supports both AoB and AoA in stimulus selection.
\par
The prediction improvement is obtained by a confidence module, which evaluates the
\par
reliability of a prediction and can be realized by probability-based or error-based meth-
\par
ods. In Step 2, the stimulation value is directly used as curiosity value, which is adopted
\par
as intrinsic rewards for learning. In this way, the controller will choose actions (based
\par
on the delayed reward) to deliberately select training examples with easily learnable
\par
regularity. Hence, the infusion of curiosity can direct the system's attention away from
\par
the exploration space that is either too predictable or too unpredictable.
\par
Schmidhuber [2006, 2009a, 2009b] formed his formal theory of creativity by general-
\par
izing the simple principle of curiosity from predictors to data compressors. A ``beautiful''
\par
sensory data is one that is simple yet has not been fully assimilated by the adaptive
\par
observer, which is still learning to compress data better. The agent's goal is to create
\par
action sequences that can extend the observation history to yield previously unpre-
\par
dictable but quickly learnable algorithmic regularities. In other words, it is looking for
\par
data with high compressibility (reflected by curiosity value).
\par
Schmidhuber's implementation of prediction improvement in nature is a comparison
\par
of prediction error between situations that are successive in time. This principle allows
\par
robots to avoid long periods of time in front of a television with white noise (completely
\par
unlearnable situations) because the error in prediction will remain large and the robots
\par
will be bored due to little prediction improvement. However, the principle is not robust
\par
enough in the alternation of completely predictable and unpredictable situations, be-
\par
cause robots can get stuck here due to large prediction improvement. To cope with such
\par
problems, Oudeyer and Kaplan [2004, 2005] refined Schmidhuber's simple principle.
\par
Instead of comparing the prediction error between situations that are successive in
\par
time, compare the prediction error between situations that are similar. They proposed
\par
a model of curiosity that allows a robot to group similar situations into regions where
\par
comparison between situations is meaningful. The learning space is divided into re-
\par
gions, and each region has an expert to make local predictions. Each expert computes
\par
the prediction improvement (curiosity value) locally and rewards its state-action pairs
\par
according to the prediction improvement. This works well in practice to handle prob-
\par
lems such as robots stuck in situations with completely predictable and unpredictable
\par
sample data in alternation.
\par
Another variation of using prediction improvement as curiosity drive has been pro-
\par
posed by Pang et al. [2009]. This model is rooted in incremental Linear Discriminant
\par
Analysis (LDA). Here, curiosity is measured as the discriminability difference (residue)
\par
between the LDA-transformed space and the original space. The infusion of curiosity
\par
can help the system actively search for informative examples to learn and improve the
\par
performance using fewer instances.
\par
3.6.2. Similarity Based Methods. In the model built by Wu et al. [2012], complexity is
\par
interpreted as the difficulty of a virtual object (stimulus) to the learner. The more
\par
knowledge points contained in a virtual object that are not new to a learner, the more
\par
complex (or difficult) it is to the learner. Hence, the complexity for a virtual object is an
\par
integer measured by the number of knowledge points (related to the virtual object) in
\par
world knowledge that have not been learned by the learner. In Step 2, Wu et al. [2012]
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:17
\par
assumed a positive correlation between stimulation and curiosity. Complexity, novelty
\par
(Section 3.1.2), uncertainty (Section 3.4.2), and conflict (Section 3.5) are aggregated by
\par
an FCM-based approach to calculate the level of curiosity. Once the learner is detected
\par
to be in a state of being lost, the agent will calculate a curiosity value for each virtual
\par
object in the learner's vicinity in the virtual world and prompt the learner to explore
\par
the virtual object with the highest curiosity value (potentially most interesting), which
\par
only considers AoB in stimulus selection.
\par
\textbf{3.7. Discussion}
\par
An interesting point worth noting is that from the machine learning perspective, there
\par
are certain recurring principles underlying curiosity-inspired algorithms. The first
\par
group of recurring principles includes generating intrinsic curious rewards based on
\par
errors [Schmidhuber 1991a; U \textasciibreve{} gur et al. 2007] or Shannon's information [Scott and
\par
Markovitch 1989]. This group of principles can redirect learning to focus on the un-
\par
known. However, they fail to distinguish noise from the novel and learnable regular-
\par
ities. The second group of recurring principles includes generating intrinsic curious
\par
rewards based on error reduction [Schmidhuber 1991b; Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004], in-
\par
formation gain [Storck et al. 1995], or compression improvement [Schmidhuber 2006].
\par
This group of principles effectively addresses the problem mentioned previously. They
\par
are able to guide learning to focus on easily learnable regularities and at the same time
\par
filter out noise. The second group of principles helps form the basis of Schmidhuber's
\par
theory of artificial curiosity, which shows success in speeding up learning and building
\par
unsupervised developmental systems [Schmidhuber 1991b]. In addition, Schmidhuber
\par
believes that these principles make machines ``creative'' and intrinsically motivate ma-
\par
chines to create action sequences that make data interesting, which forms the basis of
\par
his theory of artificial creativity [Schmidhuber 2006].
\par
\textbf{4. SUMMARY OF RESEARCH ISSUES IN COMPUTATIONAL CURIOSITY}
\par
Four key research issues are identified that deserve to be explored further to enhance
\par
the theoretical models of computational curiosity. These research issues are mainly
\par
concerned with designing agents that can demonstrate curiosity and perceive the level
\par
of curiosity exhibited by human beings.
\par
\textbf{4.1. Evaluation of Stimulation}
\par
Most of the current computational models of curiosity adopt a heuristic approach for
\par
the evaluation of stimulations. This is often done based on algorithmic characteristics
\par
and goals in machine learning. These models advance the performance of machine
\par
learning systems in learning and exploration. However, they have difficulties support-
\par
ing humanoid agents to evaluate curiosity stimuli in complex environments such as
\par
computer-based education, e-commerce, and teleconference. Some models have started
\par
from a psychological theory and evaluated stimulation levels based on one collative
\par
variable or a subset of collative variables. As of yet, how the collative variables affect
\par
the level of stimulation, either individually or collectively, has not been studied. Both
\par
qualitative and quantitative analysis of collative variables can help form a deeper un-
\par
derstanding of the working mechanism of computational curiosity and provide a clear
\par
picture of how collative variables are related to performance changes in intelligent
\par
agents.
\par
\textbf{4.2. Evaluation of Curiosity}
\par
Existing computational models mostly assume a positive correlation between stimu-
\par
lation and curiosity, which may not be always true in human beings. Studies have
\par
been done on simulating the Wundt curve to map the level of stimulation to the level
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:18 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
of curiosity, but the understanding of how this mapping affects the performance of
\par
human-like agents is still unclear. Moreover, in psychology, the curiosity zone is right
\par
next to the boredom zone and the anxiety zone. Hence, deeper studies are necessary to
\par
provide more proper mapping methods to allow human-like agents to avoid entering
\par
the boredom zone or the anxiety zone during exploration.
\par
\textbf{4.3. Curiosity-Based Decision Making}
\par
One important issue with computational curiosity is the risk management in curiosity-
\par
based decision making. As curiosity often leads to explorations, sometimes the agent or
\par
human being may be exposed to the possibility of being harmed or causing undesirable
\par
consequences to others. Hence, curious machines should operate under the protection
\par
of proper risk management systems so that they will not harm themselves or others.
\par
Ethical boundaries should also be defined for curious machines so that they will not
\par
intrude on the privacy of the users or other agents.
\par
\textbf{4.4. Recognition and Response to Users' Curiosity}
\par
Currently, computational curiosity mainly focuses on designing models for machines
\par
to demonstrate curiosity, which has been shown to be useful in improving their explo-
\par
ration and learning. Another key research direction for computational curiosity that
\par
has not been intensively studied is to understand the curiosity exhibited by the users.
\par
This topic is especially important for designing next-generation human-agent collec-
\par
tives in areas such as embodied conversational agents, computer-based education, and
\par
artificial companions. The ability of intelligent agents to recognize and respond to users'
\par
curiosity provides opportunities to enhance user experiences in human-computer in-
\par
teractions by avoiding boring and anxiety-inducing topics, or by stimulating users'
\par
curiosity appropriately. This research may also enrich the current understanding of
\par
human curiosity in psychology.
\par
\textbf{5. TEN RESEARCH AREAS THAT COMPUTATIONAL CURIOSITY WILL IMPACT}
\par
In this section, 10 important research areas where computational curiosity could play
\par
significant roles are identified. We believe that computational curiosity is a novel di-
\par
mension that can be infused into many important research areas and become a fertile
\par
ground for significant innovations.
\par
\textbf{5.1. Machine Learning}
\par
The close-knit relationship between curiosity and human learning has inspired many
\par
researchers to devise computational forms of curiosity for machine learning systems,
\par
with the expectation to enhance learning capability and potentially drive them to evolve
\par
into autonomous intelligent machines. The study of computational curiosity in machine
\par
learning systems has some overlap with other concepts, such as active learning and
\par
intrinsically motivated learning.
\par
Active learning, as the name suggests, deals with making machines ``active'' by
\par
allowing the learning system to select actions or make queries that influence what
\par
data is to be added into its training set [Cohn et al. 1996]. Active learning is especially
\par
useful when data are expensive or difficult to obtain. Curiosity can play a critical role
\par
in active learning by helping the system to determine which data are interesting. For
\par
example, Scott and Markovitch [1989] introduced a curiosity drive into supervised
\par
learning systems to actively select the most informative samples for learning. Here,
\par
the system is continually directed toward regions with highest uncertainty, which is
\par
also a general principle followed by many other active learning algorithms [Fedorov
\par
1972]. U \textasciibreve{} gur et al. [2007] infused curiosity into an SVM-based learning system to select
\par
interesting training data, which significantly reduces the number of training samples
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:19
\par
required to learn. Similarly, Pang et al. [2009] introduced curiosity into an LDA-based
\par
learning system.
\par
Intrinsically motivated learning advocates the development of intrinsic motivations
\par
for learning systems to achieve task-independent learning [Barto et al. 2004; Singh
\par
et al. 2004] or autonomous development [Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004]. These learning
\par
approaches are gaining increasing popularity among artificial intelligence researchers
\par
[Baldassarre 2011]. Intrinsically motivated learning often takes root in a reinforcement
\par
learning framework, where intrinsic motivations act as intrinsically generated rewards
\par
that are to be maximized. In human psychology, curiosity is known to be one of the most
\par
important intrinsic motivations related to learning. Hence, curiosity has often been
\par
adopted in intrinsically motivated learning algorithms. For example, Barto et al. [2004]
\par
used Berlyne's theory as the psychological foundations to develop his intrinsically
\par
motivated learning algorithm. Schmidhuber [1991b, 1999, 2009b] introduced artificial
\par
curiosity as intrinsic rewards for general learning algorithms. Oudeyer and Kaplan
\par
[2004] proposed an intelligent adaptive curiosity mechanism for intrinsically motivated
\par
robots.
\par
\textbf{5.2. Robotics}
\par
The role of curiosity in attention focus, self-development, and autonomous learning has
\par
attracted intense attention in the field of robotics [Oudeyer and Kaplan 2004; U \textasciibreve{}
\par
gur
\par
et al. 2007; Merrick et al. 2008; Karaoguz et al. 2011].
\par
With the attempt to design robots that can autonomously self-develop in a progressive
\par
manner, Oudeyer and Kaplan [2004] devised for them a mechanism that resembles
\par
human curiosity. This mechanism acts as an intrinsic motivation to motivate robots
\par
to explore into regions with new knowledge [Oudeyer and Kaplan 2007] and endows
\par
them with the ability to adapt to new environments without prior knowledge or manual
\par
adjustment. With a similar goal of designing robots that can self-develop without an
\par
explicit teacher, Ngo et al. [2012] applied Schmidhuber's principle of curiosity into a
\par
robot arm that enables robots to learn skills through playing. Pape et al. [2012] applied
\par
the same into a biomimetic robot finger for learning tactile skills.
\par
Traversibility affordance refers to the ability of robots to navigate through an en-
\par
vironment with obstacles. This ability highly depends on the robot's current location,
\par
orientation, and the shape of objects in the environment. In situations where robots
\par
physically interact with the environment to explore and learn, assuming traditional
\par
reinforcement learning methods are applied, even simple tasks such as avoiding ob-
\par
jects may require a large number of trials. This increases the risk of the robot being
\par
damaged during the exploration. To address this problem, U \textasciibreve{} gur et al. [2007] simulated
\par
curiosity in robots to select informative training samples, which can significantly re-
\par
duce the number of interactions required with minimal degradations in the learning
\par
process.
\par
Another problem in robotics is self-directed reconfiguration. Reconfigurable robots
\par
can rearrange their modules to achieve different structures, behaviors, and functions.
\par
Instead of looking into how robots can adapt in an unstructured environment, recon-
\par
figurable robots focus on adaptation to changes in their own structures and changes
\par
of goals when the actuator or effector of the robot changes. Merrick and Huntington
\par
[2008] introduced curiosity into reconfigurable robots to select informative samples to
\par
learn so that with fewer interactions, robots can still achieve better learning outcomes.
\par
One of the most important sensory-motor problems for robots when interacting with
\par
an environment is to learn a mapping from the gaze space (the location of an object) to
\par
the reach space (the movement of arms to grasp the object) in response to changes in
\par
the environment (camera replacement or changes in the physical environment) without
\par
manual recalibration of the hardware. To address this problem, Karaoguz et al. [2011]
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:20 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
devised a mechanism of curiosity that drives the exploration into learning spaces where
\par
a proper level of complexity is associated with a particular level of capability. With this
\par
mechanism, robots can concentrate on highly interesting areas that are neither fully
\par
explored nor pure noise. In addition, the mechanism can successfully direct robots'
\par
attention to regions where changes have occurred.
\par
Exploration in extreme environments can be a dangerous task for humans. Artificial
\par
agents, especially in the form of robots, have been a good substitute for humans to
\par
undertake such tasks. Exploration of unknown environments has been an active re-
\par
search field in domains such as planetary exploration, meteorite searches in Antarctic,
\par
volcano exploration, and map building of interiors. [Moorehead et al. 2001; Burgard
\par
et al. 2002; Macedo and Cardoso 2005]. In human beings, exploration is often driven
\par
by curiosity, a motivating force for attention focus, determination of interest, and gath-
\par
ering knowledge. Based on these observations, researchers devised artificial forms of
\par
curiosity for agent decision making in unknown environment. For example, Macedo
\par
and Cardoso [2001, 2002, 2005] modeled human forms of surprise and curiosity in
\par
case-based reasoning frameworks to guide the agent's exploration in unknown envi-
\par
ronments populated with objects. Graziano et al. [2011] also discussed the application
\par
of computational curiosity to solve autonomous exploration problems.
\par
To summarize, computational curiosity has the potential to contribute to various as-
\par
pects of robotic systems, including selective attention and skill development. Numerous
\par
studies on computational curiosity are continuously emerging in this field [Stojanov
\par
et al. 2006; Macedo 2010; Oudeyer 2012].
\par
\textbf{5.3. Artificial Creativity}
\par
Computational creativity explores the possibility of machines capable of generating
\par
creative artifacts that are commonly defined as being previously unknown, useful, and
\par
surprising [Boden 2009]. Computational curiosity has been studied in machines to
\par
demonstrate creative behaviors.
\par
Based on Csikzentmihalyi's system view of creativity, Saunders [2007] postulated two
\par
levels of creativity: the individual level and the society level. According to Saunders,
\par
there are two questions to be answered at the individual level of creativity: (1) how
\par
to evaluate creativity, and (2) how to produce creativity. Saunders and Gero [2004]
\par
argued that curiosity can be used to guide problem solving by finding interesting
\par
design solutions as well as discovering interesting design problems. Curious design
\par
agents were proposed to evaluate the interestingness (creativity) of designs based on
\par
novelty. Research of curiosity at the society level looks into the sociocultural influence
\par
on creativity and the acceptance of creative works by other individuals. Based on
\par
previous works, Saunders [2011] studied the society level of creativity by creating a
\par
virtual society populated with curious design agents. Simulation results showed that
\par
the artificial society exhibited certain similar behaviors as in real human societies.
\par
Schmidhuber [2006, 2007, 2009b] explored the relationship between creativity,
\par
artists, humor, and fun. He argued that art and creativity can be seen as the by-products
\par
of curiosity rewards. He further argued that the optimal curious reward framework can
\par
be sufficiently formal and precise to allow the implementation on computers and devel-
\par
opmental robots. Schmidhuber [2006] generalized his simple principle of curiosity to
\par
form artificial creativity: ``the current compressor of a given subjective observer tries to
\par
compress his history of acoustic and other inputs where possible'' and ``the compression
\par
progress becomes the wow-effect or intrinsic reward for the `creative' action selector,
\par
which is thus motivated to create more data that allows for more wow-effect.'' Later,
\par
Schmidhuber [2011] proposed a greedy but practical implementation of the basic prin-
\par
ciple of creativity: POWERPLAY, which can automatically invent or discover problems
\par
to train a general problem solver from scratch. In his survey, Schmidhuber [2009a]
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:21
\par
drew a comparison between his formal theory with less formal works in aesthetics
\par
theory and psychology.
\par
\textbf{5.4. Games}
\par
With the advances in computer technologies for graphics, processing power, and net-
\par
working, virtual worlds are emerging as platforms for massive online games. Merrick
\par
and Maher [2009] highlighted the need for new NPCs to cope with the increasing com-
\par
plexity and functionality of multiuser virtual worlds. They argued that the behaviorial
\par
traits of humans and animals generated by curiosity can also advance the performance
\par
of artificial agents when dealing with complex or dynamic environments, where only
\par
limited information is available or the information changes over time.
\par
To cope with the difficulty of predefining task specific rules or environment-specific
\par
motivation signals, Merrick et al. [2008] introduced a mechanism of curiosity into
\par
NPCs, which enables them to direct attention to relevant information and be curious
\par
about changes in the environment. Simulation results showed that the curious mo-
\par
tive led the NPCs to demonstrate higher variety and complexity in behavior patterns
\par
[Maher et al. 2008].
\par
Whereas most of the works that apply computational intelligence to games focused
\par
on the generation of behaviors, strategies, or environment, Togelius and Schmidhuber
\par
[2008] looked at the very heart of games: the rules that define a game. They proposed
\par
automatic game designs based on Koster's theory of fun and Schmidhuber's theory of
\par
artificial curiosity, where Schmidhuber's theory of curiosity is a coarse approximation
\par
of Koster's theory of fun [Koster 2005]---that is, a game is fun if it is learnable but
\par
not trivial. Li and Riedl [2010] proposed that plotlines in role-playing games can be
\par
adapted to elicit curiosity in order to improve the replay value of games, but they did
\par
not specify concrete mechanisms for modeling curiosity.
\par
\textbf{5.5. Artificial Intelligence in Education}
\par
Artificial intelligence enables the design of pedagogical agents that can play multiple
\par
roles such as tutors, tutees, or companions. These pedagogical agents are important in-
\par
gredients to form social learning environments in computerized teaching and learning
\par
environments.
\par
Pedagogical agents as tutors have been designed for achieving instructional functions
\par
[Johnson 2001]. Feedback from students plays an important role in the instruction pro-
\par
cess to help virtual tutors adapt instructional strategies. However, most of pedagogical
\par
agents are designed to collect static feedbacks at a particular time with prescribed
\par
questions. If a pedagogical agent is curious during the instruction process, then it
\par
may provide more wits by asking situational and personalized questions that can let
\par
students notice the weakness and conflicts in their own knowledge structures, which
\par
may induce students' curiosity.
\par
Another role often undertaken by pedagogical agents is that of tutee. The teachable
\par
agent is a special type of tutee agent [Blair et al. 2007]. The pedagogical meaning
\par
of such an agent is that ``when students teach, they tend to learn deeper.'' However,
\par
existing teachable agents are mostly passive learners who absorb whatever students
\par
feed them, which renders these agents less responsive during the collaborative learning
\par
process. Curiosity may make teachable agents become inquisitive in what the student
\par
teach and selective in topics based on their learned capability.
\par
In educational studies, it has been shown that curiosity is an important motive that
\par
drives self-direction and interests in learning. Stumm et al. [2011] attributed curiosity
\par
as the third pillar of academic performance, in addition to intelligence and conscien-
\par
tiousness. However, how to utilize computerized pedagogical techniques to stimulate
\par
students' curiosity is still open for discussion. Wu et al. [2012] have done some pilot
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.18:22 Q. Wu and C. Miao
\par
studies in this direction, in which they simulated curious learning companions that
\par
can make use of users' knowledge and environmental knowledge to detect potentially
\par
interesting learning objects for users. Later, they simulated a curious peer learner in
\par
virtual learning environments to mimic the behavior of a natural student [Wu and
\par
Miao 2013].
\par
\textbf{5.6. Affective Computing}
\par
Affective computing is computing that relates to, arises from, or deliberately influ-
\par
ences emotion or other affective phenomena [Picard 1997]. It requires multidisciplinary
\par
knowledge such as psychology, cognitive science, computer science, and engineering.
\par
Affective computing is gaining rapid popularity and has great potential in the next
\par
generation of human-computer interfaces. Curiosity is closely related to emotional
\par
constructs such as fear, pleasure, boredom, and anxiety. Computational curiosity offers
\par
a new dimension from which emotions can be appraised, apart from the consequences of
\par
events, the actions of agent, and the characteristics of objects [Ortony et al. 1988]. The
\par
consideration of computational curiosity in affective modeling is especially interesting
\par
in learning contexts and social contexts, where curiosity-related emotions significantly
\par
influence the believability and performance of emotional agents.
\par
\textbf{5.7. Artificial Companions}
\par
Artificial companions, designed to develop a close and long-term human-computer re-
\par
lationship, have emerged in the latter half of the 2000s. Two key words, close and long-
\par
term, have been guiding the development in this field. Researchers are working on the
\par
design of believable human-computer interfaces to provide close interactions (e.g., em-
\par
bodied conversational agent) and robust memory architectures to sustain long-term
\par
relationships [Bickmore and Picard 2005; Wilks 2010]. Computational curiosity can be
\par
an important dimension to be studied in artificial companions for enhancing both the
\par
closeness of interactions and the possibility for long-term relationships. The potential
\par
for computational curiosity in creating a closer human-computer relationship draws
\par
evidence from psychological findings that curiosity plays an important role in promot-
\par
ing the intimacy of interpersonal relationships in social context. A curious artificial
\par
companion can be more responsive, may infuse more novel twists of excitement into
\par
interactions, and might induce a natural flow of engagement between the interaction
\par
discourses. As for promoting long-term relationships, curiosity can be a motivational
\par
force to learn more about the partner and develop a historical knowledge base through
\par
interactions. A curious artificial companion may be more interested to know the part-
\par
ner, may be more inquisitive to novel changes of the partner. and may incorporate
\par
information of the partner into part of the cognitive development of the companion
\par
itself.
\par
\textbf{5.8. Persuasive Technology}
\par
Persuasive technology deals with the use of computing systems, devices, or applications
\par
to gradually change a person's attitudes or behavior [Fogg 2002]. This technology has
\par
the potential to bring constructive changes in health science, safety, and education.
\par
Examples include a digital doll to persuade kids to eat fruit and vegetables, and a vir-
\par
tual coach to persuade the elderly to exercise more [Fogg 1999]. Understanding users'
\par
curiosity and properly infusing curiosity stimuli into the human-computer interaction
\par
process can potentially help intelligent agents achieve persuasive goals. For example, if
\par
a sales agent can successfully elicit the customer's curiosity in a product, there will be
\par
a higher chance for this product to be sold. Curiosity has been harnessed to ``persuade''
\par
programmers to increase the correctness in end-user programming [Wilson et al. 2003].
\par
ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 46, No. 2, Article 18, Publication date: November 2013.Curiosity: From Psychology to Computation 18:23
\par
\textbf{5.9. Agent Negotiation}
\par
Negotiation is a process that involves two or more parties to reach an agreement.
\par
This mechanism has received increasing attention in multiagent systems for manag-
\par
ing interagent dependencies in real time [Jennings and Wooldridge 2002]. Traditional
\par
implementations of the negotiation process focused on its rational aspects to build
\par
consensus. Recently, Broekens et al. [2010] argued that negotiation is a multifaceted
\par
process in which affect plays an important role. Computational curiosity has the poten-
\par
tial to influence the human-agent negotiation process by promoting positive emotional
\par
states, responsiveness, and engagement. Enabling a negotiation agent to understand
\par
the curiosity exhibited by a user may allow it to notice the unusual, surprising, or con-
\par
flicting information offered by the user and reach agreements that are more socially
\par
optimal. A negotiation agent that can adapt its decision making based on the users'
\par
curiosity may improve its chance of gaining more utility out of the final agreement.
\par
\textbf{5.10. Trustworthy Computing}
\par
Another important issue in multiagent systems is trust management. It is useful in
\par
open and dynamic systems such as peer-to-peer systems, semantic Web, ad hoc net-
\par
works, and e-commerce [Ramchurn et al. 2004; Yu et al. 2010]. Similar to negotiation,
\par
trust management is also closely related to emotion states [Dunn and Schweitzer 2005;
\par
Schoorman et al. 2007]. The motivational role of curiosity in building interpersonal re-
\par
lationships can contribute to the trust building between strangers [Kashdan et al.
\par
2011]. Computational curiosity can potentially enhance an agent's judgment by mak-
\par
ing the agent more sensitive to novel, surprising, conflicting, and uncertain information
\par
presented in the environment.
