\par
Popul Ecol (2014) 56:41--53
\par
DOI 10.1007/s10144-013-0421-2
\par
S P E C I A L F E A T U R E : R E V I E W Mathematical Models for
\par
Effective Environmental Management
\par
Decision science for effective management of populations subject
\par
to stochasticity and imperfect knowledge
\par
Hiroyuki Yokomizo Shaun R. Coutts
\par
Hugh P. Possingham
\par
Received: 9 May 2013 / Accepted: 4 November 2013 / Published online: 5 December 2013
\par
\'O The Society of Population Ecology and Springer Japan 2013
\par
Abstract Many species are threatened by human activity
\par
through processes such as habitat modification, water man-
\par
agement, hunting, and introduction of invasive species.
\par
These anthropogenic threats must be mitigated as efficiently
\par
as possible because both time and money available for mit-
\par
igation are limited. For example, it is essential to address the
\par
type and degree of uncertainties present to derive effective
\par
management strategies for managed populations. Decision
\par
science provides the tools required to produce effective
\par
management strategies that can maximize or minimize the
\par
desired objective(s) based on imperfect knowledge, taking
\par
into account stochasticity. Of particular importance are
\par
questions such as how much of available budgets should be
\par
invested in reducing uncertainty and which uncertainties
\par
should be reduced. In such instances, decision science can
\par
help select efficient environmental management actions that
\par
may be subject to stochasticity and imperfect knowledge.
\par
Here, we review the use of decision science in environmental
\par
management to demonstrate the utility of the decision sci-
\par
ence framework. Our points are illustrated using examples
\par
from the literature. We conclude that collaboration between
\par
theoreticians and practitioners is crucial to maximize the
\par
benefits of decision science's rational approach to dealing
\par
with uncertainty.
\par
Keywords Adaptive management Information-
\par
gap decision theory Monitoring Stochastic
\par
dynamic programming Uncertainty Value of
\par
information analysis
\par
This manuscript was submitted for the special feature based on a
\par
symposium in Chiba, Japan, held on 21 October 2012.
\par
H. Yokomizo (\&)
\par
Center for Environmental Risk Research, National Institute for
\par
Environmental Studies, Onogawa 16-2, Tsukuba 305-8506,
\par
Japan
\par
e-mail: hiroyuki.yokomizo@nies.go.jp
\par
S. R. Coutts
\par
NERP Environmental Decisions Hub, Centre for Biodiversity
\par
and Conservation Science, The University of Queensland,
\par
Brisbane, QLD 4072, Australia
\par
H. P. Possingham
\par
School of Biological Sciences, The University of Queensland,
\par
Brisbane, QLD 4072, Australia
\par
H. P. Possingham
\par
Department of Life Sciences, Imperial College London, Silwood
\par
Park, Ascot, Berkshire SL5 7PY, England, UK
\par
Introduction
\par
The number of species threatened with extinction has
\par
increased (Butchart et al. 2010) in recent years in response
\par
to factors such as climate change, invasive species, and
\par
habitat loss. Efficient reduction of the risks associated with
\par
these factors is essential because finances and time avail-
\par
able for risk management are limited. Moreover, optimal
\par
resource management must be ensured for the sustainable
\par
harvest of wild populations (e.g., fisheries) and the effec-
\par
tive culling of overpopulated species such that the end
\par
result is a population size that falls within the desired range
\par
(e.g., Matsuda et al. 1999; Hauser et al. 2006).
\par
Making environmental decisions without considering all
\par
relevant factors and without the assistance of established
\par
decision science can lead to wasted resources, resulting in the
\par
conservation of fewer species than could have been achieved
\par
if the optimal set of actions had been taken (Joseph et al.
\par
2009). Decision science has been used in many population
\par
management problems, such as the conservation of
\par
12342 Popul Ecol (2014) 56:41--53
\par
endangered populations (Rout et al. 2007), control of inva-
\par
sive species (Yokomizo et al. 2009; Giljohann et al. 2011),
\par
and optimal harvesting of wild populations for use (e.g., food;
\par
Dorn 2002) or population control (i.e., culling; Hauser et al.
\par
2006). The oldest and most diverse suite of population
\par
management tools using decision science has been applied in
\par
fisheries management (Clark 1985; Gerber et al. 2005;
\par
Halpern et al. 2006). More recently, static pattern-based
\par
decision science problems have been developed extensively
\par
for the design of protected area systems, such as in the
\par
Marxan (Ball et al. 2009) and Zonation (Moilanen et al. 2009)
\par
suites. The latter suite typically ignores population and sys-
\par
tem dynamics. Previous approaches have tended to focus on
\par
economic and social considerations, as these invariably
\par
constrain or frame the optimization problem. Biological
\par
considerations, such as species ranges or species responses to
\par
protection of land, can be incorporated into these decision
\par
tools through consideration of their constraints and by using
\par
simple systems models such as species--area relationships
\par
(Murdoch et al. 2007).
\par
Decision science can assist in the selection of efficient
\par
and effective management strategies to maximize or min-
\par
imize the desired objective(s), even when existing knowl-
\par
edge of a stochastic system is imperfect. In particular, it is
\par
crucial to determine how much of a given budget should be
\par
invested in reducing uncertainty and which uncertainties
\par
should be prioritized for reduction. The mathematical tools
\par
of decision science provide rational and repeatable
\par
approaches to solving such problems (Wilson et al. 2009;
\par
McDonald-Madden et al. 2010a).
\par
Decision science is a framework within which decision
\par
makers attempt to achieve explicitly stated objectives (Shea
\par
et al. 1998) and where the optimal decision can be derived
\par
according to the following seven steps (Possingham et al.
\par
2001): (1) specify the management objectives, (2) list the
\par
possible management actions, (3) specify the variables that
\par
describe the state of the system being managed, (4) develop a
\par
model of the system, (5) specify constraints that bound the
\par
variables of decision and state, (6) specify the magnitude of
\par
uncertainty, and (7) find good solutions to the problem.
\par
Here, we review the use of decision science in envi-
\par
ronmental decision making with a specific focus on dealing
\par
with uncertainty. This review demonstrates the utility of
\par
the decision science framework for enhancing collabora-
\par
tion between modelers and practitioners to make the most
\par
of powerful tools for dealing with uncertainty.
\par
Decision science for maximizing the effectiveness
\par
of management
\par
The first step in deriving optimal management actions in
\par
decision science is specification of the management
\par
objectives. These objectives reflect management goals such
\par
as reducing extinction risk, minimizing the range of an
\par
invasive species, or maximizing the mean rate of net rev-
\par
enue from a fishery. Success in achieving such objectives is
\par
typically measured by factors such as extinction probabil-
\par
ity, genetic variability, or population size. In each case,
\par
these measures can be assessed both spatially and tempo-
\par
rally in a variety of ways, e.g., by quantifying mean pop-
\par
ulation size, geometric mean population size, or the
\par
probability of the population size falling below a threshold.
\par
Setting clear objectives is important because the best
\par
course of action to achieve those objectives depends on the
\par
objectives themselves (e.g., Yokomizo et al. 2003a; Nich-
\par
olson and Possingham 2006).
\par
In cases where the relative desirability of different
\par
population sizes can be selected, the objective will be to
\par
manage the population such that it is likely to be at the
\par
most desirable level. For example, Hauser et al. (2006)
\par
monitored a red kangaroo (Macropus rufus) population for
\par
which an intermediate level of density is most desirable.
\par
For many large herbivores, overpopulation can present a
\par
hazard or affect ecosystem function; conversely, under-
\par
population can affect the success of the tourism and
\par
hunting industries (Mansson et al. 2011). Objectives can
\par
also combine different types of costs and outcomes. For
\par
example, if a value could be assigned to the loss of a
\par
species, the objective may be to minimize the net cost of
\par
management and the value of the species weighted by its
\par
risk of extinction (Yokomizo et al. 2003b, 2007).
\par
Multiple objectives can be set for any given problem;
\par
thus, care must be taken to ensure that the objectives
\par
provide an accurate representation of aspirations. For
\par
example, the optimal translocation strategy for the bridled
\par
nailtail wallaby (Onychogalea fraenata) has been shown to
\par
depend on whether the primary objective was maximiza-
\par
tion of long-term persistence or total population size at the
\par
end of the translocation program (Rout et al. 2007). Thus,
\par
multi-criteria decision analysis or trade-off curves are
\par
required in cases of multiple objectives (Drechsler 2004;
\par
Nicholson and Possingham 2007; Kangas et al. 2010;
\par
Runge et al. 2011).
\par
Decision science demands that a set of sensible potential
\par
management actions be defined; this corresponds to the
\par
second step of the decision science framework (list the
\par
possible management actions). Possible management
\par
actions can encompass means of allocating resources
\par
among multiple regions (Wilson et al. 2006; McDonald-
\par
Madden et al. 2008a), timing of management (Gerber et al.
\par
2005; Saphores and Shogren 2005; Regan et al. 2006), or
\par
different management methods (Regan et al. 2005; Duca
\par
et al. 2009). The costs of those actions and their effects on
\par
the objective are calculated through a model of the system.
\par
Then, the variables that describe the state of the system are
\par
123
\par
1438390x, 2014, 1, Downloaded from https://esj-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s10144-013-0421-2 by Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn, Wiley Online Library on [05/05/2023]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons LicensePopul Ecol (2014) 56:41--53 43
\par
specified, e.g., the population size of an endangered species
\par
or the range of an invasive species; this corresponds to the
\par
third step in the decision science framework. The devel-
\par
opment of a system model, the fourth step in the decision
\par
science framework, requires close collaboration between
\par
field ecologists and decision scientists; conversely, the
\par
previous steps require collaboration between decision
\par
makers (managers, community, government) and decision
\par
scientists.
\par
Optimal management maximizes or minimizes the
\par
agreed objectives under constraints such as limited budget,
\par
labor, and time. Social acceptability can also impose con-
\par
straints; for example, concerns over animal welfare may
\par
limit strategies available to reduce the abundance of an
\par
invasive species (Possingham et al. 2001). Lohr et al.
\par
(2013) found that reducing the impact of feral cats on a
\par
colony of wedge-tailed shearwaters (Puffinus pacificus)
\par
was cheaper and more effective when a catch and kill
\par
method was used than when the nonlethal but more socially
\par
acceptable trap--neuter---return method was used. It is the
\par
responsibility of society as a whole to balance its desire to
\par
protect native species with the morality of killing indi-
\par
viduals belonging to invasive species and the suffering
\par
incurred during that process. Moreover, it is important to
\par
specify all constraints (economic, moral, and practical) that
\par
bound the decision variables and state variables (i.e., the
\par
fifth step in the decision science framework). Objectives
\par
and constraints can often be switched. For example, the
\par
objective could be to maximize the number of species
\par
included in a reserve system under the constraint of a fixed
\par
budget; conversely, the objective could be to minimize the
\par
cost of the reserve system under the constraint that all
\par
species are included. These types of reserve selection
\par
problems can be solved efficiently using freely available
\par
conservation planning software such as Marxan (Ball et al.
\par
2009) and Zonation (Moilanen et al. 2009).
\par
Managing populations in a dynamic and uncertain world
\par
is difficult. Ideally, the exact state of the system requiring
\par
management and the exact future values of parameters
\par
under consideration (i.e., as a consequence of any suite of
\par
actions) would be known. However, this is often impossi-
\par
ble in environmental management owing to stochasticity
\par
and imperfect knowledge of parameter values. Multiple
\par
types of uncertainty affect the precision and accuracy of
\par
population estimates, and the importance of precise and
\par
accurate population estimates depends on the survey
\par
techniques used and the biological characteristics of the
\par
species or system under management (Nuno et al. 2013).
\par
At best, the mean and variance of future outcomes (such as
\par
extinction probability) can be estimated by incorporating
\par
uncertainty. These expected outcomes and the uncertainty
\par
surrounding them can aid in the selection of different
\par
management actions.
\par
Both stochasticity and imperfect knowledge of system
\par
states and parameters make it difficult to predict the status
\par
of a population precisely, but it is important to distinguish
\par
between them. Uncertainty is caused by a lack of the
\par
assessor's knowledge regarding the current state of the
\par
system or the structure and parameters of the system
\par
model, and can be reduced through research and monitor-
\par
ing. In contrast, stochasticity such as unpredictable envi-
\par
ronmental fluctuations and random catastrophes cannot be
\par
reduced by research or monitoring. A good management
\par
strategy should consider uncertainty caused by both sto-
\par
chasticity and imperfect knowledge of the population of
\par
interest.
\par
Invariably, decisions must be made based on imperfect
\par
information because the cost of improving relevant
\par
knowledge can be high in terms of lost opportunity
\par
(Grantham et al. 2009; Martin et al. 2012). Simply ignoring
\par
uncertainty can also lead to management failure (Burgman
\par
2005), because the optimal management actions obtained
\par
assuming no uncertainty can be very different from those
\par
deduced assuming uncertainty (e.g., Regan et al. 2005).
\par
The degree of uncertainty must also be considered, because
\par
(from a practical standpoint) different degrees of uncer-
\par
tainty are modeled differently (Halpern et al. 2006).
\par
Therefore, it is necessary to specify the range of uncer-
\par
tainty for all parameters (i.e., the sixth step in the decision
\par
science framework). In cases where describing the uncer-
\par
tainty around a state or parameter is difficult, tools such as
\par
the information-gap decision theory (info-gap) (see below)
\par
can help make robust decisions. Such decisions correspond
\par
to the last step in the decision science framework, which
\par
focuses on finding good solutions to the problem(s).
\par
Decision making under uncertainty
\par
Even with perfect knowledge of a population's vital rates
\par
and current state at one point in space and time, changes in
\par
the exact state of the population cannot always be predicted
\par
owing to the temporal and spatial stochasticity in these
\par
vital rates. Population viability analyses (PVA) commonly
\par
use simulation models to predict extinction probability for
\par
populations where vital rates are stochastic over time and
\par
space (McCarthy 1996). PVA can also be used to evaluate
\par
and compare conservation strategies (Duca et al. 2009).
\par
Stochastic models can provide the systems model
\par
describing the dynamics of a population in a more formal
\par
optimization of conservation strategy (Westphal et al.
\par
2003); however, this often requires that the model is rela-
\par
tively simple. For example, Duca et al. (2009) used PVA to
\par
determine the best actions to minimize the extinction risk
\par
of the white-banded tanager (Neothraupis fasciata) in
\par
protected areas of the Cerrado in central Brazil.
\par
