\par
Uncertainty must also be incorporated in decision making
\par
when management actions cannot be implemented imme-
\par
diately, because the status of a population could change
\par
before its management commences. Often, the best deci-
\par
sion does not change even when population model
\par
parameters are changed (McCarthy et al. 2003). Reducing
\par
uncertainty in model parameters in such cases would
\par
deliver limited benefits in the decision-making process.
\par
As with population management, there are ways in
\par
which decisions about the acquisition of reserves can
\par
incorporate uncertainty. Most conservation planning
\par
approaches assume that reserves can be implemented
\par
instantaneously. Meir et al. (2004) considered the optimal
\par
design of reserves to protect biodiversity, incorporating
\par
loss of biodiversity over time in unprotected areas and
\par
protection that is not implemented instantaneously. In a
\par
dynamic setting, where sites only become available over
\par
time, using a reserve system designed under the assumption
\par
that sites can be obtained simultaneously can constrain
\par
options and lead to many fewer species being protected
\par
than in a reserve system designed to reflect the dynamic
\par
nature of site availability (Meir et al. 2004).
\par
In some cases, uncertainty surrounding important rela-
\par
tionships can make any management action ineffective. In
\par
a model of invasive species management, Yokomizo et al.
\par
(2009) demonstrated that even optimal management is
\par
ineffective when an incorrect relationship is assumed
\par
between density and the economic impact caused by an
\par
invasive species.
\par
Decision making based on the current state
\par
of the system
\par
It is important to derive management actions based on the
\par
current state of a population and its trend. For example, if a
\par
population is rapidly going extinct, it may be crucial to act
\par
on uncertain information because doing nothing is no
\par
longer an option (Martin et al. 2012). Conversely, if a
\par
population appears to be recovering, effort can be invested
\par
in other species unless the recovery leads to overabundance
\par
and conservation conflicts (e.g., Hake et al. 2010).
\par
Management actions can affect populations for several
\par
years, and actions (where, in all cases, one possible action is
\par
to do nothing) in the present can constrain future options. For
\par
example, failure to control an invasive species when its
\par
population is small can make control efforts in the future
\par
prohibitively expensive (Lee et al. 2009). Hence, it is
\par
essential to make decisions that consider both present and
\par
future gain. The performance of myopic optimization, which
\par
makes the best decision by looking only one time step ahead,
\par
can be much worse than taking a long-term perspective.
\par
Stochastic dynamic programing (SDP) is used to derive
\par
optimal management strategies in a dynamic and uncertain
\par
world, integrating the costs and benefits of outcomes and
\par
actions far into the future. SDP requires the current state of
\par
the system to be determined by its previous state (Bellman
\par
1957; Mangel and Clark 1988); one way of modeling such
\par
a system stochastically is called a Markov process (see
\par
``Box 1''). SDP allows determination of the optimal action
\par
at the current time, considering all possible future states of
\par
the system and the consequences of any actions taken in the
\par
future. SDP assumes that perfect information on the current
\par
state of the system can be obtained. Conversely, Partially
\par
Observable Markov Decision Processes (POMDPs) can be
\par
applied when imperfect information on the current state of
\par
the system is available (see the section entitled ``Decision
\par
making for management and monitoring under uncertainty
\par
of current states''). SDP has been used to obtain optimal
\par
management strategies for invasive species (Firn et al.
\par
2008; Hauser and Possingham 2008; Baxter and Possing-
\par
ham 2011) and endangered populations (Yokomizo et al.
\par
2003a; Meir et al. 2004; Wilson et al. 2006; McDonald-
\par
Madden et al. 2008b). However, one of the main draw-
\par
backs of SDP is that its applications are limited to simple
\par
low-dimensional problems (Bellman 1957).
\par
Decision making under more severe uncertainty
\par
It has long been assumed that uncertainty regarding states
\par
and parameters can be described by probability distribu-
\par
tions. Selection of such statistical distributions is often
\par
conducted heuristically and without sufficient scientific
\par
basis, despite the importance of the type of distribution
\par
used. In maximizing management performance, it is nec-
\par
essary to estimate probability distributions reliably to cal-
\par
culate management performance. However, it is often
\par
difficult to estimate distributions with limited data. Thus, it
\par
has been argued that robustness rather than performance
\par
should be maximized when making decisions under
\par
uncertainty (Ben-Haim 2012).
\par
Info-gap was developed to make robust decisions under
\par
conditions of more severe uncertainty (Ben-Haim 2006).
\par
An info-gap model allows the selection of actions in cases
\par
where there is unbounded uncertainty regarding system
\par
parameters. The uncertainty around each parameter is
\par
bound by an interval, the width of which can be infinitely
\par
wide (i.e., unbounded). Rather than specifying the extent of
\par
uncertainty in parameters, info-gap theory allows selection
\par
of the action that delivers a predetermined acceptable
\par
outcome under the greatest level of uncertainty. Thus, info-
\par
gap decision making allows minimum requirements to be
\par
met robustly, rather than maximizing or minimizing indi-
\par
cators of management performance (see ``Box 2'').
\par
Info-gap has been applied to decision making in the
\par
conservation of species (Regan et al. 2005; McDonald-
\par
Madden et al. 2008c; Post van der Burg and Tyre 2011;
