\par
Yokomizo et al. 2013), design of protected areas or marine
\par
reserves (Halpern et al. 2006; Moilanen et al. 2006;
\par
Nicholson and Possingham 2007), management of invasive
\par
species (Carrasco et al. 2010; Yemshanova et al. 2010),
\par
introduction of commercial plants (Yokomizo et al. 2012),
\par
and climate adaptation policies (Wintle et al. 2011; Hall
\par
et al. 2012).
\par
The importation of commercial plant species is a good
\par
example of a problem where there is high uncertainty, and
\par
a robust (rather than `optimal') decision is highly desirable.
\par
Introduction of commercial plant species can be highly
\par
beneficial to society, although there are large risks with
\par
very high uncertainty surrounding the probability that the
\par
species will be harmful instead of beneficial. There are
\par
many examples in which species were introduced because
\par
they were initially thought to be beneficial but became
\par
invasive; such species now cause massive economic and
\par
environmental harm (e.g., African lovegrass, Eragrostis
\par
curvula, introduced into Australia; Firn 2009). Info-gap can
\par
be used to make a robust decision about the introduction of
\par
commercial plants and their containment, given uncertainty
\par
around the probability of escape, the cost of impact caused
\par
by that escape, and the cost efficiency of containment
\par
(Yokomizo et al. 2012).
\par
The info-gap decision making approach, as with other
\par
robust decision-making approaches, can deal with uncer-
\par
tainty in both parameter values and the model itself, the
\par
latter is known as structural uncertainty. For example,
\par
McDonald-Madden et al. (2008c) derived the most robust
\par
allocation of limited funds for multiple populations of
\par
endangered species using info-gap in a case where severe
\par
uncertainty existed regarding the relationship between
\par
money spent and extinction risk. Moreover, they showed
\par
that focusing available resources on a few populations
\par
could be the most robust allocation of limited funds, even
\par
when protecting all subpopulations is optimal, assuming
\par
that there is no structural uncertainty.
\par
Decision science considering reduction of uncertainty
\par
Optimal monitoring to improve knowledge of uncertain
\par
parameters
\par
Deciding exactly how much time and money should be
\par
spent reducing uncertainty by monitoring or research is an
\par
important issue in environmental risk management. The
\par
availability of relevant and accurate information allows
\par
selection of better strategies, avoiding overinvestment in
\par
populations that are unlikely to become extinct even
\par
without intervention and underinvestment in imperiled
\par
species when greater effort could greatly improve their
\par
chances of survival (e.g., Yokomizo et al. 2003b, 2004).
\par
However, time and resources cannot be spent on both
\par
monitoring and management. Because the mistakes of the
\par
past cannot be undone, the amount of time that should be
\par
spent on learning is a crucial factor in conservation
\par
research. Obtaining accurate information is often time
\par
consuming, particularly for information on trends, but the
\par
time and effort spent monitoring comes at the expense of
\par
direct action. Species have gone extinct due, in part, to
\par
delaying decisions to act until more was known about the
\par
decline in their populations (Martin et al. 2012). Con-
\par
versely, acting too soon with incomplete knowledge may
\par
result in selection of the wrong action (Taylor and Gerro-
\par
dette 1993). Grantham et al. (2009) revealed the costs of
\par
delaying conservation action to improve knowledge of the
\par
status of proteas and demonstrated how much time should
\par
be spent collecting data before protecting habitat, even
\par
when loss of habitat is continuing. The monitoring/direct
\par
action tradeoff is also crucial in the early stages of an
\par
invasion. Thus, when information on the distribution of an
\par
invasive species is lacking, it may be better to spend time
\par
mapping the population accurately, even if this diverts
\par
resources from direct action (Baxter and Possingham
\par
2011).
\par
The tradeoff may not always be between different types of
\par
conservation actions: it could be between an action and the
\par
benefit to the rest of society. Gerber et al. (2005) considered
\par
the optimal time period to ban fishing to determine how much
\par
of a marine area should be reserved given uncertainty
\par
regarding the mean growth rate of the harvested population.
\par
During the fishing ban, there is an opportunity cost to fish-
\par
ermen (i.e., for not fishing) that must be considered in addi-
\par
tion to the cost of monitoring; it is inadvisable to wait for
\par
perfect information in such instances.
\par
Resource allocation between monitoring and direct
\par
management is also an important issue that is yet to be
\par
resolved. If too many resources are used in monitoring,
\par
accurate information will be available for the monitored
\par
species but insufficient resources may be left to under-
\par
take any required actions. Conversely, underinvestment
\par
in monitoring may leave abundant resources to under-
\par
take direct action, although these resources may be used
\par
poorly owing to the lack of available information about
\par
the monitored species (Yokomizo et al. 2004). It may be
\par
best to omit monitoring entirely in some cases, e.g.,
\par
when the management action is clear, when there is no
\par
time to change management actions, or when the costs of
\par
monitoring outweigh the improvements in management
\par
that may be gained from the extra information (Field
\par
et al. 2004; McDonald-Madden et al. 2010b).
\par
Not all monitoring is equally important. Hauser et al.
\par
(2006) found that monitoring a population of red kan-
\par
garoo (Macropus rufus) was more important when no
\par
monitoring had been conducted for several years or when
