\par
of monitoring also decreases with time because the
\par
information gained in the early years is also useful for
\par
later years (Gerber et al. 2005; Bogich et al. 2008).
\par
However, continuous monitoring may be essential in
\par
cases where system parameters change continually.
\par
Additionally, monitoring is more useful when the infor-
\par
mation acquired can alter ground management, such as
\par
for harvest quotas (Hauser et al. 2006).
\par
Monitoring efforts are also important in determining
\par
when management can be stopped. For example, it is
\par
difficult to declare an invasive species eradicated without
\par
systematic surveys, which are expensive. Regan et al.
\par
(2006) discovered a simple rule of thumb for the optimal
\par
number of clear (i.e., no detections) surveys required to
\par
declare the extinction of a species. This optimal number
\par
of absences minimizes the combination of the net
\par
expected cost of monitoring through time and the
\par
expected cost of escape and damage if eradication were
\par
declared too soon.
\par
In some cases, management efforts play a role in mon-
\par
itoring. Management efforts for invasive species can
\par
reduce uncertainty about the population size in addition to
\par
reducing the population size itself (D'Evelyn et al. 2008).
\par
For example, for control of the population size of deer in
\par
Japan, the population size was estimated by examining the
\par
response of the relative population size in each year to the
\par
known number of individuals that had been removed arti-
\par
ficially (Matsuda et al. 2002; Yamamura et al. 2008). In
\par
this case, the size of the deer harvest allowed the size of the
\par
population to be estimated more accurately.
\par
Surveillance, another form of monitoring, is not con-
\par
ducted with clear purposes and can be used to determine
\par
``unknown unknowns'' which can help avoid catastrophic
\par
outcomes (Wintle et al. 2010). For example, Wintle et al.
\par
(2010) derived a model to evaluate and compare the effi-
\par
ciency of investments in surveillance and targeted moni-
\par
toring. They found that investment in surveillance is
\par
defensible if it is more likely to discover unknown phe-
\par
nomena than targeted monitoring, and if the expected
\par
benefits of discovery by surveillance are much larger than
\par
those by targeted monitoring.
\par
Decision science for adaptive management
\par
Adaptive management deals with uncertainty around pre-
\par
dictions by continuing to monitor while management is
\par
underway, such that the level of belief in the models being
\par
used can be updated continuously (Walters 1986). Adap-
\par
tive management has been applied primarily in fisheries
\par
and the management of overpopulated species (e.g., Mat-
\par
suda et al. 2002; Gerber et al. 2007). An important part of
\par
adaptive management is the evaluation of management
\par
strategy, which employs a useful set of tools that enable
\par
comparison of the performance of multiple management
\par
strategies when making decisions under uncertainty (Chee
\par
and Wintle 2010; Bunnefeld et al. 2011).
\par
Adaptive management can be classified as passive or
\par
active (Probert et al. 2011). In passive adaptive manage-
\par
ment, learning is not included in the management plan.
\par
Conversely, learning is planned in active adaptive man-
\par
agement, and the extent to which information can be
\par
improved influences management actions. Active adaptive
\par
management balances the requirements of management
\par
with the need to learn about the system under manage-
\par
ment and contributes to making better decisions in the
\par
future. It is difficult to assess the benefits of management
\par
actions that accelerate information gain relative to the
\par
benefits of making the best management decisions given
\par
the available information at a specific time. However,
\par
decision science provides a transparent and rational
\par
framework for active adaptive management (Rout et al.
\par
2009).
\par
Decision making in active adaptive management
\par
based on the decision science framework has been
\par
developed in the fields of population management and
\par
conservation (McCarthy and Possingham 2007; Rout
\par
et al. 2009; McDonald-Madden et al. 2010a; Baxter and
\par
Possingham 2011). For example, Rout et al. (2009)
\par
derived the optimal translocation strategy for the bridled
\par
nailtail wallaby (Onychogalea fraenata) given uncer-
\par
tainty regarding population growth rates at a new site.
\par
There is a risk of losing translocated individuals at new
\par
sites if the population growth rates there are low.
\par
However, information about population growth rate
\par
cannot be obtained for new sites unless the species under
\par
consideration is actually translocated. Rout et al. (2009)
\par
demonstrated that it can be optimal to translocate indi-
\par
viduals to a site where the growth rate is suspected to be
\par
lower than that at the original site because there is a
\par
small chance that the new site may be more favorable
\par
(Rout et al. 2009).
\par
Decision making for management and monitoring
\par
under uncertainty of current states
\par
The previous section considered cases in which optimal
\par
management actions change depending on the state of the
\par
population. However, the exact state of the population
\par
(such as population size) is rarely known. In such cases,
\par
