\par
decisions must be made based on our belief regarding the
\par
current population size (or the overall state, where issues
\par
such as age and genetic structure are important). In these
\par
cases SDP cannot be used to derive optimal strategies
\par
because the exact state of the population is unknown.
\par
Rather, the POMDP can be used to optimize management.
\par
The POMDP is a procedure that makes optimal decisions
\par
dynamically based on assumptions about the current state
\par
(rather than exact state) of the population, assuming that
\par
the state of the population cannot be observed perfectly.
\par
POMDPs have been used to obtain optimal management
\par
strategies for several endangered species or natural
\par
resources (Yokomizo et al. 2004; Chades et al. 2008;
\par
McDonald-Madden et al. 2011; Williams 2011; Nicol and
\par
Chades 2012).
\par
Chades et al. (2008) applied the POMDP approach to
\par
the optimal conservation of the critically endangered
\par
Sumatran tiger (Panthera tigris sumatrae). According to
\par
this approach, managers were prompted to decide whether
\par
to continue management or conduct surveys to reduce
\par
uncertainty (regarding whether the species is present)
\par
after being unable to find the species in their management
\par
area for several years. Two population states were con-
\par
sidered in this approach: extinct or extant. Chades et al.
\par
(2008) discovered that managing a protected area for
\par
Sumatran tigers can be optimal, even if there is a com-
\par
paratively low chance the population still exists. If the
\par
species remains unseen, belief in the existence of the
\par
species eventually drops to such a low level that it
\par
becomes optimal to stop managing and shift efforts into
\par
surveying for Sumatran tigers. Eventually, in the absence
\par
of any record of a tiger, both monitoring and management
\par
should cease. The optimal timing of the shifts from
\par
managing to monitoring to abandonment depends on
\par
available estimates of detection, yearly local extinction
\par
probabilities, and the costs of different actions (Chades
\par
et al. 2008).
\par
Owing to computational limitations, POMDPs can only
\par
be applied when there are few discrete states. For example,
\par
investigation of the density of a population can consider
\par
classes of large, intermediate, and low density. Recently,
\par
Nicol and Chades (2012) used an algorithm known as the
\par
Continuous U-Tree (CU-Tree) algorithm to represent a
\par
continuous state space discretely using only the states
\par
necessary to discern between the effects of the manage-
\par
ment actions.
\par
Value of information theory
\par
In many cases, uncertainty remains regarding several
\par
aspects of a management problem. In such cases, it is
\par
important to consider which uncertainty is worth reducing
\par
in the context of making the best decision. If management
\par
actions do not change when the uncertainty regarding a
\par
parameter or process is reduced, there is no value in
\par
gaining that information because it cannot affect the
\par
actions taken (and, therefore, the outcome achieved). Value
\par
of information analysis evaluates which uncertainties affect
\par
management actions and by how much (Yokota and
\par
Thompson 2004; Runge et al. 2011; Moore and Runge
\par
2012), and calculates the expected value of perfect infor-
\par
mation (EVPI), the expected value of partial perfect
\par
information, or the expected value of sample information
\par
(also known as the expected value of imperfect informa-
\par
tion). The EVPI indicates the maximum benefit obtained by
\par
monitoring and is an indicator of whether more data should
\par
be gathered about a parameter or process. Conversely, the
\par
expected value of partial perfect information indicates the
\par
relative importance of reducing uncertainty across several
\par
different parameters. Because it is often difficult to obtain
\par
perfect information (even for one uncertain parameter), it is
\par
often necessary to use the expected value of sample
\par
information, which represents the management benefit
\par
derived from imperfect information. Hence, value of
\par
information analysis enables managers to target monitoring
\par
or research efforts at the most critical issues (Moore and
\par
Runge 2012). The mathematical explanation of value of
\par
information theory is shown in ``Box 3''.
\par
Moore et al. (2011) investigated whether eradication,
\par
containment, or taking no action was the best strategy for
\par
an invasive population of Acacia paradoxa in South Africa,
\par
where the current extent of the population is uncertain.
\par
Value of information analysis demonstrated that the cost of
\par
impact could be reduced by up to 8 \% if infestation size
\par
were known perfectly (Moore et al. 2011). In this case
\par
extra monitoring was not considered to be worthwhile
\par
because the cost of impact was found to be less than the
\par
cost of obtaining the information (Moore et al. 2011).
\par
Similarly, Runge et al. (2011) applied value of information
\par
analysis to management of the whooping crane (Grus
\par
americana). In this example, expert elicitation was used to
\par
develop preliminary predictions of management response
\par
under a series of hypotheses; then, the EVPI was used to
\par
measure how much management performance could be
\par
improved if different kinds of uncertainty were resolved.
\par
Discussion and conclusions
\par
We have demonstrated the utility of decision science for
\par
the management of populations when budgets are limited
\par
and uncertainty is present. Using the decision science
