\par
framework to turn conservation problems into mathemati-
\par
cal problems allows well-tested tools like SDP or POMDP
\par
to be used. This can be a daunting process, and it can be
\par
very difficult to get ecologists or managers to adopt deci-
\par
sion science. In practice, a wide range of knowledge and
\par
skills is required to conduct this process; thus, collabora-
\par
tion between ecologists/managers, policy makers, mathe-
\par
maticians, economists, and decision scientists is necessary
\par
to apply decision science in the real world.
\par
Multiple risk factors, such as habitat loss and invasive
\par
species, are involved when considering the protection of
\par
specific species or regions. Decision makers often consider
\par
these factors separately when determining policy. How-
\par
ever, once a management objective (such as maximizing
\par
the number of extant species) is set, it becomes obvious
\par
that objectives must be considered together to ensure
\par
effective management.
\par
More recently, conservation has targeted ecosystem
\par
function or ecosystem services rather than individual
\par
species or species diversity alone (e.g., Macfadyen et al.
\par
2012). Here, a decision-making approach that embraces
\par
uncertainty is even more important because the rela-
\par
tionship between management actions and improvements
\par
in ecosystem function is invariably poorly understood or
\par
quantified. Decision science can play an important role
\par
in effective decision making under such high
\par
uncertainty.
\par
Because human behavior is the driver of most extinc-
\par
tion, socioeconomic issues cannot be neglected in wildlife
\par
management. Coupling ecological and social dynamics
\par
emphasizes the importance of considering people's
\par
behavioral choices (Iwasa et al. 2007, 2010; Suzuki and
\par
Iwasa 2009a, b; Coutts et al. 2013; Halpern et al. 2013; Lee
\par
and Iwasa 2014). However, uncertainty remains regarding
\par
ecological dynamics, social dynamics, and the relation-
\par
ships between them (Schlu \textasciidieresis ter et al. 2012). In ecology, very
\par
little research has been conducted to investigate optimal
\par
management coupling ecological and social dynamics with
\par
a decision science framework, yet such research is essential
\par
to operationalize popular concepts such as resilience.
\par
Several strands of economic research, however, have
\par
adopted decision science to couple human desires and
\par
behaviors with simple ecological models. For example,
\par
more modern cost--benefit analysis that considers uncer-
\par
tainty over the future discounted benefit of a harvested
\par
population often favors sustainable harvesting over
\par
destructive exploitation (Ludwig et al. 2005).
\par
We have provided many examples of the utility of the
\par
decision science framework for effective population man-
\par
agement. To use decision science, it is essential to for-
\par
mulate mathematical models of the system under
\par
management and investigate how the system will respond
\par
to each management intervention. Because such systems
\par
are often stochastic, the best strategy can often be found
\par
only by using SDP, although it is often difficult to imple-
\par
ment the strategies found by SDP because they may
\par
involve rapid redeployment of effort. Nevertheless, the
\par
dynamic conservation management strategies produced by
