Box 2: Information-gap decision theory
\par
An information-gap model is an unbounded set of
\par
nested parameters that can be expressed by
\par
g g j j=g a, where a is the horizon of uncertainty
\par
and g is the best estimate of the uncertain parameter
\par
g. The range of parameter g becomes wider with
\par
increases in the horizon of uncertainty a (Fig. 2a).
\par
Information-gap models do not aim to maximize or
\par
minimize objectives but rather to maximize robust-
\par
ness to meet a minimum requirement. Assuming suf-
\par
ficient data are available, a probability distribution
\par
can be parameterized for each uncertain parameter.
\par
Then, an optimal management action can be chosen to
\par
maximize the expected performance measure based
\par
on the variance of these probability distributions
\par
(Fig. 2b). Conversely, when uncertainty is so severe
\par
that the degree of uncertainty cannot be specified,
\par
information-gap decision theory chooses the action
\par
that meets a minimum requirement under the largest
\par
horizon of uncertainty (Fig. 2c). In data-poor situa-
\par
tions, it is much easier to determine the minimum
\par
requirement than to specify the degree of uncertainty.
