\par
Statistical methods in
\par
experimentation recommendation
\par
models for discovering gene
\par
regulation pathways
\par
Changwon Yoo{$\ast$}
\par
In recent years, scientists and health professionals have become overwhelmed
\par
by the amount of genomic data available for epidemiological studies and patient
\par
care. In April 1996, the genome sequence of the yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae
\par
was completed. By December 1999, the first plant's complete genome sequence
\par
from Arabidopsis thaliana was published. The Human Genome Project and Celera
\par
completed a working draft reference of the human genome DNA sequence in June
\par
2000. Microarray and high throughput RNA sequence technology has opened a
\par
new era for gene expression studies. It enables us to measure the level of mRNA
\par
from different genes among different cells. Large microarray datasets provide
\par
opportunities to learn causal relationships among the genes, and compile in-depth
\par
knowledge of physiology of different diseases. We have to deal with many issues
\par
to achieve this, including:
\par
{$\bullet$} Validity of the microarray and high throughput RNA sequence measurements;
\par
{$\bullet$} Experimental design of gene expression studies;
\par
{$\bullet$} Analysis of gene expression experimental results.
\par
For meaningful interpretation of experimental results, we need to start
\par
with a sound experimental design. However, not most of the statistical methods
\par
concentrate on issues related to gene expression data analyses. They do not take
\par
into consideration experimental design of microarray studies. The main topic of
\par
this article is review of modeling methods of the expected value of experimentation
\par
for discovering gene regulation pathways. In this article, we use experimentation
\par
as both interventions (e.g., an experiment with gene knock-outs) and observations
\par
(e.g., passively observing the expression level of genes with no interventions).
\par
