\par
How to cite this article:
\par
WIREs Comput Stat 2013, 5:121--134. doi: 10.1002/wics.1244
\par
\textbf{Keywords: }experimentation recommendation models; gene regulation pathways;
\par
microarray experiments; high-throughput RNA sequencing
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Fisher1 mentioned that experimental design and
\par
statistical analysis procedure are not two different
\par
aspects. To get meaningful results from the statistical
\par
{$\ast$}Correspondence to: cyoo@fiu.edu
\par
Department of Biostatistics, Florida International University,
\par
Miami, FL, USA
\par
analysis, you need to have a sound experimental
\par
design.
\par
This article reviews statistical methods that
\par
are concerned with developing and evaluating a
\par
decision-analytic system that can assist biologists in
\par
designing and analyzing studies of cellular pathways
\par
with currently popular high throughput data
\par
sources.
\par
V o l u m e 5, M a r c h/A p r i l 2 0 1 3 \copyright{} 2 0 1 3 W i l e y P e r i o d i c a l s, I n c. 121Advanced Review wires.wiley.com/compstats
\par
Background
\par
In this section, we will review experimental design
\par
methodology. Later we will focus on experimental
\par
design methods used in high throughput gene
\par
expression data. But first, we provide some
\par
background about gene array chips along with RNA
\par
sequencing and give an overview of the experimental
\par
design methods that we will review in this article.
\par
Gene Array Chips and RNA Sequencing
\par
In human DNA, about three billion base pairs are
\par
arranged along the chromosomes in a particular order
\par
for each individual. If we use four bits to represent
\par
a base, we need approximately 1.5 GB to represent
\par
an individual genome sequence. Recently scientists
\par
announced that humans may have 30,000 to 40,000
\par
genes.2
\par
It takes several steps for a gene to be expressed
\par
and generate a folded protein. First, DNA has
\par
to be transcribed to an RNA molecule (usually
\par
called messenger RNA, or mRNA). This procedure
\par
begins when RNA polymerase (RNAP) binds in a
\par
certain position on the DNA. The binding point
\par
will determine the transcription direction and the
\par
portion of the DNA strand to be transcribed. RNAP
\par
will transcribe an RNA molecule, which has the
\par
complement of the DNA strand. After splicing the
\par
introns, the portions of the RNA strand that are
\par
not translated, a final mRNA is produced. Inside a
\par
ribosome, transfer RNA (tRNA) links peptides to
\par
form a protein. Finally, the generated protein folds
\par
into a particular three-dimensional shape.
\par
Gene expression has to be regulated. Without
\par
any regulation, transcription would generate large
\par
amounts of proteins in an uncontrolled fashion; at
\par
the other extreme, no transcription would take place,
\par
which would be equally detrimental. Various proteins
\par
called transcription factors regulate transcription by
\par
binding to control sites in the DNA. Different
\par
transcript-factor binding sites bind preferentially with
\par
different transcription factors, which act as enhancers,
\par
promoters, or repressors of transcription.
\par
Even though most of the transcription regulation
\par
mechanisms in the genome are not well understood
\par
yet, evidence shows that transcription factors play an
\par
essential role. Uncertainty in locating these factor-
\par
binding sites has been modeled using nucleotide
\par
distribution matrices, clustering methods,3 and neural
\par
networks4 to predict the regulatory signals such as
\par
promoters and repressors.
\par
Several gene-expression measurement technolo-
\par
gies are currently available for measuring the expres-
\par
sion levels of many genes at once. One is called
\par
a cDNA microarray, or simply DNA microar-
\par
ray;5 another is called an oligonucleotide array, or
\par
GeneChip\textregistered;6 and a third technique is called serial
\par
analysis of gene expression (SAGE). Also recent
\par
advance in sequencing technology has enabled high
\par
throughput RNA sequence (RNA-seq).7,8 We concen-
\par
trate our discussion on the first two techniques and
\par
RNA-seq, since they are high throughput methods,
\par
whereas SAGE is a more time consuming method.
\par
The DNA microarray technique uses user-definable
\par
probes{$\ast$} of DNA microarray, and the oligonucleotide
\par
array uses small oligonucleotide (usually 200 or 300
\par
bases) as factory-built probes. RNA-seq converts the
\par
mRNA into short fragments of cDNA and aligning
\par
the short fragments back to the genome can explain
\par
the sequence of mRNA and its expression level.
\par
The gene expression level measurement tech-
\par
niques are significantly different among the three
\par
methods. The cDNA microarray typically uses two
\par
different fluorescent dyes to measure the different
\par
gene expression levels of the control and experimental
\par
cells. Oligonucleotide arrays use different probes, one
\par
with a perfect match and the others with a mismatch
\par
(one nucleotide is altered from the probe of the perfect
\par
match). RNA-seq does not require any probes since it
\par
directly sequences from mRNA.
\par
As in Brown and Botstein,5 assume that the
\par
experimenter wants to compare the gene expression of
\par
vegetative and sporulating yeast cells. The cDNA array
\par
technique uses two different fluorescent dyes, green
\par
and red, and labels the vegetative and sporulating
\par
yeast cells with green and red respectively. Those two
\par
targets are later mixed and hybridized with the probes
\par
in the DNA microarray chip. The experimenter can
\par
configure the probes in the DNA microarray chip with
\par
the genes that he or she is interested in. For example,
\par
if the experimenter is interested in the TEP1 gene,
\par
he or she may include that gene in the probes in
\par
the microarray. Further, the greater abundance of the
\par
TEP1 gene in the sporulating yeast cell results in a
\par
high ratio of red light to green for the spot of the
\par
TEP1 gene probe.5 That ratio of light can be detected
\par
automatically.
\par
To perform the same analysis in the oligonu-
\par
cleotide array, the experimenter first has to purchase
\par
a chip that includes the probe of the TEP1 gene. The
\par
chip contains a perfect match probe and a mismatch
\par
probe that differs by only one nucleotide from the per-
\par
fect match probe. The chip is duplicated and one chip
\par
is hybridized with a target (cells from vegetative yeast)
\par
{$\ast$}According to the nomenclature recommended by B. Phimister of
\par
Nature Genetics, a probe is the nucleic acid with known sequence,
\par
whereas a target is the free nucleic acid sample whose abundance
\par
level is being detected.
\par
122 \copyright{} 2 0 1 3 W i l e y P e r i o d i c a l s, I n c. V o l u m e 5, M a r c h/A p r i l 2 0 1 3
\par
19390068, 2013, 2, Downloaded from https://wires.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wics.1244 by Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn, Wiley Online Library on [27/07/2023]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons LicenseWIREs Computational Statistics Statistical methods in experimentation recommendation models
\par
and the other chip is hybridized with the other tar-
\par
get (cells from sporulating yeast). A gene's expression
\par
level is calculated based on the difference of a perfect
\par
match probe intensity level and a mismatch probe
\par
intensity level; the gene's expression level is estimated
\par
to be proportional to the difference between the two
\par
probes. For a detailed information, see Marshall and
\par
Hodgson,9 Brown and Botstein,5 and Lipshutz et al.6
\par
The following are the four steps in data
\par
preparation of microarray data:10
\par
1. Convert raw pixel-by-pixel fluorescence data
\par
to a single measurement for each probe. This
\par
involves defining the boundary of the feature,
\par
averaging the pixels inside the boundary, and
\par
subtracting the background.
\par
2. (Oligonucleotide array only) Convert the pairs
\par
(usually 20) of match and mismatch probe
\par
measurements to a single summary expression
\par
level for each gene.
\par
3. (Oligonucleotide array only) Normalize the
\par
arrays, i.e. make sure an expression level of
\par
all the genes on one array means the same level
\par
on the other arrays.
\par
4. Apply a transformation (if needed) to the data
\par
so that each observation has the same variance
\par
The following are the steps in data preparation
\par
of RNA-seq data7,11:
\par
1. An RNA sample extracted from single or
\par
multiple cells is first converted to a library of
\par
cDNA fragments of a few hundred base pairs.
\par
2. The sequence segments on one or both ends of
\par
the cDNA fragments are sequenced. One such
\par
sequenced segment is called a sequence read and
\par
is typically 30bp to 100bp long, depending on
\par
the sequencing platform.
\par
3. In a typical RNA-seq experiment, tens of
\par
millions of sequence reads are obtained for a
\par
sample and the expression of a gene is measured
\par
by the number of sequence reads mapped to this
\par
gene.
\par
There are many potential errors in each step.
\par
To minimize the errors, different approximation
\par
models have been suggested based on different array
\par
techniques and different experimental conditions.12--17
\par
RNA-seq has several informatics challenges to map the
\par
short reads from RNA-seq to the reference genome,
\par
or to assemble them into contigs before aligning
\par
them to the genomic sequence to reveal transcription
\par
structure.7
\par
Overview of the Experimental Design
\par
Methods
\par
A Decision-Analytic Perspective on Expected
\par
Value of Experimentation
\par
In experimental design, an experimenter has to answer
\par
four major questions: First, what will the experimental
\par
conditions be (e.g., what temperature and nutrients to
\par
use)? Second, which experimental manipulations will
\par
be used (e.g., which genes to knock out)? Third, when
\par
should measurements be taken (e.g., after one gene
\par
is knocked out, do we measure the expression levels
\par
of other genes at one hour, two hours, or something
\par
else)? Fourth, how many times should the experiment
\par
be repeated in order to have statistically meaningful
\par
analyses and results? To answer these questions well,
\par
the experimenter should have some method to rank
\par
the possible experimental designs based on his or her
\par
scientific interest and preferences. A natural way of
\par
modeling information gain that is sensitive to user
\par
preferences is to use expected value of information
\par
from decision theory.
\par
Example of Using Expected Value
\par
of Experimentation
\par
Assume that Dr. A wants to study the genes related to
\par
Alzheimer's disease in different regions of the brain.
\par
Ultimately, he wants to develop a causal pathway
\par
among those genes from the different brain regions.
\par
Dr. A knows some of the mouse neurological genes
\par
of interest18 from previous studies, but he wants to
\par
confirm that they are relevant. He may also want
\par
to discover additional genes that are not previously
\par
known to be related to Alzheimer's disease. The results
\par
of his first phase of study show that some additional
\par
genes are indeed involved in Alzheimer's disease in the
\par
nucleus basalis, a brain region just under each frontal
\par
lobe.
\par
Among the candidate genes now identified, Dr. A
\par
wants to do further study by performing a knock-out
\par
experiment involving three genes: X, Y, and Z. Ideally,
\par
he would like to perform other knock-out experiments
\par
as well, but among the genes that are currently under
\par
consideration, only X, Y, and Z are available as
\par
knock-outs in mice (the organism model being used)
\par
at the present time. Also, given limited funds, he can
\par
only afford to purchase 40 microarray chips. For the
\par
baseline study, Dr. A purchases 10 normal mice, 10
\par
mice with gene X deleted, 10 with Y deleted, and 10
\par
with Z deleted. He uses a cDNA microarray for each
\par
of the 40 experiments. Suppose that the microarray
\par
measures the expression levels for all known mice
\par
genes. He performs a data analysis of the results of
\par
these 40 experiments. To his surprise, both gene Z and
\par
gene Y appear to regulate many other genes of interest.
\par
