RELATED WORK OF EXPERIMENTAL
\par
DESIGN METHODS
\par
In order to give recommendations about designing a
\par
gene-expression experimental study, we need a way to
\par
represent an experimenter's preferences into a decision
\par
model. Typically the decision model is build based on
\par
decision theory. In this section, we will review the
\par
Decision Theory
\par
Decision theory formalizes how to combine uncer-
\par
tainty and objectives to derive how a rational agent
\par
should make a decision under different circumstances.
\par
Uncertainties are modeled in decision theory as prob-
\par
abilities. Multiple objectives (usually conflicting) force
\par
a decision maker to assess the trade-offs among the set
\par
of options. These trade-offs are explicitly modeled as
\par
a utility (based on the utility theory21) of the decision
\par
maker.
\par
Assessing a decision maker's utility involves
\par
many steps, and different methods are available for
\par
each step. For example, to assess a utility function,
\par
one can use the preference method introduced by
\par
von Neumann and Morgenstern22 or the strength-of-
\par
preference method pursued by psychophysicists von
\par
Winterfeldt and Edwards.23 Whatever method the
\par
analyst decides to use, he or she must make clear
\par
the definitions of the attributes, which are the major
\par
factors in the decision-making process, and try to
\par
construct a natural quantitative value for them. The
\par
next step is to construct a utility function of the
\par
decision maker using the defined attributes.
\par
Many times the analyst will want to include
\par
two or more attributes for assessing the decision
\par
maker's utility function. In this case, the analyst may
\par
use multiattribute utility theory (MAUT) weighting
\par
techniques to construct a weighted model among the
\par
evaluation objects. Several different techniques are
\par
available to construct the model. In MAUT, as in a
\par
single-attribute case, either variation of the simple
\par
multiattribute rating technique (SMART)24 or the
\par
indifference technique21 can be applied for utility
\par
assessment. A simple weighted additive model can
\par
be usually used as a model, assuming that an additive
\par
assumption holds among the attributes. If the analyst
\par
believes there is a violation in this assumption, he or
\par
she has to either reformulate the attributes or use a dif-
\par
ferent weighted model, such as a multiplicative model.
\par
Since the utility assessment process is a tedious
\par
task, many adaptive methods have been developed to
\par
ease the assessment process.25 These adaptive methods
\par
assume that a prior utility model is available to the
\par
decision maker. Based on the prior utility model,
\par
the adaptive methods build the decision maker's
\par
utility function with a relatively smaller number of
\par
assessment questions.
\par
Based on the assessed probabilities and utility
\par
function, a decision model (e.g., a decision tree) is
\par
constructed. A decision tree consists of three types of
