Experimentation. Likewise, without experimental cost
\par
modeling, the recommendations will not be compre-
\par
hensive.
\par
Active Learning in Bayesian Networks
\par
The Bayesian network (BN) model, also known as a
\par
probabilistic causal network, has been widely used to
\par
learn predictive models from data. The BN overcomes
\par
the shortcomings of the simple Bayesian method, first
\par
explored extensively in the early 1960s, that assumed
\par
conditional independence of the evidence given an
\par
hypothesis. BNs can model causality based on expert's
\par
knowledge, data or both. It is also used in many
\par
medical related domains because of its ability to per-
\par
form inference in the forward direction, backward
\par
direction, or any combination.40--42 One practical
\par
limitation of BNs is that inference within them is
\par
NP-hard,43 which is a frequent limitation of power-
\par
ful reasoning methodologies; in response, researchers
\par
have developed approximate inference methods to
\par
apply to complex BN inference tasks.
\par
A causal Bayesian network (or causal network
\par
for short) is a Bayesian network in which each arc
\par
is interpreted as a direct causal influence between a
\par
parent node (variable) and a child node, relative to the
\par
other nodes in the network.44 Figure 1 illustrates the
\par
structure of a hypothetical causal Bayesian network
\par
structure containing five nodes that represent genes.
\par
The probabilities associated with this causal network
\par
structure are not shown.
\par
The causal network structure in Figure 1 indi-
\par
cates, for example, that the Gene1 can regulate
\par
(causally influence) the expression level of the Gene3,
\par
which in turn can regulate the expression level of
\par
the Gene5. The causal Markov condition gives the
\par
conditional independence relationships specified by a
\par
causal Bayesian network: A variable is independent of
\par
its non-descendants (i.e., non-effects) given its parents
\par
(i.e., its direct causes).
\par
The causal Markov condition permits the joint
\par
distribution of the nvariables in a causal Bayesian
\par
network to be factored as follows44:
\par
P(x1, x2,. . . , xn\textbar K)=
\par
n
\par
P(xi\textbar{$\pi$}i, K), (3)
\par
i=1
\par
where xi denotes a state of variable Xi, {$\pi$}i denotes
\par
a joint state of the parents of Xi, and K denotes
\par
background knowledge. Bayesian networks have
\par
been used in analyzing gene expression data from
\par
microarray experiments in order to model gene
\par
regulated pathways.45--47
\par
Murphy and Mian48 showed that the Boolean
\par
network model,33 the linear model,49 and the
\par
nonlinear weighted model50 are all special cases
\par
of dynamic Bayesian networks (DBN). A DBN
