\par
An extension of supervised learning, active learn-
\par
ing was applied to learning causal Bayesian networks
\par
in scientific discovery.53 Tong and Koller used edge
\par
entropy loss functions and a myopic search in order
\par
to recommend the next best experiment to perform.
\par
Their main assumptions are: (1) discrete variables
\par
only; (2) no missing data; and (3) no modeling of
\par
latent (hidden) variables.
\par
They modeled all variables in their experiments
\par
as a query variable Q and the particular instantiation
\par
for Q as a query q. A query represents the variables
\par
that can be experimentally manipulated. The result
\par
of the query is called the response and it is a
\par
randomly sampled instance x from the distribution of
\par
all nonquery variables. They modeled manipulation
\par
and selection using the manipulation representation
\par
in Cooper and Yoo.54
\par
For example, consider the hypothetical Bayesian
\par
network shown in Figure 1. Assume the experimenter
\par
wants to determine the causal relationship between
\par
Gene2 (X2) and Gene3 (X3), then Q = \textbraceleft X2,X3\textbraceright. Fur-
\par
ther assume that the experimenter can only do the
\par
following intervention: the experimenter can knock
\par
out Gene2, so the experimenter in this study can
\par
either set Gene2's expression level to false (which
\par
represents a knocked out state) or passively observe
\par
the Gene2's expression level. In this setting, query
\par
q can either be setting X2 to be false or passively
\par
observing X2. Suppose the experimenter knocked out
\par
the Gene2 and then passively observed the output of
\par
the nonquery variables (X1, X3, X4, and X5). Assume
\par
the outcome is: (X1 = f, X3 = f, X4 = f , and X5 = f )
\par
then the result of the query x is this outcome that the
\par
experimenter has just observed.
\par
Tong and Koller introduce a myopic active
\par
learner l that selects a query q based upon the cur-
\par
rent distribution over structure S and parameters of S,
\par
which are represented as \texttheta S. The learner updates the
\par
distribution over S and \texttheta S with the resulting response
\par
x. To measure the quality of the updated distribution,
\par
Tong and Koller use a loss function, Loss(P), and
\par
define the expected posterior loss of the query as:
\par
ExPLoss(P(S, \texttheta s)\textbar q)= Ex{$\sim$}P(X\textbar q)Loss(P(S, \texttheta S\textbar q, x)).
\par
Choosing a query that has the lowest expected
\par
posterior loss is not practical because the possible
\par
number of structures S is exponential in the number
\par
of modeled variables. Additionally, for each structure,
\par
an exponential number of possible responses to the
\par
query have to be considered. For this article's proposed
\par
work, we have to deal with more complex models
\par
than Tong and Koller did because we are allowing the
\par
number of experimental repeats to be more than one.
\par
Tong and Koller point out that the loss
\par
function can be any function as long as it efficiently
\par
evaluates how close the predicted distribution is to
\par
the generating distribution. They used a simple edge
\par
entropy loss function on every pairwise relationship.
\par
The edge entropy loss function is given as:
\par
Loss(P(S, \texttheta s))=
\par
H(Xi {$\leftrightarrow$} Xj), (5)
\par
i,j
\par
where
\par
(4)
\par
H(Xi {$\leftrightarrow$} Xj)= - P(Xi {$\rightarrow$} Xj) log(P(Xi {$\rightarrow$} Xj))
\par
- P(Xi \textleftarrow{} Xj) log(P(Xi \textleftarrow{} Xj))
\par
- P(Xi Xj) log(P(Xi.Xj)).
\par
To make the computation of Eq. tractable, they
\par
assume there is a total ordering among the variables.
\par
Restricting the number of parents to be at most k,
\par
Friedman et al.45 showed a polynomial time algorithm
\par
to evaluate the structure scores. Tong and Koller use
\par
the algorithm of Friedman et al.45 to implement their
\par
system. To extend their system, they suggested using
\par
a method of searching over the possible orderings.55
\par
Applying the local computation for Bayesian network
\par
inference algorithm56 and using the assumption
\par
that there are at most kcandidate parents, the
\par
computation of (4) becomes linear in the number of
\par
variables.
\par
Tong and Koller applied their algorithm to three
\par
Bayesian networks with 5, 8, and 16 nodes, respec-
\par
tively. They limited the number of the query nodes
\par
to be at most 4 and k= 5. On the basis of their
\par
simulations, they showed that active learning per-
\par
forms better in finding BN structures than randomly
\par
choosing of the query nodes. Tong and Koller indeed
\par
developed a recoomendation system that can incor-
\par
porate the first phase results and Dr. A's preferences
\par
from the example given in Section Example of Using
\par
Expected Value of Experimentation. However, since
\par
the recommendations do not incorporate the cost of
\par
the experiments, they are not as comprehensive as
\par
the recommendations that are provided by the system
\par
discussed in the next section.
