Causal Discovery in Gene Expression Data
\par
Using Expected Value of Experimentation
\par
Yoo and Cooper19 introduced a system called
\par
gene expression data using expected value of
\par
experimentation (GEEVE) that can recommend (under
\par
assumptions) the best experiment at any stage. For
\par
example, in the example given in Section Example
\par
of Using Expected Value of Experimentation, by
\par
incorporating the first phase results and Dr. A's
\par
preferences, the system can recommend experiments
\par
for Dr. A's initial study. Instead of the simple
\par
breakdown of the number of mice that Dr. A.
\par
purchased, the system might recommend that Dr. A
\par
purchase 7 normal mice, 8 gene X-deleted mice, 15
\par
gene Y-deleted mice, and 10 gene Z-deleted mice. The
\par
system would also help Dr. A in analyzing the results
\par
obtained from those 40 microarray experiments.
\par
GEEVE implements expected value of experi-
\par
mentation in discovering gene-gene causal interactions
\par
using gene expression data. GEEVE provides the fol-
\par
lowing assistance, which is intended to help biologists
\par
in their quest to discover gene-regulation pathways:
\par
{$\bullet$} Recommending which experiments to perform
\par
(with a focus on ``knock-out'' experiments) using
\par
an expected value of experimentation (EVE)
\par
method.
\par
{$\bullet$} Recommending the number of measurements
\par
(observational and experimental) to include in
\par
the experimental design, again using an EVE
\par
method.
\par
{$\bullet$} Providing a Bayesian analysis that combines prior
\par
knowledge with the results of recent microarray
\par
experimental results to derive posterior proba-
\par
bilities of gene regulation relationships.
\par
In recommending which experiments to per-
\par
form (and how many times to repeat them) the
\par
EVE approach considers the biologist's preferences
\par
for which genes to focus on during the discovery
\par
process. Also, since exact EVE calculations are expo-
\par
nential in time, GEEVE incorporates approximation
\par
methods. GEEVE is able to combine data from knock-
\par
out experiments with data from wild-type experiments
\par
to suggest additional experiments to perform and then
\par
to analyze the results of those microarray experimen-
\par
tal results. It models the possibility that unmeasured
\par
(latent) variables may be responsible for some of the
\par
statistical associations among the expression levels of
\par
the genes under study.19
\par
The GEEVE system consists of two modules.
\par
First, a gene data analysis module outputs the
\par
most probable causal pathways (that it can locate
\par
under assumptions) that are consistent with prior
\par
background knowledge (e.g., as obtained from the
\par
biologist user and typically based on the current biol-
\par
ogy literature) and previous experimental data (in raw
\par
form, rather than distilled form as is often found in the
\par
literature). Second, a decision-tree evaluation module
\par
outputs an experimental design (including the number
\par
of repeats to perform) that approximates the maxi-
\par
mum expected value of experimentation. This cycle
\par
of analysis and experiment generation continues until
\par
the experimenter is satisfied with his or her level of
\par
understanding of the genes under study.
\par
More specifically, the GEEVE system consists of
\par
two modules called the causal Bayesian network (see
\par
Section Active Learning in Bayesian Networks) update
\par
(CBNU) module and the decision tree generation
\par
and evaluation (DTGE) module (Figure 2). The
\par
CBNU module uses causal Bayesian network learning
\par
algorithm57,58 to analyze the current microarray
\par
data in light of the user's prior beliefs about the
\par
causal relationships among the genes under study.
\par
The DTGE module evaluates a decision tree that
\par
was generated based on the results of the CBNU
\par
module and the experimenter's preferences, which are
\par
expressed with GEEVE as a utility function. Finally
\par
(under assumptions) the best possible experiments are
\par
recommended to the experimenter. The experimenter
\par
then chooses the next experiment to perform, which
\par
may or may not be the one suggested by GEEVE.
\par
When the results are available, they can be submitted
\par
to the CBNU module for a new round of analysis.19
\par
Yoo and Cooper19 conducted a randomized
\par
controlled study that involved 10 biologists who
\par
are familiar with the SNF1 pathway in yeast. They
\par
consulted with an expert in the yeast SNF1 pathway
\par
to model the pathway for use in a simulator.
\par
The simulator generated measurements of cDNA
\par
microarray data based on experiments requested by
\par
the biologist participants in the study. The biologists
\par
were divided into two groups: (1) a control group who
\par
saw a fairly typical display of microarray experimental
\par
data and had access to a relatively basic statistical
\par
analysis of the simulated data and (2) an intervention
\par
group who saw both the basic statistical analysis and
\par
GEEVE's causal analysis plus had access to GEEVE's
\par
experimental recommendations.
\par
This study showed that most of the time the
\par
intervention group performed better than the control
\par
group in predicting whether pairs of genes (of interest
\par
to the biologist study participant) act independently
\par
or have a causal relationship. It also showed that the
\par
intervention group designed experiments more effi-
\par
ciently than the control group by not selecting gene
\par
pairs and number of experiments using a blocked
