\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
In gene expression microarray studies an exper-
\par
imenter is usually interested in discovering rela-
\par
tionships among thousands of genes.59,60 Different
\par
approaches are needed in microarray studies for causal
\par
discovery and experimental design recommendation.
\par
This article has reviewed such approaches.
\par
As stated in Section Active Learning in Bayesian
\par
Networks, Tong and Koller53 used a single-case
\par
approach to recommend to the experimenter the
\par
best possible pairwise relationship. However, often
\par
it is more efficient to repeat a given experiment
\par
multiple times, rather than to repeat the experiment
\par
sequentially.
\par
Tong and Koller53 and Ideker et al.38 used edge
\par
entropy loss functions to search for the next best
\par
experiment to perform. This approach can be useful
\par
when the experimenter does not have any preference
\par
toward the relationships among the genes. However,
\par
the experimenter will usually know for which genes
\par
to study in greater detail. Thus, a recommendation
\par
system that incorporates the preferences of the exper-
\par
imenter seems desirable. On the other hand, GEEVE
\par
allows repeats of an experiment and experimenter's
\par
preferences to be modeled. These improvements make
\par
GEEVE more applicable to real-world design of gene
\par
expression experiments.
\par
The Tong and Koller53 method uses expected
\par
value of experimentation (EVE) method but unlike the
\par
EVE approach of Tong and Koller,53 GEEVE (1) uses
\par
local search; (2) assumes no ordering among vari-
\par
ables; (3) models the possibility of a hidden common
\par
cause; (4) models an experimenter's prior knowledge
\par
of causal relationships; (5) incorporates a cost model;
\par
and (6) can recommend more than one experiment at
\par
a time.
\par
Unlike Ideker et al.38 method, GEEVE
\par
(1) models uncertainty; (2) uses a local search method;
\par
(3) models the possibility of a hidden common cause;
\par
(4) models an experimenter's prior knowledge of
\par
causal relationships; (5) models an experimenter's
\par
preference for which gene pairs to study;
\par
(6) incorporates a cost model; and (7) can recommend
\par
more than one experiment at a time in a study.
\par
Experiments provide benefit in terms of infor-
\par
mation, but they also have costs in terms of human
\par
labor and the expense of laboratory supplies. GEEVE
\par
uses a cost model that is assessed from the biologist
\par
experimenter. Considering preferences, costs, and a
\par
current model of causal relationships, GEEVE recom-
\par
mends the most cost-effective experiment it can find
\par
in its search of the space of experiments.
\par
More generally, GEEVE could be applied to
\par
medical diagnosis to support sequential diagnosis,
\par
which has been long studied in medicine.61 GEEVE
\par
is most useful when there are a large number of
\par
diagnostic tests that are related to the diagnosis. This
\par
is because GEEVE is designed to model and search
\par
among a large number of variables. While medical
\par
diagnosis is interested in the statistical dependency
\par
among diagnostic tests and diagnostic hypotheses,
\par
GEEVE focuses on the discovery of the causal rela-
\par
tionships. Thus, appropriate adjustments have to be
\par
applied to GEEVE for support of sequential diagno-
\par
sis. More directly, however, GEEVE is applicable to
\par
discovery of causal relationships among the pairs of
\par
large sets of variables when there is the possibility for
\par
many interventions on the variables; in biology, this
\par
seems most immediately applicable to the discovery
\par
of cellular function.
\par
In Section A Decision-Analytic Perspective on
\par
Expected Value of Experimentation, we mentioned
\par
four major questions that the experimenter has to
\par
answer to develop an experimental design. Most
\par
of the experimentation recommended systems have
\par
only covered two questions: (1) which experiment to
\par
choose and (2) how many times to repeat the exper-
\par
iment. The other two issues---how to set external
\par
experimental conditions (e.g., temperature) and the
\par
precise times at which to sample gene expression
\par
levels---are not covered in these systems.
\par
Regarding external experimental conditions,
\par
they could be modeled as exogenous variables in
\par
a causal Bayesian network. Extending the type of
\par
experimental interventions that are modeled in exper-
\par
imentation recommended systems is also left for
\par
upcoming experimentation recommended systems to
\par
incorporate. Regarding modeling the time course
\par
of gene expression, and determining precisely when
\par
to sample cells during experimentation, temporal
\par
Bayesian networks or other longitudinal statistical
\par
models appear a natural choice.45,48,62
