@article{yuOptimalSelectionRetailers2013,
  title = {Optimal Selection of Retailers for a Manufacturing Vendor in a Vendor Managed Inventory System},
  author = {Yu, Yugang and Hong, Zhaofu and Zhang, Linda L. and Liang, Liang and Chu, Chengbin},
  year = 2013,
  journal = {European Journal of Operational Research},
  volume = {225},
  number = {2},
  pages = {273--284},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  langid = {english},
  note = {European Journal of Operational Research 225 (2013) 273--284
\par
Contents lists available at SciVerse ScienceDirect
\par
European Journal of Operational Research
\par
j o u r n a l h o m e p a g e : w w w . e l s e v i e r . c o m / l o c a t e / e j o r
\par
Production, Manufacturing and Logistics
\par
Optimal selection of retailers for a manufacturing vendor
\par
in a vendor managed inventory system
\par
Yugang Yua,b, Zhaofu Honga,d, , Linda L. Zhangc, Liang Liang b, Chengbin Chu d
\par
a School of Management, Lanzhou University, Lanzhou, PR China
\par
b School of Management, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, PR China
\par
c IESEG School of Management (LEM-CNRS), Catholic University of Lille, Lille, France
\par
d Laboratoire G\'enie Industriel, Ecole Centrale Paris, France
\par
a r t i c l e i n f o
\par
a b s t r a c t
\par
Article history:
\par
Received 17 May 2011
\par
Accepted 25 September 2012
\par
Available online 17 October 2012
\par
Keywords:
\par
Supply chain management
\par
Vendor managed inventory
\par
Retailer selection
\par
Stackelberg game theory
\par
Hybrid algorithm
\par
A Vendor Managed Inventory (VMI) system consists of a manufacturing vendor and a number of retailers.
\par
In such a system, it is essential for the vendor to optimally determine retailer selection and other related
\par
decisions, such as the product's replenishment cycle time and the wholesale price, in order to maximize
\par
his profit. Meanwhile, each retailer's decisions on her willingness to enter the system and retail price are
\par
simultaneously considered in the retailer selection process. However, the above interactive decision mak-
\par
ing is complex and the available studies on interactive retailer selection are scarce. In this study, we for-
\par
mulate the retailer selection problem as a Stackelberg game model to help the manufacturer, as a vendor,
\par
optimally select his retailers to form a VMI system. This model is non-linear, mixed-integer, game-theo-
\par
retic, and analytically intractable. Therefore, we further develop a hybrid algorithm for effectively and
\par
efficiently solving the developed model. The hybrid algorithm combines dynamic programming (DP),
\par
genetic algorithm (GA) and analytical methods. As demonstrated by our numerical studies, the optimal
\par
retailer selection can increase the manufacturer's profit by up to 90\% and the selected retailers' profits
\par
significantly compared to non-selection strategy. The proposed hybrid algorithm can solve the model
\par
within a minute for a problem with 100 candidate retailers, whereas a pure GA has to take more than
\par
1 h to solve a small sized problem of 20 candidate retailers achieving an objective value no worse than
\par
that obtained by the hybrid algorithm.
\par
\'O2012 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
VMI (Vendor Managed Inventory) is a popular inventory man-
\par
agement strategy that allows a vendor to access his retailers' sales
\par
data and manage his retailers' inventory levels. The implementa-
\par
tion of VMI is beneficial for both the vendor and his retailers (Sahin
\par
and Robinson, 2002; Waller et al., 1999) since the vendor can man-
\par
age the inventory without information distortion, and coordinate
\par
the product replenishment of multiple retailers. Such coordination
\par
enables the vendor to adopt a common replenishment cycle for all
\par
retailers to reduce inventory level and cost. The successful applica-
\par
tions of VMI implementation have been widely reported. For
\par
instances, the VMI-type partnership between Procter \& Gamble
\par
(P\&G) and Wal-Mart dramatically improves P\&G's on-time deliver-
\par
ies and Wal-Mart's sales by 20--25\% and the inventory turnover by
\par
30\% (Yu et al., 2009a). Barilla made an inventory reduction by
\par
nearly 50\% at its retailers through VMI in 1988 (Hammond,
\par
2003). Dell and HP also operate efficient supply chains through
\par
VMI to reduce inventory level and cost (Tyan and Wee, 2003).
\par
Selecting retailers suggests itself to be a fundamental decision
\par
process for the vendor in a VMI system; the vendor has to decide
\par
whether to accept a retailer or not, and vice versa. This is especially
\par
true for manufacturers with limited production capacity. As the
\par
vendor, the manufacturer has to select retailers, and determines
\par
the total demand of the selected retailers within his production
\par
capacity. Even if the manufacturer has certain redundant capacity,
\par
involving more retailers for increasing capacity utilization may not
\par
be an acceptable option. This is because the additional retailers
\par
may lead to high delivery costs, inventory costs, etc. Moreover,
\par
selecting retailers often entails an interactive game process. When
\par
the manufacturer decides to add a new retailer, he needs to take
\par
into account changes on his other decisions, e.g., the wholesale
\par
price. Meanwhile, all retailers respond to the manufacturer's
\par
decision changes, and may decide to quit the VMI system. Due to
\par
the complexities involved, decision making in the above
\par
interactive selection process is likely to be difficult. Consequently,
\par
Corresponding author at: Laboratoire G\'enie Industriel, Ecole Centrale Paris,
\par
France. Tel.: +33 (0)1 41 13 18 15.
\par
E-mail address: hongms06@gmail.com (Z. Hong).
\par
0377-2217/\$ - see front matter \'O2012 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
\par
http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ejor.2012.09.044274 Y. Yu et al. / European Journal of Operational Research 225 (2013) 273--284
\par
a manufacturer in VMI systems expects functional models and
\par
algorithms to support his retailer selection, in attempting to
\par
maximize his profits and those of the selected retailers.
\par
This paper, as one of the first efforts, aims to help a manufac-
\par
turer to select retailers to be included in his VMI system while
\par
considering the optimal responses from the candidate retailers.
\par
The objective is to maximize individual profits of both the manu-
\par
facturer and the selected retailers. In the VMI system, the manufac-
\par
turer produces one type of product, and delivers the products to
\par
the selected retailers within common replenishment cycle. Each
\par
selected retailer buys the products from the manufacturer at a
\par
wholesale price, and sells them at a retail price. Besides the
\par
decision variables for the manufacturer to select retailers and for
\par
each retailer to express her willingness to join the VMI system,
\par
we simultaneously consider some other influencing decisions such
\par
as the product's wholesale price and backorder rate. Also consid-
\par
ered is the retailer's decision on the product's retail price. With
\par
the VMI policy, the manufacturer can access to the candidate
\par
retailers' sales data during the selection process, and observe their
\par
responses to his decisions.
\par
To optimally select retailers to form a VMI system described
\par
above, we develop a model based on Stackelberg game theory.
\par
The game model consists of two non-linear mixed-integer
\par
programming models at two levels (i.e., two sub-models). The
\par
upper level sub-model addresses the manufacturer's profit optimi-
\par
zation, whilst each retailer can make her optimal response using
\par
the lower level sub-model.
\par
Methodologically, it is difficult to solve the proposed model
\par
since any pure analytical method is intractable and the objective
\par
is non-concave (see details in Section 4). We therefore develop a
\par
hybrid algorithm that combines Dynamic Programming (DP),
\par
Genetic Algorithm (GA), and analytical methods. The hybrid
\par
algorithm can well make use of the advantages of each component
\par
algorithm; DP can optimally determine retailers whose demands
\par
best match the manufacturer's production capacity. GA is adopted
\par
to cope with the non-concavity of objective functions and the ana-
\par
lytical intractability of some decision variables. The analytical
\par
methods are developed for deciding on backorder rates and retail
\par
prices for saving computation time as well. The numerical results
\par
show that the proposed algorithm can solve the model within
\par
20 s for a problem with 100 candidate retailers; whereas it takes
\par
a pure GA one hour without getting a satisfactory solution/objec-
\par
tive value as gained by the hybrid algorithm even for a small prob-
\par
lem with only 20 candidate retailers (see Section 5.3).
\par
Numerical examples are also conducted to compare our retailer
\par
selection strategy with a non-selection strategy, where all candi-
\par
date retailers are included in the VMI system. The results show that
\par
the profits of both the manufacturer and the selected retailers
\par
increase significantly under the selection strategy.
\par
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2
\par
reviews the related work. In Section 3, we describe the retailer
\par
selection problem, following which we introduce the Stackelberg
\par
game model for selecting retailers. Section 4 studies the optimal
\par
strategies of the retailers and the manufacturer, and presents a hy-
\par
brid algorithm for solving the Stackelberg game model. We report
\par
numerical examples and sensitive analyses in Section 5, and con-
\par
clude the paper in Section 6 by highlighting the managerial in-
\par
sights obtained.
\par
2. Literature review
\par
The work most relevant to this study can be classified into two
\par
groups. The first group relates to partner selection in a supply
\par
chain, and the second is associated with game theory in inventory
\par
management.
\par
2.1. Partner selection in a supply chain
\par
Generally speaking, retailer selection in a VMI system is a kind
\par
of partner selection in a supply chain. The operations research of-
\par
fers a range of methods and techniques for the supplier selection
\par
problem. Typical examples of such methods are multi-criteria deci-
\par
sion-making approaches and integer programming models.
\par
The multi-criteria decision-making approaches include Analytic
\par
Hierarchy Process, Data Envelopment Analysis, Analytic Network
\par
Process, fuzzy theory, Simple Multi-Attribute Rating Technique,
\par
Multi-Attribute Utility Theory, etc. (Aissaoui et al., 2007; De Boer
\par
et al., 2001; Ho et al., 2010; Wan Lung, 2008).
\par
In regard to integer programming models, Kasilingam and Lee
\par
(1996) propose a mixed-integer programming model for a firm to
\par
select vendors by determining order quantities to minimize the
\par
costs of purchasing, transportation, receiving poor quality parts.
\par
Degraeve et al. (2000) formulate a supplier selection model based
\par
on the total cost of ownership. With the real world data and case
\par
study, they point out that the model within inventory management
\par
is useful to reduce the costs of the system and helpful for supplier
\par
selection. Ghodsypour and O'Brien (2001) present a non-linear
\par
mixed-integer programming model to solve the multiple sourcing
\par
problem in vendor selection focusing on minimizing the total cost.
\par
They transform the model into a pure non-linear programming by
\par
branching the integer variables, and substituting their values in
\par
the programming, and then solve the problem by Excel Solver. Tal-
\par
luri (2002) proposes a 0--1 integer linear programming to evaluate
\par
alternative vendor bids set by the buyer, and to select an optimal set
\par
of bids. Murthy et al. (2004) consider the vendor selection problem
\par
in a make-to-order supply chain. In their approach, a 0--1 integer
\par
linear programming model is formulated to minimize sourcing
\par
and purchasing costs under the capacity constraint. To solve the
\par
problem, they develop a heuristic procedure based on Lagrangian
\par
relaxation. Che et al. (2009) discuss a cooperator selection and
\par
industry assignment problem in supply chain networks. Based on
\par
the line balancing technology, they formulate a mathematical mod-
\par
el, and a genetic algorithm is adopted with an objective of minimiz-
\par
ing the total delivery delay loss. Li et al. (2009) study a supplier
\par
selection problem considering the supply contract within non-
\par
stationary stochastic price and demand. They show that the dura-
\par
tion of the contract is an import factor for the replenishment policy
\par
and selection decision. Mendoza and Ventura (2010) develop a
\par
mathematical model for supplier selection and inventory control
\par
problems in a serial system. Their model is used to select the sup-
\par
pliers for the manufacturer and allocate the orders to the selected
\par
suppliers. Wu et al. (2010) consider the risk factors in the supplier
\par
selection problem and propose a fuzzy multi-objective program-
\par
ming model to solve their problem. Recently, Zhang and Zhang
\par
(2011) and Mansini et al. (2012) consider the quantity discounts
\par
and constrained order quantities in the supplier selection problem.
\par
To summarize, most of the literatures on the supplier selection
\par
problem only consider the optimal objective of the buyer. Few
\par
papers like Gheidar Kheljani et al. (2009) consider the costs of both
\par
the suppliers and the buyer with an objective of minimizing the
\par
overall costs of the supply chain. Our research is different with
\par
them, we consider a retailer selection problem for a VMI system,
\par
where the retailers can optimize their decisions via reacting the
\par
vendor's decisions and the vendor can observe the reactions of
\par
the retailers under a Stackelberg game frame. Both the manufac-
\par
turer and retailers can maximize their individual profits during
\par
the retailer selection process.
\par
2.2. Game theory in inventory management
\par
Game theory has been widely used for analyzing the interac-
\par
tive decision processes among firms in a supply chain (e.g.Y. Yu et al. / European Journal of Operational Research 225 (2013) 273--284 Cachon and Zipkin, 1999; Cai and Kock, 2009; Granot and So\v sic \textasciiacute{}
\par
,
\par
2003; Slikker et al., 2005; Wang and Parlar, 1994; Wang and
\par
Gerchak, 2001). Wu et al. (2005) study the coordination in a
\par
one-vendor and one-retailer supply chain under a VMI contract.
\par
According to the contract, the vendor, as a Stackelberg leader,
\par
manages the retailer's inventory and bears inventory holding
\par
cost; the retailer sells goods and determines the retail prices.
\par
Their results suggest that such a contract achieve optimal profit
\par
coordination between the vendor and the retailer, where the
\par
vendor and retailer freely interact between the vendor and its
\par
retailer. Bichescu and Fry (2009) analyze a decentralized supply
\par
chain operating with a VMI agreement and a continuous review
\par
inventory policy. Among different channel power relationships,
\par
they find that when the vendor acts as the Stackelberg leader,
\par
the lowest system costs incurr. Their findings also indicate that
\par
power concentration at the retailer leads to a higher cost than
\par
a system where the vendor is the leader. However, such a sys-
\par
tem is more efficient than the one where power is equally dis-
\par
tributed between the vendor and the retailer. Viswanathan
\par
(2009) models discount pricing decisions in a vendor--buyer
\par
supply chain using Stackelberg game. The results show that
\par
the leader's optimal conditional strategy leads to a perfect coor-
\par
dination for the VMI system. Similarly, Tyan and Wee (2003)
\par
point out that the dominant relationship of a vendor in a VMI
\par
system is the key to ensure success for the system as a whole.
\par
Likewise, Netessine and Rudi (2004) prove that the Stackelberg
\par
leader can benefit more than that of the Nash equilibrium. Chen
\par
et al. (2010) deal with coordination problems in a separated
\par
distribution system under VMI. By modeling decision-making
\par
with a Stackelberg game in cooperative and non-cooperative
\par
settings, they find that the non-cooperative decentralization leads
\par
to a higher retail price, less inventory and channel-wide profit.
\par
Yu et al. (2009a) study a pricing problem in a VMI system
\par
where one manufacturer serves multiple retailers. In the system,
\par
the manufacturer purchases materials to produce products, and
\par
distributes them to the retailers. The retailers buy the products
\par
from the manufacturer at a wholesale price, and sells them at
\par
retail prices. Under the Stackelberg game frame, the manufac-
\par
turer knows the retailers' complete information. The manufac-
\par
turer also knows how to utilize the information to maximize
\par
his profit. Their analyses also show that both the manufacturer
\par
and the retailer can improve their profits using a cooperative
\par
contract. As an extension of the above study, Yu et al. (2009b)
\par
consider both advertising and pricing problem in a VMI-type
\par
supply chain. However, none of these papers can be used to
\par
determine which retailers should be selected by the manufac-
\par
turer to form a VMI system.
\par
