\par
3. Problem description and retailer selection model
\par
In this section, we first define research problem and notations,
\par
and then propose the retailer selection model after deriving the
\par
cost and revenue functions of the manufacturer and his retailers.
\par
3.1. Problem description and notations
\par
To form a VMI system, the manufacturer selects m retailers
\par
from a set of candidate retailers \dh 1;. . . ; m\TH. In the selection process,
\par
the manufacturer also takes into account his decisions on the com-
\par
mon production replenishment cycle for his retailers (C), inventory
\par
backorder at his retailers' warehouses (bi), and his wholesale price
\par
(cp). The primary objective of the decisions is to maximize the prof-
\par
it of the manufacturer (NPm) while the profit maximization of each
\par
retailer i \dh NPbi; i{$\frac{1}{4}$} 1;. . . ; m\TH{} is also considered in the manufac-
\par
turer's decisions. Retailer i 's decisions are her retail price and will-
\par
ingness to enter the system. The above decision process is based on
\par
the following assumptions defining our research context.
\par
(1) Each retailer is willing to join the VMI system if doing so is
\par
profitable to her.
\par
(2) Under a VMI policy, each candidate retailer is willing to pro-
\par
vide her information about inventory costs and demand data
\par
to the manufacturer, and agrees to let inventory managed by
\par
the manufacturer if selected. The manufacturer is willing to
\par
manage the inventory of his retailers. The manufacturer and
\par
his retailers share the inventory costs. Each retailer pays fi
\par
dollar/unit to the manufacturer as her inventory cost accord-
\par
ing to the demand volume, and the manufacturer pays for
\par
the rest of the inventory costs.
\par
(3) The manufacturer produces one product type under a pro-
\par
duction capacity constraint. Each retailer buys the product
\par
from the manufacturer, and sells it in independent market.
\par
In order to streamline the supply chain, the manufacturer
\par
expects to synchronize his production cycles with his retail-
\par
ers' orderings. The vendor serves his retailers with a com-
\par
mon replenishment cycle policy to reduce inventory levels
\par
and costs (Yu et al., 2009a, 2011, 2009b) and ease the VMI
\par
management; In Barilla, the common replenishment cycle
\par
mostly is one week (Hammond, 2003). In addition, using
\par
the common replenishment policy allows us to compare
\par
our results with those in Yu et al. (2009a).
\par
The notations throughout the paper are listed as follows:
\par
2.3. Summary of the literature review
\par
In the literature focusing on partner selection, the candidate
\par
vendors and retailers are unable to simultaneously respond to their
\par
respective selectors. Such interactive responses are common in the
\par
real world, whilst they are basically missing in the literature.
\par
The literature considering game theory mainly focuses on either
\par
supply chain coordinations or different power structures between
\par
the vendor and retailers. There is an implicit assumption underly-
\par
ing these studies: supply chain partners (i.e., vendors and retailers)
\par
are pre-determined. In this regard, partner selection is rarely com-
\par
bined with game theory.
\par
To our best knowledge, there is little work on the partner selec-
\par
tion problem for a VMI system. Thus, in this study, we try to fill up
\par
the gap of the retailer selection problem for a VMI system by (1)
\par
developing a Stackelberg game model and (2) developing an effi-
\par
cient hybrid algorithm for solving the model.
