3.3. Decision process and model complexity
\par
The general decision process of Model SM is as follows. The
\par
manufacturer tries all his possible decisions by considering the
\par
optimal responses from the retailers. Based on the responses, the
\par
manufacturer selects those retailers who can help to maximize
\par
his profit. The specific decision process is elaborated below.
\par
The manufacturer first assigns a set of values to the variables x,
\par
C, b, and cp; retailers 1,. . ., m react to these values. Each retailer i
\par
determines whether to enter the VMI system (yi = 1) or not
\par
(yi = 0). If she enters, she determines the optimal retail price. Based
\par
on all such responses from the retailers, the manufacturer calcu-
\par
lates a profit. The manufacturer can then try another set of values
\par
of decision variables to calculate a new profit. The process contin-
\par
ues until the manufacturer's profit cannot be increased any more.
\par
The optimal selection of retailers is then finished, and the system
\par
reaches a so-called Stackelberg equilibrium. At this point, both
\par
the manufacturer and retailers are not willing to deviate from
\par
the equilibrium. This is because the equilibrium maximizes profits
\par
of all, and any deviation from it will bring loss to whoever causes
\par
the deviation. The above process can be simplified if the manufac-
\par
turer gets analytical, instead of numerical, responses from retailers.
\par
In achieving this, the lower sub-model including Constraints (10)--
\par
(13) can be replaced with the analytical responses of the retailers,
\par
and be taken as the constraints of the upper sub-model capturing
\par
the manufacturer's decisions.
\par
Solving the SM is very complex. A number of decision variables
\par
are not analytically tractable. In regard to the manufacturer's deci-
\par
sions, for example, the optimal variable cp cannot be analytically
\par
obtained since its optimal values numerically rely on the retailer
\par
selection x. The concavity of the objective function (5) in cp and C
\par
does not exist since the function value can go up and down with
\par
x in our numerical examples. The reason for this is that x is not
\par
continuous, which, in turn, makes Pm
\par
i{$\frac{1}{4}$}1 xiDi \dh pi \TH{} discontinuous.
\par
Additionally, it is NP-hard to obtain optimal values for the deci-
\par
sion variable x, since, even if the values of cp and C are known, the
\par
remaining problem ( i.e., determining the optimal x) is equivalent
\par
to a knapsack problem, which is NP-hard (Michael and Johnson,
\par
1979).
\par
In the light of the above difficulties, an algorithm, which can
\par
cope with the NP-hardness, non-concavity, and analytical intracta-
\par
bility, is required to solve the proposed Stackelberg game model.
\par
We therefore develop a hybrid algorithm to solve Model SM by
\par
dealing with these difficulties.
\par
