4.2.1. Genetic algorithm for computing cp and C
\par
In order to obtain the values of x, it is necessary to know cp and
\par
C numerically. Furthermore, as soon as cp and C are known, a
\par
corresponding optimal value of x can be obtained by solving a
\par
knapsack problem. Therefore, the optimal strategy consists of
\par
obtaining the optimal values of cp and C. Unfortunately, it is impos-
\par
sible to get an analytical expression of NPm in function of cp and C,
\par
since it is impossible to write the analytical expression of x. It is
\par
even possible that this function is not concave because of the dis-
\par
crete nature of x. Observing that only two decision variables, cp and
\par
C, need to be solved numerically, we are encouraged to introduce
\par
an intelligent algorithm. Therefore, the search for optimal or near
\par
optimal values of cp and C is carried out in a numerical way with
\par
GA, because GA is very powerful for finding a global search
\par
efficiently (Akyol and Bayhan, 2007; Liu, 1998; Solnon et al.,
\par
2008) by comparing individual numerical results and without
\par
requirements in concavity and analytical tractability.
\par
In this genetic algorithm, an individual corresponds to the val-
\par
ues of cp and C, and it is coded by a chromosome with two genes:
\par
one gene being the value of cp and the other being the value of C.
\par
The chromosomes in the generations afterwards are generated
\par
by using operations of selection, crossover and mutation. The
\par
selection operation with an elitist strategy (Onwubolu and Muting,
\par
2001) allows the best individuals of the current generation (with a
\par
ratio of 2\%) to survive from the parents as the offsprings. The cross-
\par
over operation is performed in the following way. A pair of chro-
\par
mosomes are selected as parents with probability 0.1 to generate
\par
two offspring (i.e., two chromosomes) for the next generation.
\par
The better the fitness value a chromosome has, the larger the
\par
chance that the chromosome will be selected as a parent. Once a
\par
pair are selected, some chromosome genes (cp and C) of one parent
\par
are randomly selected as crossover points (Poon and Carter, 1995),
\par
