5.2. Sensitivity analyses
\par
In this study, sensitivity analysis is conducted with respect to
\par
manufacturer-related parameters, retailer-related parameters,
\par
and other parameters.
\par
5.2.1. Manufacturer-related parameters
\par
5.2.1.1. Production capacity. Production capacity changes may af-
\par
fect the number of selected retailers, the manufacturer's maximum
\par
profit, and the actual production capacity utilized. We, thus,
\par
change the values of the manufacturer' production capacity P from
\par
100 (a low value) to 4000 (a high value) in our model to see the
\par
corresponding changes to the above variables. The results are ob-
\par
tained, as shown in Fig. 3. As a general trend, the increase of pro-
\par
duction capacity results in the increase of the optimal number of
\par
retailers, the manufacturer's maximum profit (under both strate-
\par
gies), and actual production capacity utilized.
\par
With the above results, we conclude:
\par
(1) It is not reasonable for the manufacturer to select all candi-
\par
date retailers to form his VMI system even the production
\par
capacity is high enough. Even if a retailer is profitable herself
\par
and willing to join in the system (yi = 1), the retailer should
\par
be rejected by the manufacturer (xi = 0) if she has a high
\par
operation cost such as inventory management cost and
\par
transportation cost, which bring a loss to the manufacturer.
\par
As shown in Fig. 3a and b, although his production capacity
\par
increases after P = 2200, the manufacturer is encouraged not
\par
to select more than 6 retailers. Next, if the production capac-
\par
ity is relatively short compared to the number of the retail-
\par
ers, as shown Fig. 3a, more retailers are selected with P
\par
increasing from 100 to 2100. Still some retailers may not
\par
be selected because a profitable retailer may be not profit-
\par
able to the manufacturer or a retailer cannot gain a positive
\par
profit.
\par
(2) The increase of the manufacturer's profit resulting from
\par
retailer selection is always obtainable if the production
\par
capacity is limited. This profit increase is very significant
\par
when the production capacity P is low. Our model allows
\par
the manufacturer to select the retailers most profitable to
\par
him, thus his added profit as the result of selection is high
\par
when P is relatively low since the retailers most profitable
\par
to him are firstly selected. As shown in Fig. 3b, the increase
\par
is more than 50\% if P is between 400 and 1400.
\par
(3) The optimized production capacity heavily depends on the
\par
candidate retailers profitable to the manufacturer, instead
\par
of their own profitability. If the production capacity is ample
\par
but retailers profitable to the manufacturer are short, it is
\par
not wise for him to seek full utilization of his production
\par
capacity or boost his production capacity limitlessly. As
\par
shown in Fig. 3c, if P = 4000, a half utilization is enough for
\par
maximizing the manufacturer's profit. On the other hand,
\par
the utilization of the production capacity is close to 100\% if
\par
retailers profitable to him are adequate compared to the pro-
\par
duction capacity.
\par
5.2.2. Retailer-related parameters
\par
Sensitivity analysis with respect to retailer-related parameters
\par
is used to understand why retailers are selected and rejected.
\par
The conclusions obtained are important for retailers to improve
\par
themselves in order to get selected.
\par
5.2.2.1. Price elasticity and market scale. Price elasticity ai and mar-
\par
ket scale Ki suggest themselves to be important market attributes
\par
of retailers, affecting retailer selection.
\par
For both parameters, we randomly take a retailer, who is re-
\par
jected in the base example in Section 5.1: retailer 4. If we change
\par
price elasticity a4 of retailer 4 from 0.96 to 0.40, the manufacturer
\par
will include retailer 4 in his VMI system (i.e., x4 {$\frac{1}{4}$} 1). This is be-
\par
cause a smaller price elasticity makes the product less sensitive
\par
to the price change. With retailer 4 selected, the manufacturer gets
\par
an additional demand of D4 = 63.94 for his product. If we change
\par
market scale K4 from 510 to 2000, the manufacturer also selects re-
\par
tailer 4 (i.e., x4 {$\frac{1}{4}$} 1). Consequently, retailer 4's profit increases to
\par
1.02 105. The manufacturer's profit also increases to 1.37 106
\par
by 2.24\%. Therefore, enlarging candidate retailers' market scale
\par
can strengthen the retailers' competitiveness, which contributes
\par
to profit increases of both the manufacturer and retailers.
\par
5.2.2.2. Inventory cost. As shown in Eq. (11), retailer selection can
\par
also be greatly influenced by the product's inventory cost fi. Taking
\par
retailer 8 as an example, the retailer is initially selected in the base
\par
example. However, it will be excluded if fi increases from 20.80 to
\par
150 (a high cost value). In this scenario, retailer 8's decision is
\par
y8 = 0, and the manufacturer's optimal decisions become x = (1,
\par
1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0). Correspondingly, the manufacturer's profit
\par
falls from 1.34 106 to 1.31 106 by 2.24\%. That is to say, the
\par
manufacturer often excludes a retailer if the inefficient inventory
\par
management of the retailer results in a high VMI-contract inven-
\par
tory cost fi. Therefore, retailers should improve their storage facil-
\par
ity management to get selected by reducing inventory cost. Such
\par
low inventory cost benefits both the manufacturer and selected
\par
retailers.
\par
5.2.2.3. Transportation cost. It is understandable that transportation
\par
cost affects the manufacturer's decisions on retailer selection. Tak-
\par
ing retailer 3, who is rejected initially in Section 5.1, as an example.
\par
If his transportation cost /i decreases from 792.73 to 30, retailer 3
\par
is selected by the manufacturer. With retailer 3 selected, the man-
