6. Conclusions
\par
This paper is one of the few studies on retailer selection for a
\par
VMI system. With focus on maximizing the manufacturer's profit,
\par
the candidate retailers' responses to the manufacturer's decision
\par
are also considered. The manufacturer also considers the product's
\par
wholesale price, and delivers the products to the selected retailers
\par
by following the VMI policy. Each retailer decides her willingness
\par
to join the VMI system and the product's retail price. We intro-
\par
duced a Stackelberg game model, in which retailer selection and
\par
retailer responses are combined. We further developed a hybrid
\par
algorithm combining GA, DP and analytical methods to solve the
\par
model. The hybrid algorithm only takes less than one minute for
\par
solving a problem with 100 candidate retailers. It is much more
\par
efficient than a pure GA algorithm, which takes several hours for
\par
a problem with only 20 candidate retailers. From the numerical
\par
studies, we obtain some important managerial insights as follows:
\par
(1) Retailer selection is very important for a manufacturing ven-
\par
dor in a VMI system to improve his profit. On average, both
\par
the manufacturer and the selected retailers can get their
\par
profits increased significantly through the selection policy
\par
(see Fig. 3 and Table 2). When the production capacity is lim-
\par
ited, by selecting the retailer most profitable to him, the
\par
manufacturer gains the highest profit increase compared to
\par
a non-selection strategy. Because of limited production
\par
capacity, the manufacturer only selects the retailers who
\par
can bring the most profits to him, instead of selecting most
\par
profitable retailers. In addition, the manufacturer should
\par
select a reasonable number of retailers, who share some
\par
fixed costs (seen from Eqs. 2, 3 and 5). In Fig. 3, it shown that
\par
the manufacturer's profit can increase by up to 90\% at
\par
around P = 1100.
\par
(2) The manufacturer is encouraged to maximize his production
\par
capacity utilization if his capacity is limited. However, it is
\par
not wise to commit his full production capacity or increase
\par
his production capacity limitlessly to boost his profit if there
\par
are not enough candidate retailers, whose joining is benefi-
\par
cial to him. Increasing the wholesale price to reduce the284 Y. Yu et al. / European Journal of Operational Research 225 (2013) 273--284
\par
demand sometimes is better than maximizing the capacity
\par
utilization. For example, as shown in Fig. 3 the manufacturer
\par
almost uses up his capacity if P is less than 2100 but at
\par
P = 4000, a half utilization would be enough for maximizing
\par
the manufacturer's profit.
\par
(3) A very profitable retailer may not be selected by the manu-
\par
facturer, e.g., the most profitable retailer 3 in Table 2. This is
\par
because the manufacturer only cares about his own profit-
\par
ability, instead of the retailers'.
\par
(4) For retailers, expanding market scale, cutting down product
\par
inventory costs, or reducing transportation costs can
\par
increase their competitiveness for entering a VMI system.
