Key Points for Decision Makers
\par
The use of network meta-analysis (NMA) instead of
\par
pairwise meta-analysis (PMA) for evidence synthesis
\par
is becoming popular, but it can be argued that this
\par
choice does not result in practical differences for
\par
economic evaluations
\par
In a real-world case study, the use of NMA instead of
\par
PMA to synthesize evidence on treatment effect
\par
substantially affected both the estimate of the benefit
\par
of future research and the type of research that
\par
should be pursued
\par
Recent recommendations on the use of NMAs
\par
instead of PMAs for evidence synthesis can have
\par
substantial practical implications for economic
\par
evaluations, especially in valuing future research
\par
1 Introduction
\par
Economic evaluation of healthcare technologies is con-
\par
cerned with maximizing measures of health in a target
\par
population given a constrained budget. Two key functions
\par
of economic evaluations are to inform the `adoption deci-
\par
sion' and the `research decision' [1, 2]. The adoption
\par
decision is concerned with which health technology to
\par
adopt given the existing evidence. The research decision
\par
tackles the issue of whether more evidence is required to
\par
support such an adoption decision. The set of activities
\par
informing decisions on future research is often referred to
\par
as expected value of information (EVI) analysis [3].
\par
Although the adoption and research decisions can be car-
\par
ried out using a single source of evidence (e.g., economic
\par
evaluation alongside a randomized controlled trial [RCT]),
\par
a comprehensive approach often requires decision-analytic
\par
modeling and comprehensive evidence synthesis from
\par
disparate sources [4, 5].
\par
A core component of such an economic evaluation is the
\par
estimates of treatment effect (e.g., hazard ratio, risk dif-
\par
ference, relative risk, odds ratio) among the treatments
\par
being evaluated. Such estimates are often generated
\par
through evidence synthesis (i.e., meta-analysis) of the
\par
RCTs that compare two of the treatments of interest. Tra-
\par
ditionally, evidence synthesis is performed on the effect of
\par
one treatment vs. another. The evidence generated through
\par
such a pairwise meta-analysis (PMA) can be represented by
\par
probability distributions for treatment effects between two
\par
treatments. Such evidence is then propagated through the
\par
decision-analytic model to generate measures of concern
\par
for policy making.
\par
When the economic evaluation considers more than
\par
two treatments, it is likely that estimates of treatment
\par
effect are available from several RCTs of various
\par
designs each including a subset of treatments. Together
\par
such studies construct a `network' of comparisons, in
\par
that each two treatments (represented by two nodes in
\par
the network) are directly and/or indirectly connected.
\par
Network meta-analysis (NMA) is the extension of PMA
\par
that allows evidence synthesis from studies that each
\par
might have compared only a subset of treatments [6].
\par
Evidence on the treatment effect between any two
\par
treatments is generated by considering the entire evi-
\par
dence in the network. This provides two key advantages
\par
over the more traditional pairwise analyses. First,
\par
NMAs can gain precision by `borrowing strength' from
\par
a larger set of RCTs that make up sources of evidence
\par
[7]. When the economic evaluation involves more than
\par
two strategies, without the use of NMAs, the conven-
\par
tional paradigm is to treat one strategy (e.g., the pla-
\par
cebo arm) as the `reference' and synthesize evidence in
\par
a series of PMAs. This practice inevitably ignores
\par
studies comparing alternative strategies with each other.
\par
Second, the evidence generated through a NMA is
\par
always consistent; for example, if the effect size is on
\par
the rate ratio (RR) scale, then the RR of treatment A
\par
vs. C is the product of the RRs of A vs. B and B vs. C
\par
(or similar logical relations for other measures of
\par
treatment effect) [8]. This is not necessarily the case if
\par
effect sizes are estimated from separate PMAs. Given
\par
these, NMAs seem to be the natural choice for evidence
\par
synthesis in economic evaluations where more than two
\par
interventions are compared.
\par
Of course, the difference in the methodological
\par
framework and the data that inform NMA and PMA
\par
suggest that the results of both the cost-effectiveness
\par
analysis (CEA) and EVI analyses may be affected by the
\par
choice of the evidence-synthesis framework. While the
\par
impact of NMA ves. PMA on measures of cost effec-
\par
tiveness has received some attention [9, 10], the impact
\par
on EVI outcomes, such as the expected value of perfect
\par
information (EVPI), has not hitherto been examined. It is
\par
likely that the added precision provided by incorporating
\par
the full evidence in NMAs compared with PMAs will
\par
result in lower values of EVI metrics. However, to what
\par
extent this phenomenon occurs in a real-world setting has
\par
not been demonstrated. In this work, we use a case study
\par
of pharmacotherapies of chronic obstructive pulmonary
\par
disease (COPD) to demonstrate the impact of the evi-
\par
dence synthesis paradigm on the magnitude of EVPI, and
\par
the ranking of future RCTs comparing subsets of treat-
\par
ment, based on their EVPIs.Network Meta-Analysis and Expected Value of Information 2 Methods
\par
We built a decision-analytic model to evaluate the CEA of
\par
the following five interventions for pharmacotherapy of
\par
COPD: no treatment (represented by placebo in the
\par
underlying efficacy studies), inhaled corticosteroids (ICS),
\par
inhaled long-acting beta agonists (LABA), inhaled long-
\par
acting muscarinic agents (LAMA), and a combination of
\par
ICS ?LABA.
\par
2.1 Pairwise Meta-analysis and Network Meta-analysis
\par
of Pharmacotherapies for Chronic Obstructive
\par
Pulmonary Disease (COPD)
\par
We synthesized the evidence from the published RCTs
\par
comparing the above-mentioned treatments using both
\par
NMA and PMA (placebo comparisons) to generate esti-
\par
mates for the treatment effect. Details of the NMA are
\par
provided elsewhere [11] (Please refer to the Online Sup-
\par
plementary Material-Appendix I, for details on the evi-
\par
dence-synthesis models). In summary, all RCTs involving
\par
patients with moderate/severe COPD and comparing two or
\par
more of the treatments with regard to their effect in
\par
reducing the rate of COPD exacerbations were considered.
\par
The treatment effect of interest was the RR, compared with
\par
placebo, of treatments on the rate of COPD exacerbation. A
\par
total of 19 trials (14 two-arm trials, 1 three-arm trial, and 4
\par
four-arm trials) including 28,172 patients constituted the
\par
evidence base for treatment effects. Figure 1 provides a
\par
schematic illustration of the network of evidence. Most
\par
interventions had been compared head-to-head in at least
\par
one RCT.
\par
RR estimates were obtained using a Bayesian Poisson
\par
regression NMA model. Specifically, we followed the
\par
published guidelines to perform a random-effects NMA,
\par
with non-informative uniform priors between 0 and 2 for
\par
the between-study standard deviation, and convergence
\par
was ensured by examining whether the trace plots have
