2.3 Analysis
\par
We ran the model and calculated the model outputs using
\par
10,000 random inputs. We reported the average value of
\par
costs, exacerbation rates, and QALYs for each treatment,
\par
and calculated the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio
\par
(ICER) and the incremental net monetary benefit (INMB,
\par
at a willingness-to-pay [WTP] value of Can\$50,000/
\par
QALY), compared with placebo, for both PMA- and
\par
NMA-based analyses.
\par
The main metric of interest for the present study was the
\par
expected value of perfect information (EVPI). EVPI is
\par
equal to the opportunity loss because of uncertainty in the
\par
underlying evidence [16]. As a secondary outcome, we also
\par
calculated the expected value of partial perfect information
\par
(EVPPI) associated with treatment effect, which quantifies
\par
the opportunity loss because of uncertainty in the treatment
\par
effects. Furthermore, we calculated the EVPPI for the costs
\par
and utilities. Calculation of EVPPI generally requires two-
\par
level Monte Carlo simulations. Given that the treatment
