\par
rankings of comparisons based on their EVI outcomes
\par
vastly differed across the two evidence-synthesis para-
\par
digms. Thus, even if both NMA- and PMA-based analyses
\par
indicate that future research is potentially justifiable, they
\par
might result in different designs for future studies.
\par
Although no overarching conclusions can be made from a
\par
single case study, our results confirmed our initial intuition
\par
that the use of evidence from indirect comparisons and the
\par
resulting change in the amount of uncertainty around theNetwork Meta-Analysis and Expected Value of Information treatment effect can have a pronounced impact on the
\par
results of the EVI analysis.
\par
Previous authors have evaluated the impact of the evi-
\par
dence-synthesis paradigm on the outputs of economic
\par
evaluations [9]. Cooper et al. [9] have studied the value of
\par
using NMAs vs. PMAs in a sample of economic evalua-
\par
tions. They concluded that the NMA-based approach to
\par
evidence synthesis allows the evidence on clinical effec-
\par
tiveness to be treated as a coherent whole, include more
\par
data, and sometimes relaxes the assumptions made in the
\par
PMA. Our study extends such conclusions to the EVI
\par
context.
\par
There were some limitations in our study. First and
\par
foremost, our results represent a single case study, and they
\par
should be interpreted accordingly. The overall finding that
\par
NMA-based EVI outcomes for treatment effect are smaller
\par
than their PMA counterparts will be reproducible in most
\par
realistic settings. However, there are instances in which the
\par
incorporation of the indirect evidence actually reduces the
\par
precision, thus potentially increasing EVI values; an
\par
example is when the overall between-study heterogeneity
\par
is increased with the inclusion of indirect evidence. We did
\par
not comprehensively evaluate all EVI metrics (such as the
\par
expected value of sample information) or EVPPIs for
\par
several other model parameters. The former is likely to
\par
follow the same pattern as the EVPI, and the latter is likely
\par
to have a complex and non-predictable association with the
\par
choice of evidence-synthesis paradigm, as our results on
\par
EVPPI for costs and utilities indicate; however, these
\par
remain to be empirically investigated. Our decision-ana-
\par
lytic model of COPD might be considered simplistic. For
\par
example, recent evidence postulates that the occurrence of
\par
COPD exacerbation negatively affects the course of the
\par
disease, and as such, pharmacotherapies can indirectly
\par
affect the rate of progression [19]. Further, the treatment
\par
effects were estimated from the trials that for the most part
\par
followed participants for less than a year. Thus, we have
\par
made the strong assumption that the treatment effect will
\par
persist over the span of 10 years. Such concerns regarding
\par
the validity of the model, however, are separate from the
\par
conceptual developments, implementation of the methods,
\par
and implications of the results.
\par
Given these results, a natural question that follows is:
\par
`what form of evidence synthesis, PMA or NMA, is the
\par
preferred paradigm informing the research decision?' In
\par
addressing this question, we first note that both PMA and
\par
NMA are statistical models generating probability distri-
\par
butions for an outcome of interest from a set of data points.
\par
The validity of inference from both models hinges upon the
\par
suitability of the underlying assumptions in the given
\par
context. Compared with PMA, NMA provides inference on
\par
the joint distribution of effect sizes and therefore provides
\par
information on additional parameters pertaining to the
\par
1003
\par
covariance between outcomes. This has generated some
\par
misgivings as to the transferability of evidence across trials
\par
that have non-overlapping interventions [20, 21]. However,
\par
it has already been pointed out that in its core, NMA is an
\par
extension of PMA and therefore relies on the same core
\par
assumption of exchangeability (i.e., homogeneity) of
\par
treatment effects across trials [8]. In addition, in probabi-
\par
listic decision analyses involving multiple strategies,
\par
making assumptions on such joint distributions is inevita-
\par
ble [22]. Overall, aside from the issue of model validity, we
\par
believe the principle of comprehensiveness in decision
\par
making is strongly in favor of the use of NMA in both the
\par
adoption and research decisions [4]. The principle under-
\par
lying NMA is to use the entire network of evidence, instead
\par
of a series of pairwise comparisons, in a consistent statis-
\par
tical framework for evidence synthesis. While such net-
\par
works have typically been constructed for the estimation of
\par
treatment effect, they can theoretically be expanded to
\par
jointly estimate the distribution of multiple parameters
\par
such as adverse events or the health state utility values. In
\par
addition, the Bayesian nature of NMA allows one to use
\par
informative priors to incorporate external information (e.g.,
\par
expert opinion). All in all, NMA is a flexible statistical
\par
paradigm allowing efficient use of the available informa-
\par
tion to better inform both treatment and research decisions.
\par
As the importance of comprehensiveness in formal
\par
evaluations of competing health technologies grows, so
\par
should the rigor of the analyses underlying such evalua-
\par
tions. Comprehensiveness and objectivity in synthesizing
\par
the evidence base is strongly emphasized [4, 23]. Quanti-
\par
tative methods of evidence synthesis that use the entire
\par
network of evidence offer conceptual and practical
\par
advantages over more traditional pairwise methods such as
\par
consistency and added precision. The methodology behind
\par
such forms of evidence synthesis is also actively being
\par
developed and the predicaments in the use of such methods
\par
(e.g., lack of familiarity of the analyst or the consumers of
\par
economic evaluations, lack of standardized and user-
\par
friendly software) are being addressed [24]. Although
\par
based on one case study, the present research provides
\par
empirical support that adopting this form of evidence
\par
synthesis can have important consequences in informing
\par
the `research decision'.
