In this paper we show how sensitivity analysis for a maintenance optimisation problem can be
\par
undertaken by using the concept of expected value of perfect information (EVPI). This concept is
\par
important in a decision-theoretic context such as the maintenance problem, as it allows us to explore the
\par
effect of parameter uncertainty on the cost and the resulting recommendations. To reduce the
\par
computational effort required for the calculation of EVPIs, we have used Gaussian process (GP) emulators
\par
to approximate the cost rate model. Results from the analysis allow us to identify the most important
\par
parameters in terms of the bene t of'learning' by focussing on the partial expected value of perfect
\par
information for a parameter. The analysis determines the optimal solution and the expected related cost
\par
when the parameters are unknown and partially known. This type of analysis can be used to ensure that
\par
both maintenance calculations and resulting recommendations are suf ciently robust.
