1. Introduction
\par
Unexpected failure has adverse effects not only in terms of
\par
system ef ciency, but also in terms of maintenance costs. Pre-
\par
ventive maintenance (PM) is employed as a means to control the
\par
decreasing with time reliability of deteriorating systems, reduce
\par
the risk of unexpected failure and offset the cost of corrective
\par
maintenance (CM), which is typically high. PM can be further
\par
divided into time-based (TM) maintenance, where maintenance
\par
activities take place at predetermined time intervals, and
\par
condition-based maintenance (CBM), where activities occur in
\par
reaction to the system state. An example of CBM is the delay time
\par
model [1,2], which takes into account observed warning signs to
\par
assess the risk of incipient failures. Within the literature one can
\par
nd extensive reviews on preventive maintenance [3--5].
\par
The effect of maintenance on the system condition can be
\par
described broadly as perfect or imperfect repair. Perfect repair
\par
brings the system to a new condition and is often described as As
\par
Good As New (AGAN) [6]. Minimal or As Bad As Old (ABAO) repair,
\par
when maintenance restores the system to the condition it was just
\par
before failure [7], is a limiting case of imperfect maintenance.
\par
Within the literature one can nd a number of other models that
\par
describe cases not as extreme as perfect or minimal maintenance,
\par
such as the Brown--Proschan model [8], where the effect of
\par
maintenance is AGAN with some probability p and ABAO with
\par
the remaining probability 1-p, and the virtual age models [9].
\par
Nakagawa in [10] proposes a model where the failure rate
\par
increases at PM by some proportion.
\par
PM is anticipated and scheduled, and its cost is usually lower
\par
than that of corrective maintenance, which is unexpected. How-
\par
ever, early preventive maintenance adds little to the reliability of
\par
the equipment and can lead to unnecessary costs. Typically,
\par
maintenance strategies often comprise of a combination of types
\par
of maintenance, both PM and CM. The challenge is to identify the
\par
strategies that achieve the best balance between these types of
\par
maintenance and minimise overall maintenance costs, judged over
\par
an appropriate time period.
\par
In this type of maintenance problems, as in other modelling
\par
problems, studying the sensitivity of the model output (optimal
\par
strategy) with respect to the changes in the model parameters (e.g.
\par
reliability parameters) is of great interest. In this paper we will
\par
investigate the issue of sensitivity analysis for maintenance opti-
\par
misation models. To achieve this we will consider two of the most
\par
commonly discussed time-based maintenance policies: the age-
\par
replacement policy (ARP) and the block-replacement policy (BRP).
\par
Both policies are discussed in [7]. Under the ARP, a component is
\par
replaced (AGAN repair) either at failure or when it has reached age
\par
T---whichever occurs rst. Under the BRP, one or more components
\par
are replaced periodically (AGAN repair) at times kT (k{$\frac{1}{4}$} 1,2,\dots ),
\par
regardless of their failure history, whereas individual components
\par
are replaced when failed. Since one does not have to record the
