\par
Sensitivity analysis in maintenance optimisation problems is
\par
important because if the cost-calculations are not suf ciently
\par
robust, the use of the maintenance model can lead to optimisation
\par
recommendations that are themselves not robust. Variance based
\par
methods [12] give a partial answer to this problem and can be
\par
used to assess the degree to which uncertainty in each variable
\par
contributes to the overall variance in model output. However,
\par
variance-based methods do not take account of the decision-
\par
making context properly. To address this issue, we use the concept
\par
of the expected value of perfect information (EVPI) to perform
\par
decision-informed sensitivity analysis: to identify the key para-
\par
meters of the problem and quantify the value of learning about
\par
certain aspects of the system. This information is of great impor-
\par
tance within a maintenance context, where decisions may not only
\par
relate to replacement timings, but also to accumulation of infor-
\par
mation about aspects of the system, like the ageing process.
\par
However, decision-theoretic sensitivity analysis within a mainte-
\par
nance optimisation context is demanding: the computation of
\par
expected utility requires the use of numerical integration or
\par
Monte-Carlo simulation techniques. To partially overcome this
\par
computational dif culty, we follow the work of Oakley in [13]
\par
and perform sensitivity analysis by using Gaussian process
\par
emulators.
\par
This paper is structured as follows: Section 2 presents the
\par
maintenance optimisation problem, discusses decision-theoretic
\par
sensitivity analysis and explains the use of Gaussian process (GP)
\par
emulators within this context. In Section 3 the suggested metho-
\par
dology is illustrated by using two settings of different
\par
complexities: the rst setting is a simple one with an analytic
\par
cost expression that we use to illustrate and validate the method,
\par
whereas in the second setting the additional complexity of system
\par
and maintenance policy requires us to use Monte-Carlo simula-
\par
tion to compute costs. Finally, we conclude with a discussion in
\par
Section 4.
\par
