determination of the contribution of each parameter to the
\par
variance of the model output analytically. The variance of condi-
\par
tional expectation [18] serves as an indicator of how strong the
\par
in uence of a certain parameter is on the model variability.
\par
However, within a decision-making context like the maintenance
\par
optimisation problem, it would be interesting to explore the effect
\par
of parameter uncertainty on the corresponding utility instead. To
\par
achieve this, we will use the concept of the expected value of
\par
perfect information (EVPI) as a measure of parameter importance
\par
[19,13], de ned in terms of cost reduction, rather than uncertainty
\par
reduction. The EVPI approach will allow us to perform sensitivity
\par
analysis on the maintenance optimisation model and identi es the
\par
model parameters for which, collecting additional information
\par
(learning) prior the maintenance decision, would have a signi -
\par
cant impact on total cost.
