\par
has a normal distribution. In the particular context, we are
\par
interested in computing partial EVPIs as in (9). By using an
\par
emulator to approximate utility U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH, expectations of the utility
\par
can be computed fast, reducing considerably the computational
\par
burden of sensitivity analysis, as described here.
\par
In the last decade, Gaussian process emulators have been used
\par
widely for a range of analyses: uncertainty analysis, sensitivity
\par
analysis, calibration, optimisation, etc. O'Hagan [21] has published
\par
a tutorial on how to use of GP emulators in practice. There, one can
\par
nd comparisons between using Monte-Carlo and emulator meth-
\par
ods to deal with uncertainty and sensitivity analyses and relevant
\par
examples. On the aggregate, both methods are able to produce an
\par
estimate for the model of interest, with an error term to represent
\par
model uncertainty. In principle, model uncertainty can be reduced
\par
to any desired level by making enough model runs. In the case of
\par
Monte-Carlo methods, it will typically take thousands (or even
\par
tens or hundreds of thousands) of model runs to achieve accep-
\par
table accuracy. In the case of the GP emulator, the set of model
\par
runs is used to build the emulator: achieving acceptable accuracy
\par
would require only a handful of runs for a model with just one or
\par
two inputs, or up to a few hundred for a complex function of many
\par
inputs. Therefore, achieving the desirable precision can be a
\par
cumbersome business for a complex model when using Monte-
\par
Carlo methods. Similarly, Kennedy and O'Hagan [22] carry out an
\par
investigation on Bayesian calibration of complex models using the64
\par
A. Zitrou et al. / Reliability Engineering and System Safety 120 (2013) 60--71
\par
emulators for some real-world case studies. In 2013, Daneshkhah
\par
and Bedford [24] use the emulator-based sensitivity analysis to
\par
investigate the changes of the system availability and reliability
\par
with respect to the changes in time-to-failure and time-to-repair
\par
distributions' parameters. In this study, tens to hundreds of model
\par
runs are enough to build the emulator (depending on the com-
\par
plexity of system under study), and subsequently compute the
\par
sensitivity measures. They report that the computation of the
\par
same sensitivity indexes for the system availability of a similar
\par
sized model would require 107 model runs if Monte-Carlo simula-
\par
tion is used instead.
\par
The objective of the maintenance optimisation problem
\par
described here is to identify decision T (time where PM is
\par
performed) that maximises the utility given in (3). Essentially,
\par
there are two approaches to the problem:
\par
A Assume that T belongs to a nite set S comprising of s
\par
options, S {$\frac{1}{4}$} fT1,T2,\dots,Tsg, or
\par
B Assume that T can take any value in \dh Tmin,Tmax\TH.
\par
Under approach A, to identify the optimal decision we need to
\par
determine the vector of outputs Y {$\frac{1}{4}$} \dh Y1,\dots,Ys\TH, where
\par
Yj{$\frac{1}{4}$} U\dh Tj,{$\Theta$}\TH{} \dh j{$\frac{1}{4}$} 1,\dots s\TH{} is described in (3). As discussed in [25],
\par
there are essentially two procedures for emulating a code with a
\par
multi-dimensional output like this one: the multi-output emulator
\par
(MO) and the many single outputs (MS) emulator. Alternatively,
\par
under approach B, we need to determine output Y\dh T\TH{} {$\frac{1}{4}$} U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH{} as a
\par
function of decision variable T. To do so, one can use a time input
\par
(TI) emulator [25].
\par
The MS emulator procedure treats fY1,\dots,Ysg as independent
\par
random variables, and emulates each output Yj separately. This means
\par
that s separate GP emulators are built, each describing the utility for
\par
each decision T{$\in$}S. Rougier [26] reports that this approach is
\par
unpopular, because it imposes the unattractive condition that the
\par
output components are conditionally independent given the model
\par
inputs. Where the outputs are related, it is likely that de ciencies in
\par
the emulator will lead to systematic errors which ought to be
\par
represented as non-zero typically positive covariances.
\par
The MO emulator is a multivariate version of the single output
\par
emulator, where the dimension of the output space is s. In this
\par
case, the standardised expression in (17) is distributed as a s-
\par
variate Student's process. This process allows for the representa-
\par
tion of any correlations existing among the multiple outputs. The
\par
corresponding posterior mean and covariance are similarly multi-
\par
variate versions of the ones presented in (16) and (17), respec-
\par
tively, and the details can be found in [25].
\par
The last method (under approach B) involves building just one
\par
single-output emulator. The model is then regarded as including
\par
the decision variable as an extra input, so that the output is
\par
considered as yT {$\frac{1}{4}$} U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH, where T{$\in$}\dh Tmin,Tmax\TH. The optimisation
\par
problem mentioned above is now reduced to emulating U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH.
\par
Note that, by formulating the problem this way, we avoid building
\par
a multi-output emulator. Thus, we can directly use the formulae
\par
presented in the previous sections for the single output Gaussian
\par
process emulator. To build this emulator, we evaluate U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH{} at
\par
n s design points chosen according to a space lling design (e.g.
\par
Latin hypercube) in the grid f\texttheta 1,\texttheta 2,\dots,\texttheta ng S. The advantage of
\par
this approach is that S does not have to be a nite space, and
\par
utility U\dh T,{$\Theta$}\TH{} can be determined for any value of T over some
\par
interval \dh Tmin,Tmax\TH. This is the approach that we are taking in
\par
this paper.
\par
In [25] the three methods are compared against two criteria:
\par
computational ef ciency and exibility. In terms of computational
\par
ef ciency both the MO and the MS emulators require n number of
\par
runs, where n is the number of points in the training set. The TI
\par
emulator has increased training requirements, needing n s num-
\par
ber of runs. However, required mathematical operations such as
\par
the inversion of the correlation matrix can be done relatively
\par
ef ciently.
\par
Flexibility is explored across two features: the covariance
\par
structure between outputs at different inputs but at the same
\par
time, and at different inputs at different times.
\par
With relevance to the rst feature, note that both the MO and
\par
TI emulators assume the same roughness parameters across
\par
T1,T2,\dots,Ts, whereas the MS emulator ts different parameters at
\par
different decisions (PM times) Tj. On this basis, the MS emulator is
\par
more exible, as its structure does not assume that a change in the
\par
input (reliability and maintainability parameters) has a similar
\par
effect on the output (cost rate) across the different decisions (PM
\par
times).
\par
When it comes to the covariance between outputs at different
\par
inputs and times, the MO emulator allows a general structure that
\par
is built in the covariance matrix. In the MO case, the effect of
\par
change is, thus, determined for point \dh Ti,Tj\TH{} speci cally. In other
\par
words, the covariance between U\dh Ti,\texttheta 1\TH{} and U\dh Tj,\texttheta 2\TH{} (i.e. between
\par
the values of different outputs at different input settings) for the
\par
MO emulator approach is measured by (13) times the \dh Ti,Tj\TH{} entry
\par
of their covariance matrix. Therefore, there are some quite severe
\par
restrictions on the joint covariance structure that are imposed for
\par
tractability, as discussed in [26]. In contrast, the TI emulator
\par
assumes that the effect of a change in the inputs is a function of
\par
Ti-Tj for i{$\neq$}j. More precisely, the covariance between U\dh Ti,\texttheta 1\TH{} and
\par
U\dh Tj,\texttheta 2\TH{} for the TI emulator will generally be equal to the Gaussian
\par
process variance given in (13) multiplied by expf-rT \dh Ti-Tj\TH 2g,
\par
where rT is the smoothness parameter, which can be numerically
\par
estimated using the MCMC method as described above (see [25]
\par
for further technical details). An alternative covariance structure
\par
for the TI emulator is developed in [26] which we will brie y
\par
discuss in Section 2.4 below. When one ts a MS emulator, on the
\par
other hand, one does not de ne any covariance structure between
\par
outputs at different inputs and times at all; in particular, it is
\par
assumed that the model outputs are conditionally independent
\par
given the inputs, and the design points are not used jointly to t
\par
the emulator.
\par
For the maintenance optimisation problem examined here, the
\par
TI emulator has a very important advantage over the other two: it
\par
allows for decision T to be a continuous variable. Expectations E{$\frac{1}{2}$}Y
\par
are continuous functions of T, and the utilities of the optimal
\par
strategies are calculated without restricting the decision-maker to
\par
choose amongst a pre-determined, nite number of options. We
\par
believe that this feature outweighs the more general correlation
\par
structure provided by the MO emulator. We note, however, that
\par
this may not be the case in dynamic codes, where the representa-
\par
tion of the temporal structure of some physical process is key.
