4. Conclusions
\par
In this paper we have undertaken sensitivity analysis on
\par
maintenance optimisation problems by using the concept of
\par
expected value of perfect information (EVPI). Here, we suppose
\par
that the decision-maker can choose amongst an in nite set of
\par
decisions (critical age or periodic interval); therefore, our method
\par
is an extension of the approach presented in [13].
\par
Within a decision-making context, like the maintenance sche-
\par
duling problem, an EVPI-based approach bears some important70
\par
A. Zitrou et al. / Reliability Engineering and System Safety 120 (2013) 60--71
\par
advantages over more traditional variance-based approaches. EVPI
\par
allows the decision-maker to explore the effect of parameter
\par
uncertainty on cost calculations, and may inform maintenance
\par
recommendations in a more robust manner. Results from this type
\par
of analysis may also inform decisions related to accumulation of
\par
information: the aspects of the system with the highest learning
\par
value are identi ed. Further testing or training may be economic-
\par
ally justi ed within this context.
\par
The determination of EVPIs involves the computation of multi-
\par
dimensional intervals, that are often computationally demanding.
\par
To reduce the computational intensity of EVPI-based sensitivity
\par
analysis, we have used Gaussian process emulation to statistically
\par
approximate the cost rate model. In particular, we have built a
\par
time-input GP emulator to obtain expected utilities as continuous
\par
functions of the decision parameter T (critical age or periodic
\par
interval). Using a TI emulator for a maintenance optimisation
\par
problem, as opposed to, e.g. a many single outputs emulator [30]
\par
or a multi-output emulator, bears the advantage of allowing the
\par
decision-maker to choose any value over a continuous interval,
\par
rather than restricting the maintenance decision to a number of
\par
discrete nite options. This exibility can have a considerable
\par
effect on the overall cost of the maintenance strategy.
\par
To calculate the multi-dimensional integrals necessary, we have
\par
used Bayesian quadrature. The GP properties enable us to make
\par
such calculations relatively quickly. However, one needs to ensure
\par
that the size of the training set used to estimate the emulator
\par
parameters will allow for reasonably accurate results to be
\par
produced. When building a TI emulator, the inversion of the
\par
correlation matrix can be a computational challenge. Here, we
\par
have t a TI emulator on the whole training set T , but used the
\par
roughness parameters estimated by splitting T in two sets. The
\par
resulting emulator demonstrates a good t, even though the
\par
inversion of the correlation matrix takes longer.
\par
To illustrate this approach to sensitivity analysis, we have used
\par
two examples. The rst example is a problem of age-replacement
\par
for a component, whereas the second example is a block-
\par
replacement problem of a multi-component system. Due to the
\par
complexity of the system in the example we have used a Monte-
\par
Carlo simulation model to estimate the maintenance cost using
\par
AvSim \th. Emulating this type of model is slightly more complex:
\par
due to the simulation aspect, one may get different cost outputs
\par
when using the same inputs. In this case, the sensitivity analysis
\par
results are subject to an additional type of uncertainty: this
\par
inherent in the model approximated by the emulator.
\par
The sensitivity results have identi ed the most`important'
\par
parameters in terms of the bene t induced by'learning'. The
\par
optimal strategy may change if a parameter becomes known prior
\par
to a maintenance decision, and this may have signi cant effect on
\par
the resulting cost. Identifying the important parameters to this
\par
sense can provide guidance in decision-making regarding pro-
\par
cesses like further testing or maintenance staff training. The GP
\par
outputs include indicators of quality of t that can be used to gain
\par
assurance about the conclusions.
